{"row_id": "q030", "objective": "SGR-4174 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression or shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["SGR-4174"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q030-batch", "query": ["SGR-4174 Mia PaCa2 monotherapy tumour regression xenograft", "SGR-4174 PDAC xenograft tumour shrinkage single agent"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["SGR-4174 Mia PaCa2 monotherapy tumour regression xenograft", "SGR-4174 PDAC xenograft tumour shrinkage single agent"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 3088.2, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "SOS1: tracking the evolving path from promising to actionable ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12943-026-02632-7?error=cookies_not_supported&code=89f85997-69de-41bd-b40a-f6985ed2b656", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "In vivo assays demonstrated that in monotherapy, BI-3406-mediated SOS1 inhibition (50 mg/kg; bid; 21 days) resulted in potent tumor volume reduction in certain KRAS oncogenic xenograft models including G12C (PDAC: Mia PaCa-2), G12S (NSCLC: A549), G12V (PDAC: PATX53; CRC: SW620), and G13D (CRC: LoVo) [39].\n...\nIn vivo studies showed that single-agent administration (100 mg/kg; q.d; 21 days) inhibited tumor growth in a PDAC KRAS^G12C^ mutated model (Mia PaCa-2) but not in the KRAS^G12C^ CRC (SW837) or the KRAS^G12D^ PDAC (AsPC-1) models.\n...\nOral administration of HW071021 as a single-agent significantly caused TGI (78%) in a murine xenograft model of KRAS^G12C^-driven oncogenicity (Mia PaCa-2 cells) [79].\n...\nMechanistically, SGR-4174 binds to the catalytic domain of SOS1, disrupting SOS1::KRAS^G12C^ PPI (IC~50~ of 13 nM) [80].\nPreclinical in vitro studies demonstrated that SGR-4174 administration (IC~50~ values ranging from 42 to107 nM) resulted in a robust impairment of RAS-mediated signal transduction and reduced cell viability (3D cell cultures) across a wide spectrum of human cancer cell lines harboring KRAS^G12C/D/V^ or EGFR mutations (since we had no access to the panel of the specific cells employed in this study, this information is not included in Supplementary Table 2) [80].\nIn KRAS^G12C^-mutated tumor xenograft models, including NSCLC and PDAC, single-agent administration of SGR-4174 elicited dose-dependent TGI (please note that we were unable to find the precise dosing regimens employed in this study) [80].\nIn addition, concomitant administration of SGR-4174 and Sotorasib resulted in a deeper and more sustained inhibition of KRAS signal transmission, pronounced inhibition of cell proliferation in 3D cell culture models, and tumor regression in xenograft models when compared to the respective monotherapies [80].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-19"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Schrödinger, Inc. - 临床试验 - 新药情报库- 智慧芽", "url": "https://synapse.zhihuiya.com/organization/ee6d0cc13d500f7740bc81b30e6d5978", "domain": "synapse.zhihuiya.com", "snippet": "In KRAS G12C tumor xenograft mouse models, SGR-4174 monotherapy demonstrated dose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and a decrease in the tumor PD biomarker pERK.SGR-4174 was examined in combination with KRAS G12C inhibitor sotorasib and MEK inhibitor trametinib in vitro and in vivo.\nWhen combined with sotorasib, SGR-4174 demonstrated deeper and more sustained inhibition of the KRAS signaling pathway, stronger suppression of cell proliferation in 3D cell culture, and tumor regression in xenograft models compared to single agent activity.\n...\nCompared to other published SOS1 inhibitors (BI-3406, MRTX0902), SGR-4174 is more potent, and demonstrated similar anti-tumor activity and target engagement at a lower dose both in monotherapy and combination therapy studies in xenograft tumor models.\nSGR-4174 was well tolerated as monotherapy and in combination with sotorasib or trametinib.SGR-4174 demonstrated favorable PK properties across preclinical species and toxicity profile was well characterized.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-11"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "SOS1: tracking the evolving path from promising to actionable ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1186/s12943-026-02632-7.pdf?error=cookies_not_supported&code=aa72aed6-b31a-4006-88df-722541dec95c", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "In vivo studies showed that single-agent administra­\ntion (100 mg/kg; q.d; 21 days) inhibited tumor growth in a PDAC KRASG12C mutated model (Mia PaCa-2) but not in the KRASG12C CRC (SW837) or the KRASG12D PDAC\n(AsPC-1) models.\n...\nOral administration of \nHW071021 as a single-agent significantly caused TGI \n(78%) in a murine xenograft model of KRASG12C-driven \noncogenicity (Mia PaCa-2 cells) [79].\n...\nSGR-4174 appears as a novel, potent, and highly selec­\n…\n...\nMechanistically, SGR-\n4174 binds to the catalytic domain of SOS1, disrupting \nSOS1::KRASG12C PPI (IC50 of 13 nM) [80].\n...\ngraft models, including NSCLC and PDAC, single-agent administration of SGR-4174 elicited dose-dependent\nTGI (please note that we were unable to find the pre­ cise dosing regimens employed in this study) [80].\nIn addition, concomitant administration of SGR-4174 and", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-03"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Schrödinger, Inc. - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical trials", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/ee6d0cc13d500f7740bc81b30e6d5978", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "In KRAS G12C tumor xenograft mouse models, SGR-4174 monotherapy demonstrated dose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and a decrease in the tumor PD biomarker pERK.SGR-4174 was examined in combination with KRAS G12C inhibitor sotorasib and MEK inhibitor trametinib in vitro and in vivo.\nWhen combined with sotorasib, SGR-4174 demonstrated deeper and more sustained inhibition of the KRAS signaling pathway, stronger suppression of cell proliferation in 3D cell culture, and tumor regression in xenograft models compared to single agent activity.\nIn addition, when combined with trametinib, SGR-4174 suppressed KRAS pathway reactivation by MEK inhibition and led to robust tumor cell death and tumor stasis.\nCompared to other published SOS1 inhibitors (BI-3406, MRTX0902), SGR-4174 is more potent, and demonstrated similar anti-tumor activity and target engagement at a lower dose both in monotherapy and combination therapy studies in xenograft tumor models.\nSGR-4174 was well tolerated as monotherapy and in combination with sotorasib or trametinib.SGR-4174 demonstrated favorable PK properties across preclinical species and toxicity profile was well characterized.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "SGR-4174 / Schrodinger - Larvol Delta", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=1b3e9eec-7ffe-4dfd-8404-27376217e477", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## Preclinical characterization of SGR-4174, a potent and selective SOS1 inhibitor for the treatment of pan KRAS mutant cancers in combination with KRAS pathway inhibitors (AACR 2025) - \"Compared to other published SOS1 inhibitors (BI-3406, MRTX0902), SGR-4174 is more potent, and demonstrated similar anti-tumor activity and target engagement at a lower dose both in monotherapy and combination therapy studies in xenograft tumor models. SGR-4174 was well tolerated as monotherapy and in combination with sotorasib or trametinib.SGR-4174 demonstrated favorable PK properties across preclinical species and toxicity profile was well characterized. Our comprehensive preclinical efficacy and safety data support progressing SGR-4174 to clinical development to treat KRAS mutant cancers.\"\n...\n## Schrödinger Presents New Preclinical Data at AACR Annual Meeting (Businesswire) - \"SGR-4174 Data at AACR: The poster (Abstract #4376)...includes preclinical data demonstrating the superior binding, selectivity, and drug-like properties of SGR-4147 compared to MRTX0902 as assessed via comprehensive in vitro potency, ADME, and safety assays. The preclinical data for SGR-4174 also show robust suppression of the RAS signaling pathway and potent cell killing activity across multiple cancer types harboring diverse KRAS mutations as well as EGFR and SOS1 mutations. SGR-4174 monotherapy achieved dose-dependent target engagement and tumor growth inhibition and induced tumor shrinkage when used in combination with MEK or KRAS inhibitors in preclinical models of pancreatic and non-small cell lung cancer.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Schrödinger Presents New Preclinical Data at AACR Annual Meeting", "url": "https://s203.q4cdn.com/609444515/files/doc_news/Schrdinger-Presents-New-Preclinical-Data-at-AACR-Annual-Meeting-2025.pdf", "domain": "s203.q4cdn.com", "snippet": "The data also demonstrated that\nSGR-4174 has strong tumor growth inhibition as a monotherapy as well as in combination with MEK or KRAS inhibitors, while maintaining a favorable safety pro�le.\n...\nThe poster (Abstract #4376), “Preclinical characterization of SGR-4174, a potent and selective SOS1 inhibitor for the treatment of pan KRAS mutant cancers in combination with KRAS pathway inhibitors,” includes preclinical data demonstrating the superior binding, selectivity, and drug-like properties of SGR-4147 compared to MRTX0902 as assessed via comprehensive in vitro potency, ADME, and safety assays.\nThe preclinical data for SGR-4174 also show robust suppression of the RAS signaling pathway and potent cell killing activity across multiple cancer types harboring diverse KRAS mutations as well as EGFR and SOS1 mutations.\nSGR-4174 monotherapy achieved dose- dependent target engagement and tumor growth inhibition and induced tumor shrinkage when used in combination with MEK or KRAS inhibitors in preclinical models of pancreatic and non-small cell lung cancer.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-08"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "BI-3406 / Boehringer Ingelheim, Novo Nordisk", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=84a5b22a-f2c8-4c2e-a238-a5dc4a21c610", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "March 26, 2025\nPreclinical characterization of SGR-4174, a potent and selective SOS1 inhibitor for the treatment of pan KRAS mutant cancers in combination with KRAS pathway inhibitors\n…\n- \"Compared to other published SOS1 inhibitors (BI-3406, MRTX0902), SGR-4174 is more potent, and demonstrated similar anti-tumor activity and target engagement at a lower dose both in monotherapy and combination therapy studies in xenograft tumor models.\nSGR-4174 was well tolerated as monotherapy and in combination with sotorasib or trametinib.SGR-4174 demonstrated favorable PK properties across preclinical species and toxicity profile was well characterized.\nOur comprehensive preclinical efficacy and safety data support progressing SGR-4174 to clinical development to treat KRAS mutant cancers.\"\n...\n- \"Accordingly, in this study we used a mouse model of KRAS mutant LUAD (CC-LR) to compare the therapeutic effects of BI-3406, IL-6 blockade, and MEK inhibitor (trametinib).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-04"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Schrödinger to Present Preclinical Data at AACR Annual ...", "url": "https://s203.q4cdn.com/609444515/files/doc_news/Schrdinger-to-Present-Preclinical-Data-at-AACR-Annual-Meeting-2025.pdf", "domain": "s203.q4cdn.com", "snippet": "The SGR-4174 presentation will include data characterizing SGR-4174 in preclinical oncology models.\n...\nPreclinical data suggest SGR-\n4174 has a di�erentiated pro�le and support its continued development as a potential therapy for patients with\nKRAS mutant cancers and in rare diseases arising from KRAS pathway mutations.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-12-17"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Cloudflare", "url": "https://aacrjournals.org/mct/article-pdf/17/8/1761/1858346/1761.pdf", "domain": "aacrjournals.org", "snippet": "Cloudflare is currently unable to resolve your requested domain (aacrjournals.org).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "RESULT(S)", "url": "https://www.sedapds.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/2770-000_PS22-PosterTemplate_No2_v3.pdf", "domain": "sedapds.com", "snippet": "2. Predicted ORR from a single mouse was 99.9% (99%, \n100%) which was significantly higher than the ORR, \n37% (95%CI: 29-46) in the Phase II trial of non-small\ncell lung cancer (NSCLC) patients [6], Pred.\n(MIA-\nPaCa-2) in Figure 2\n• The inaccuracy was expected as the exposure-\n...\nimproved the correlation to clinical ORR – new \nprediction of ORR 50% (95%CI: 42-58), Pred.\n(MIA-", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-03-29"}], "raw": {"id": "d50e4522-6240-4c1c-b8b5-4fd897689e9d"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:35:58+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q031", "objective": "SPR2015 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression or shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC) animal models", "anchors": ["SPR2015"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q031-batch", "query": ["SPR2015 HPAC xenograft TGI percentage monotherapy", "SPR2015 AsPC-1 xenograft tumour regression shrinkage", "SPR2015 PDAC monotherapy xenograft tumour growth inhibition", "SPR2015 HPAC AsPC-1 xenograft AACR 2026 abstract 5978"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["SPR2015 HPAC xenograft TGI percentage monotherapy", "SPR2015 AsPC-1 xenograft tumour regression shrinkage", "SPR2015 PDAC monotherapy xenograft tumour growth inhibition", "SPR2015 HPAC AsPC-1 xenograft AACR 2026 abstract 5978"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 2112.7, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "SPR2015是一种高效且选择性的KRAS G12D（ON）抑制剂，在临床前模型中...", "url": "https://www.posterkeep.com/c/aacr-2026/p/5660", "domain": "posterkeep.com", "snippet": "SPR2015 showed significantly superior efficacy in these in vivo CRC xenograft models, achieving a 64.7% objective response rate (ORR) and 94.1% disease control rate (DCR) as monotherapy.", "date": "2026-04-17", "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Merck, SciBrunch Launch Up-to-$2.13B Collaboration ...", "url": "https://www.genengnews.com/topics/cancer/merck-scibrunch-launch-up-to-2-13b-collaboration-to-develop-preclinical-kras-inhibiting-molecular-glue/", "domain": "genengnews.com", "snippet": "At the 2026 American Association for Cancer Research (AACR) Annual Meeting, a team of SciBrunch researchers that included founder, chairman, and CEO Tao Hu, PhD, presented preclinical data showing SPR2015 to have demonstrated what it termed compelling antitumor efficacy as a monotherapy across multiple *in vivo* cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft (CDX and PDX) models.\n...\nIn a head-to-head mouse study comparing a single oral once-daily dose at 100 mg/kg of SPR2015 to another unspecified G12D (ON) inhibitor in over 15 CRC CDX or PDX models, SPR2015 showed significantly superior efficacy in the *in vivo* CRC xenograft models, achieving a 64.7% objective response rate (ORR) and 94.1% disease control rate (DCR) as monotherapy.", "date": "2026-09-29", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Revolution Medicines - MedPath Trial", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/organization/revolution-medicines", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "- SPR2015 is a molecular glue inhibitor that showed nanomolar antiproliferative activity in KRAS G12D-mutant cell lines with selectivity over KRAS wild-type cells, plus monotherapy antitumor activity in xenograft models.", "date": "2026-09-28", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "KRAS G12D Inhibitor's $2.13B Deal Tests Cross-Border Tech Transfer", "url": "https://www.pharmtech.com/view/kras-g12d-inhibitor-s-2-13b-deal-tests-cross-border-tech-transfer", "domain": "pharmtech.com", "snippet": "- SPR2015 functions as an ON-state molecular glue, showing nanomolar antiproliferative activity in KRAS G12D cell lines with wild-type selectivity and tumor regression in xenograft models.\n...\nSPR2015 is described as a molecular glue inhibitor that engages the ON form of the mutant protein.^1^ It showed nanomolar antiproliferative activity across multiple KRAS G12D-mutant cell lines while maintaining selectivity over wild-type cells, and it demonstrated monotherapy antitumor efficacy in cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft models.", "date": "2026-09-28", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "MSD takes a $400m bite of SciBrunch's KRAS drug", "url": "https://pharmaphorum.com/news/msd-takes-400m-bite-scibrunchs-kras-drug", "domain": "pharmaphorum.com", "snippet": "The company reported studies at this year's American Association for Cancer Research (AACR) congress which showed antitumour efficacy for SPR2015 as a monotherapy across multiple *in vivo* cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft models.", "date": "2026-10-02", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Merck Licenses Preclinical KRAS G12D Inhibitor from SciBrunch", "url": "https://www.pharmexec.com/view/merck-licenses-preclinical-kras-g12d-inhibitor-scibrunch", "domain": "pharmexec.com", "snippet": "KRAS G12D is the most common oncogenic RAS mutation found in human tumors, arising from a substitution of glycine with aspartate at position 12, a change that leaves KRAS active and continuously signaling for cell proliferation and survival.^1^ SPR2015 has also shown compelling antitumor efficacy as a monotherapy across multiple in vivo cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft models, according to preclinical data presented at the 2026 AACR Annual Meeting.^2^", "date": "2026-09-28", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Merck Enters into Exclusive Global License Agreement ...", "url": "https://www.merck.com/news/merck-enters-into-exclusive-global-license-agreement-with-scibrunch-therapeutics-for-spr2015-an-investigational-oral-kras-g12d-on-inhibitor/", "domain": "merck.com", "snippet": "In addition, SPR2015 has demonstrated compelling antitumor efficacy as a monotherapy across multiple in vivo cell-derived and patient-derived xenograft (CDX/PDX) models, as reported in preclinical data presented at the 2026 AACR Annual Meeting.", "date": "2026-10-02", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Anirban Maitra on X: \"This #AACR26 abstract is worth $400 ...", "url": "https://x.com/Aiims1742/status/2104884186620494308", "domain": "x.com", "snippet": "Abstract 5978: SPR2015, a highly potent and selective KRAS G12D (on) inhibitor, exhibits favorable...", "date": "2026-09-29", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Merck licenses SPR2015, an oral KRAS G12D inhibitor, from ...", "url": "https://chemxplore.com/news/merck-scibrunch-spr2015-kras-g12d-license", "domain": "chemxplore.com", "snippet": "- SPR2015 is a preclinical oral KRAS G12D (ON) molecular‑glue inhibitor showing nanomolar antiproliferative activity and monotherapy efficacy in CDX and PDX models.\n...\nSPR2015 is an investigational preclinical oral KRAS G12D (ON) molecular‑glue inhibitor that has demonstrated nanomolar antiproliferative activity in various KRAS G12D‑mutant cell lines with good selectivity over KRAS wild‑type cells.\nIt has shown antitumor efficacy as a monotherapy across multiple in vivo cell‑derived and patient‑derived xenograft (CDX/PDX) models, with preclinical data presented at the 2026 AACR Annual Meeting.", "date": "2026-09-28", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "SPR2015: Merck Pays $400M, Up to $2.13B - novapharmanews.com", "url": "https://novapharmanews.com/apac/news/spr2015-merck-400m-2-13b-license", "domain": "novapharmanews.com", "snippet": "- SPR2015 is still preclinical.\nThe release cites nanomolar cell-line activity and tumor activity in xenograft models shown at AACR 2026, without a response rate.\n...\nIt also cites antitumor activity as a single agent in cell-derived and patient-derived xenografts, from preclinical data presented at the 2026 AACR meeting.\nNo tumor-shrinkage percentage is printed.", "date": "2026-09-29", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}], "raw": {"id": "07b991f4-a866-498f-8b25-e63c84622546"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:35:59+00:00"}
