{"row_id": "q038", "objective": "KRAS G12D drugs approved against the target and their regulatory approval status", "anchors": ["ZE980277"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q038-batch", "query": ["ZE980277 KRAS G12D approval status FDA EMA", "ZE980277 KRAS G12D regulatory approval", "ZE980277 KRAS G12D approved therapy status"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["ZE980277 KRAS G12D approval status FDA EMA", "ZE980277 KRAS G12D regulatory approval", "ZE980277 KRAS G12D approved therapy status"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 925.1, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Search Orphan Drug Designations and Approvals - FDA", "url": "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/opdlisting/oopd/detailedIndex.cfm?cfgridkey=1124625", "domain": "accessdata.fda.gov", "snippet": "|Generic Name:|KRAS G12D inhibitor|\n|--|--|\n|Date Designated:|05/01/2026|\n|Orphan Designation:|treatment of pancreatic cancer|\n|Orphan Designation Status:|Designated|\n|FDA Orphan Approval Status:|Not FDA Approved for Orphan Indication|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-11"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Oncology (Cancer)/Hematologic Malignancies Approval ...", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/resources-information-approved-drugs/oncology-cancerhematologic-malignancies-approval-notifications", "domain": "fda.gov", "snippet": "|FDA approves sotorasib with panitumumab for KRAS G12C-mutated colorectal cancer|On January 16, 2025, the Food and Drug Administration approved sotorasib (Lumakras, Amgen Inc.) with panitumumab (Vectibix, Amgen Inc.) for adult patients with KRAS G12C-mutated metastatic colorectal cancer (mCRC), as determined by an FDA-approved test, who have receive", "date": "2026-09-24", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Ongoing | Cancer Accelerated Approvals", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/resources-information-approved-drugs/ongoing-cancer-accelerated-approvals", "domain": "fda.gov", "snippet": "|Krazati (adagrasib)|Adult patients with KRAS G12C-mutated locally advanced or metastatic non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), as determined by an FDA-approved test, who have received at least one prior systemic therapy.|12/12/2022|4378-1: Conduct a randomized comparative clinical trial of adagrasib in adult patients with KRAS G12", "date": "2026-09-17", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Oral Investigational Agent Zoldonrasib Elicits Objective Responses ...", "url": "https://www.aacr.org/about-the-aacr/newsroom/news-releases/oral-investigational-agent-zoldonrasib-elicits-objective-responses-in-patients-with-kras-g12d-mutated-lung-cancer/", "domain": "aacr.org", "snippet": "However, around 4% of NSCLC cases harbor KRAS G12D mutations, a mutation against which there are no approved targeted therapies, explained Kathryn C. Arbour, MD, an assistant attending and thoracic medical oncologist at Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center, who presented the study.\n...\nZoldonrasib is a novel RAS(ON) tri-complex inhibitor that selectively targets the G12D mutation.\n...\nThe efficacy analysis included 18 patients with NSCLC who enrolled at least eight weeks before the data cutoff.\nAmong these patients, 61% had an objective response, and 89% experienced disease control.", "date": "2025-04-27", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Therapeutic inhibition of RAS in non-small cell lung cancer - Frontiers", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/oncology/articles/10.3389/fonc.2026.1817943/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "As of this writing, both drugs retain their accelerated approvals, but neither have received full or standard FDA approvals based on these results, and neither study has easily interpretable OS results, limited by both sample size and crossover.\n...\n|Zoldonrasib (RMC-9805)|KRAS G12D (ON)|Molecular glue / tri-complex inhibitor (RAS(ON))|NCT06040541|RMC-9805-001 (phase 1/1b)|\n|ASP3082|KRAS G12D|PROTAC / targeted degrader|NCT05382559|ASP3082 first-in-human (phase 1)|", "date": "2026-06-30", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "KRAS G12D - My Cancer Genome", "url": "https://www.mycancergenome.org/content/alteration/kras-g12d/", "domain": "mycancergenome.org", "snippet": "Of the therapies with KRAS G12D as a predictive biomarker, 2 are FDA-approved and 7 have NCCN guidelines in at least one clinical setting.", "date": "2012-12-01", "last_updated": "2026-02-21"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "cancer.aestheticsadvisor.com · 2026 · 04KRAS Inhibitors (2026): The Complete Guide to Targeted Therapy...", "url": "https://cancer.aestheticsadvisor.com/2026/04/kras-inhibitors-2026-complete-guide-to.html", "domain": "cancer.aestheticsadvisor.com", "snippet": "Zoldonrasib is a covalent, G12D-selective RAS(ON) inhibitor and holds FDA Breakthrough Therapy Designation for KRAS G12D-mutated disease.\n...\nNo targeted therapy is currently FDA-approved specifically for RAS G12D-mutant cancers; zoldonrasib and related G12D-directed programs (including earlier-stage compounds such as MRTX1133) remain under active clinical investigation.\n...\nNot yet as a G12D-selective agent.\nDaraxonrasib's pancreatic cancer approval covers RAS G12-mutant disease broadly (including G12D and G12V) rather than being G12D-specific.\nZoldonrasib, a G12D-selective RAS(ON) inhibitor, remains investigational, with Phase 3 trials ongoing.\n...\n- No KRAS G12D-selective drug is yet approved anywhere; zoldonrasib is the most clinically advanced candidate, in Phase 3 development.", "date": "2026-09-07", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "KRAS Inhibitor Clinical Trial Landscape, September 2026: 115 ...", "url": "https://datalookout.com/kras-inhibitor-clinical-trials", "domain": "datalookout.com", "snippet": "Revolution Medicines is leading the development of pan-RAS inhibitors that target multiple KRAS mutations simultaneously, including G12D, the dominant mutation in pancreatic cancer:\n...\n- **RMC-9805 (Revolution Medicines)**: KRAS G12D-selective.\nPhase 1 in KRAS G12D-mutant solid tumors, particularly PDAC.\n...\nThe broader KRAS-mutant population (G12D, G12V) remains without an approved targeted therapy.", "date": "2026-10-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "KRAS G12D inhib News", "url": "https://sigma.larvol.com/moa.php?MoaId=1012573&tab=newstrac", "domain": "sigma.larvol.com", "snippet": "Whoops!\nThis page doesn’t exist or can’t be found..", "date": "2025-07-12", "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Precision Targeting of KRAS-Mutant Cancers: Beyond G12C ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13362513/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "At present, the clinical use of approved KRAS inhibitors is largely limited to KRAS G12C-mutated advanced cancers.\nIn NSCLC, sotorasib and adagrasib are approved as single agents for patients with locally advanced or metastatic KRAS G12C-mutated disease who have received at least one prior systemic therapy.\nThus, these agents are primarily used in the second-line or later setting rather than as first-line standard therapy.\n...\nIn contrast, KRAS G12D inhibitors, pan-RAS/RAS(ON) inhibitors, and KRAS degraders remain investigational and are not yet established as routine clinical therapies.\n...\nAmong these, zoldonrasib (RMC-9805) has emerged as an investigational RAS(ON)-selective KRAS G12D inhibitor currently under early clinical evaluation, with preliminary activity reported in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma [55] (NCT06040541).", "date": "2026-06-26", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}], "raw": {"id": "d7d860f6-415a-4202-8345-2e230806fb04"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:04+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q041", "objective": "competing agents in the same mechanistic class for this indication DB-3Q, Direct Biologics LLC, Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease", "anchors": ["DB-3Q"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q041-batch", "query": ["DB-3Q MSC-derived extracellular vesicle Crohn's competitors", "DB-3Q extracellular vesicle Crohn's disease clinical trials", "DB-3Q MSC-derived EV medically refractory Crohn's"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["DB-3Q MSC-derived extracellular vesicle Crohn's competitors", "DB-3Q extracellular vesicle Crohn's disease clinical trials", "DB-3Q MSC-derived EV medically refractory Crohn's"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 1953.0, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Study Details | NCT07625293 | DB-3Q for the Treatment of ...", "url": "https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT07625293", "domain": "clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "## DB-3Q for the Treatment of Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease\nClinicalTrials.gov ID ** NCT07625293\nSponsor ** Direct Biologics, LLC\n...\nLast Update Posted ** 2026-06-12\n**Not yet recruiting** **\n...\nThis research study is studying DB-3Q as a possible treatment for medically refractory Crohn's disease.\nThe purpose of this study is to research and evaluate safety and effectiveness of the administration of bone marrow mesenchymal stem cell (bmMSC) derived extracellular vesicles product, DB-3Q, the study drug for Crohn's disease.\nOfficial Title\nA Phase 2a Study of DB-3Q for the Treatment of Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease\nConditions **\nCrohn Disease (CD)\nCrohn Disease (CD)\nCrohn Disease (CD)\nCrohn Disease (CD)\nIntervention/Treatment **\n- Biological: DB-3Q\n- Other: Placebo\nOther Study ID Numbers **\n- 3Q-CD-201\nStudy Start (Estimated) **\n2026-06\nPrimary Completion (Estimated) **\n2027-06\nStudy Completion (Estimated) **\n2027-12\nEnrollment (Estimated) **\n36\nStudy Type **\nInterventional\nPhase **\nPhase 2\n...\nStudy Contact **\n**Name: ** Bill Arana\n**Phone Number: ** 15123547124\n**Email: ** barana@directbiologics.com\nThis study has 1 location\nUnited States\nMissouri Locations\n**St Louis, Missouri, United States, 63110**\nWashington University, St. Louis\nContact :\nStudy Coordinator\n314-273-0301 luluhuang@wustl.edu\n...\nInclusion Criteria:\n1. Written informed consent from participant\n2. Males and females 18-75 years of age\n3. Diagnosed with Crohn's disease of at least 6 months duration with medically refractory symptoms that failed to respond or responded but recurred after one advanced immunologic therapy (must have been receiving at least one advanced immunological therapy for 14 weeks duration prior to screening, including, but not limited to, adalimumab, certolizumab, , infliximab, risankizumab, upadacitinib, ustekinumab and vedolizumab), or is intolerant, or has a contraindication to advanced immunological therapy with a next step of subtotal colectomy or escalation in medical management\n4. Active CD as defined by a CDAI score ≥ 220 and/or SES-CD score ≥ 4\n5. Exposure to corticosteroids, 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) drugs, thiopurines, methotrexate, anti-TNF therapy, anti-integrin and anti-interleukin in the past are permitted but a washout period of 8 weeks for any monoclonal antibody is necessary\n...\nExclusion Criteria:\n1. Lack of informed consent\n2. Pregnant woman, woman of childbearing potential without a documented negative urine or serum pregnancy test, or woman who is breast feeding\n3. Clinically significant medical conditions within the six months before initial administration of IMP: e.g., myocardial infarction, active angina, congestive heart failure or other conditions that would, in the opinion of the investigators, compromise the safety of the participant\n4. Confirmed Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV), Hepatitis B, or Hepatitis C infections\n...\n12. Microscopic, ischemic or infectious colitis\n...\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Intervention/Treatment **|\n|--|--|\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Experimental: DB-3Q 15 mL IV|\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Experimental: DB-3Q 30 mL IV|\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Experimental: DB-3Q 45 mL IV|\n|Participant Group/Arm **|Placebo Comparator: Placebo IV|\n...\n|--|--|--|\n|Change from baseline in SES-CD Score|Simple Endoscopic Score for Crohn's Disease (SES-CD) is a standardized tool used by gastroenterologists to assess and quantify the severity of Crohn's disease during an endoscopy or colonoscopy.\nThe scale goes from 0 to greater than 15, with a lower score being better.|12 Weeks|", "date": "2026-06-12", "last_updated": "2026-06-30"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease", "url": "https://ichgcp.net/clinical-trials-registry/NCT06918808", "domain": "ichgcp.net", "snippet": "# DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\n...\n## A Phase 2a Study of Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product (DB-3Q) in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\nThis is a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled, sequential cohort, ascending dose clinical trial to evaluate the safety and determine the efficacy of ascending doses of DB-3Q for the treatment of Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease.\n...\n#### Status\nRecruiting\n...\n#### Study Type\nInterventional\n#### Enrollment (Estimated)\n36\n#### Phase\n- Phase 2\n...\nNew York\nNew York, New York, United States, 10032\n- Recruiting\n- Columbia University Irving Medical Center/NYPH\nContact:\n- Claudia Musat\n...\n#### Ages Eligible for Study\n...\nInclusion Criteria:\n- Written informed consent from participant\n- Men and women 18-75 years of age with a diagnosis of Crohn's Disease for at least six months duration prior to Day 1\n- Single and/or multi-tract perianal fistula(s) with 2 or fewer openings, that are actively draining\n- Failed at least one medical therapy within the last year including, but not limited to, antibiotics, immunomodulators (6-MP, methotrexate, azathioprine), monoclonal antibodies (adalimumab, certolizumab, golimumab, guselkumab, infliximab, risankizumab, ustekinumab, vedolizumab), or small molecule inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib)\n- Previous failed surgical intervention, including seton placement at least two weeks prior to screening, or are not candidates for surgical intervention or are not willing to undergo surgical intervention for the management of their fistula\n- Medical therapy for CD stable for at least 2 months prior to Day 1 (Changes in dosing or dosing intervals related to serum drug levels are not permitted)\n...\n**Primary Purpose**: Treatment **Allocation**: Randomized **Interventional Model**: Sequential Assignment **Masking**: Quadruple\n...\n|--|--|\n|Experimental: Single dose DB-3Q 15 mL direct injection|DB-3Q (IMP) is the frozen liquid formulation of acellular secretome proteins and extracellular vesicles isolated from human bmMSC and processed under current Good Manufacturing Process standards.|\n|Placebo Comparator: Single dose Placebo 15 mL direct injection|0.9% NaCl|\n|Experimental: Single dose DB-3Q 30 mL direct injection|DB-3Q (IMP) is the frozen liquid formulation of acellular secretome proteins and extracellular vesicles isolated from human bmMSC and processed under current Good Manufacturing Process standards.|\n|Placebo Comparator: Single dose Placebo 30 mL direct injection|0.9% NaCl|\n|Experimental: Single dose DB-3Q 15 ml IV and DB-3Q 30 mL direct injection|DB-3Q (IMP) is the frozen liquid formulation of acellular secretome proteins and extracellular vesicles isolated from human bmMSC and processed under current Good Manufacturing Process standards.|\n|Placebo Comparator: Single dose Placebo 15 ml IV and Placebo 30 mL direct injection|0.9% NaCl|\n...\n|## Outcome Measure|## Measure Description|## Time Frame|\n|--|--|--|\n|Combined Remission Time Frame: 24 Weeks|Clinically confirmed 100% closure of all treated external openings, without development of new fistulas or abscesses and drainage > 1 cm by the external openings, occurring spontaneously or after gentle finger compression; and on blinded central review of the MRI results, combined imaging endpoint of >50% reduction in MAGNIFI-CD score and >20% increase in perianal fistula fibrosis-to-fluid ratio.|24 Weeks|\n...\n|Combined Remission Time Frame: 12 Weeks|Clinically confirmed 100% closure of all treated external openings, without development of new fistulas or abscesses and drainage > 1 cm by the external openings, occurring spontaneously or after gentle finger compression; and on blinded central review of the MRI results, combined imaging endpoint of >50% reduction in MAGNIFI-CD score and >20% increase in perianal fistula fibrosis-to-fluid ratio.|12 Weeks|\n...\n#### Study Start (Actual)\nMay 19, 2025\n#### Primary Completion (Estimated)\nApril 30, 2026\n#### Study Completion (Estimated)\nOctober 31, 2026", "date": "2025-06-13", "last_updated": "2025-07-24"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Columbia Enrolls Nation's First Patient in Direct Biologics' ...", "url": "https://columbiasurgery.org/news/columbia-enrolls-nations-first-patient-direct-biologics-phase-2-clinical-trial-fistulizing", "domain": "columbiasurgery.org", "snippet": "Columbia Enrolls Nation's First Patient in Direct Biologics' Phase 2 Clinical Trial for Fistulizing Crohn's Disease | Columbia Surgery\n...\nColumbia University Irving Medical Center has enrolled the first patient in a multicenter Phase 2 clinical trial investigating the effectiveness of Direct Biologics' proprietary extracellular vesicle technology (DB-3Q, extracellular vesicles (EVs) secreted from bone marrow-derived mesenchymal stem cells) for the treatment of perianal fistulizing Crohn's Disease.\n...\n\"This study aims to determine if this approach is a viable option to treat anal fistula in patients with Crohn's disease specifically.\"\n...\nEVs may offer a game-changing avenue for treating autoimmune diseases, with their ability to repair damaged tissues thanks to their anti-inflammatory and immunomodulatory properties.\n\"The advantages of EVs over traditional stem cell therapies are multiple”, notes Dr. Zoccali.\n\"While derived from stem cells, it is a much more stable product, hence easier to store and administer.\nIt's also less immunogenic, which can mitigate some of the problems associated with stem cell treatment due to the body's response to foreign DNA.\"\nThis phase 2 multicenter randomized study will have multiple cohorts with three different doses and administration routes.\nThe first uses an initial dose administered in the operating room via direct injection near the fistula.\nThe second phase will double that dose.\nThe third phase will include local injections and add an IV infusion of the medication.\n...\nWe are very optimistic that this trial will determine the appropriate dosage to help patients with this novel modality.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Claudia Musat, MD - Columbia Surgery", "url": "https://columbiasurgery.org/sites/default/files/2025-03/colorectal-ibd-trials-2025.pdf", "domain": "columbiasurgery.org", "snippet": "DB-3Q: Bone marrow mesenchymal stem cell (bmMSC) derived extracellular \nvesicles product in Patients with Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease (CD) \nPI: Marco Zoccali, MD \nIRB: AAAV6645 \nDisease: Perianal Fistulas due to Crohn's Disease (CD)\nTreatment: DB-3Q (a bone-marrow mesenchymal stem cell product) or Placebo \nThe purpose of this study is to evaluate the efficacy and safety of ascending doses of DB-3Q, a bone-marrow mesenchymal stem cell (bmMSC) derived secretome with extracellular vesicles\n(EVs) isolate product, via direct injection and intravenous (IV) administration, as treatment for \nparticipants with perianal fistulizing Crohn’s disease (PFCD).\nThe investigational product is experimental and has not been approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA).\nThe experimental study drug is a biologic product which is purified from the bone marrow of a healthy well-screened individual.\n36 participants with perianal fistulizing Crohn’s disease, randomized 3:1 to IMP or placebo in \nblocks of 4 participants (3 randomized to IMP, and 1 randomized to placebo) such that each cohort will have 12 participants (9 randomized to IMP and 3 randomized to placebo).\nYour participation in this study will be approximately 24 weeks long.\nEligibility: \n1. 18-75 years of age with a diagnosis of Crohn’s Disease for at least six months duration \nprior to Day 1 \n2. Single and/or multi-tract perianal fistula(s) with 2 or fewer openings, that are actively \ndraining \n3. Failed at least one medical therapy within the last year \n4. Previous failed surgical intervention or not interested in surgical intervention \n5. NO ileal anal pouch and/or history of proctectomy and severe proctitis", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-25"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Direct Biologics LLC - Drug pipelines, Patents, Clinical trials", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/7ab73070909872e45913d775d2ff7416", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Active Indication Respiratory Distress Syndrome, Acute [+1]|\n|Inactive Indication Colitis, Ulcerative [+7]|\n|Drug Highest PhasePhase 3|\n...\n|Active Indication Perianal fistula due to Crohn's disease|\n|Inactive Indication-|\n|Drug Highest PhasePhase 2|\n...\n12\nClinical Trials associated with Direct Biologics LLC\nNCT06918808\n/ Not yet recruitingPhase 2\nA Phase 2a Study of Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product (DB-3Q) in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\nThis is a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled, sequential cohort, ascending dose clinical trial to evaluate the safety and determine the efficacy of ascending doses of DB-3Q for the treatment of Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease.\n|Start Date15 Apr 2025|\n|--|\n|Sponsor / Collaborator Direct Biologics LLC|\nNCT05127122\n/ WithdrawnPhase 1/2\nBone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicles Infusion Treatment for Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome: A Phase I/II Clinical Trial\n...\n|Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicles (Direct Biologics)|Respiratory Distress Syndrome, Adult More|Phase 3|\n|Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product(Direct Biologics)|Perianal fistula due to Crohn's disease More|Phase 2|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "NCT06918808: An ongoing trial by Direct Biologics, LLC", "url": "https://fdaaa.trialstracker.net/trial/NCT06918808/", "domain": "fdaaa.trialstracker.net", "snippet": "## NCT06918808: An ongoing trial by Direct Biologics, LLC\nThis trial is ongoing.\nIt must report results 1 year, 10 months from now.\n...\n|Full entry on ClinicalTrials.gov|NCT06918808|\n...\n|Title|**A Phase 2a Study of Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product (DB-3Q) in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease**|\n|Results Status|Ongoing|\n|ACT or pACT?|This is what FDAAA officially calls an \"Applicable Clinical Trial\"|\n|Start date|May 19, 2025|\n|Completion date|July 30, 2027|\n|Required reporting date|July 29, 2028, midnight|\n|Actual reporting date|None|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-11"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Direct Biologics | MTEC", "url": "https://mtec-sc.org/life-sciences/direct-biologics", "domain": "mtec-sc.org", "snippet": "Developer of investigational secretome-based biologics derived from adult bone marrow mesenchymal stem cells (MSC).\nThe company focuses on cGMP manufacturing of extracellular vesicle (EV)-enriched secretome products, clinical trial execution across phases, and multi-omics characterization using AI-enabled analytics to support mechanism-of-action and potency assay development.\nPrograms target conditions with unmet needs including ARDS and inflammatory bowel disease.\n...\n### DB-3Q — Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\nInvestigational secretome-based product candidate being evaluated in clinical trials for perianal fistula due to Crohn's disease (Phase II noted).\n### DB-3Q — Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease\nInvestigational secretome-based product candidate under clinical evaluation for medically refractory Crohn's disease.\n...\n### DB-3Q\nAn investigational secretome-based product being evaluated in clinical trials for Perianal Fistulizing Crohn’s Disease and Medically Refractory Crohn’s Disease.", "date": "2025-02-13", "last_updated": "2026-09-26"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived ...", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/76f5bfcac1da44b8ac9629daf1034a48", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product(Direct Biologics)\n...\nDrug Type\nExosomes, Mesenchymal stem cell therapy\nSynonyms\nDB-3Q\n...\nActive Indication\nPerianal fistula due to Crohn's disease\nInactive Indication\nOriginator Organization\nDirect Biologics LLC\n...\nClinical Trials associated with Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product(Direct Biologics)\nNCT06918808\nRecruiting\nPhase 2\nA Phase 2a Study of Allogeneic Bone Marrow Mesenchymal Stem Cell Derived Extracellular Vesicle Isolate Product (DB-3Q) in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\nThis is a double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled, sequential cohort, ascending dose clinical trial to evaluate the safety and determine the efficacy of ascending doses of DB-3Q for the treatment of Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease.\nStart Date\n19 May 2025\nSponsor / Collaborator\nDirect Biologics LLC\n100\n...\nIndication\nHighest Phase\nCountry/Location\nOrganization\nDate\nPerianal fistula due to Crohn's disease\nPhase 2\nUnited States\nDirect Biologics LLC\n19 May 2025\n...\nNo Data", "date": "2026-08-18", "last_updated": "2026-05-25"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Direct Biologics Clinical Research Programs", "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/direct-biologics_our-clinical-development-programs-span-multiple-activity-7490811433820266496-1073", "domain": "linkedin.com", "snippet": "Our clinical development programs span multiple investigational products, indications, and stages of research.\nAs shown in our pipeline, Direct Biologics currently has Phase 2 through Phase 3 clinical research programs underway.\n...\nHere are our current trials:\nExtracellular Vesicle Treatment for Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) (EXTINGUISH ARDS) https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT05354141?intr=exoflo&rank=7 DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06918808?lead=Direct%20Biologics DB-3Q for the Treatment of Medically Refractory Crohn's Disease\nLike", "date": "2026-08-05", "last_updated": "2026-08-18"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Filgotinib in the Induction and Maintenance of Remission in Adults ...", "url": "https://www.clareohealth.com/clinical-trials/NCT02914561", "domain": "clareohealth.com", "snippet": "RECRUITINGPhase 2\n### DB-3Q bmMSC-EVs in Patients With Perianal Fistulizing Crohn's Disease\nNCT06918808 · Perianal Fistula Due to Crohn's Disease\n...\n### Trial snapshot\nStatusCOMPLETED\nPhasePHASE3\nEnrollment1,372\nStart dateOct 2016\nLast updatedDec 18, 2023\n### Condition\nCrohn's Disease\n### Locations shown\nDigestive Health Specialists of the Southeast\nDothan, Alabama\nArizona Digestive Health - Sun City\nSun City, Arizona\nSouthern California Research Center, Inc.\nCoronado, California", "date": "2016-10-31", "last_updated": "2026-05-25"}], "raw": {"id": "e937382d-7092-4e60-ba45-95e3df4afa63"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:06+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q040", "objective": "Active Ulcerative Colitis Moderate to Severe Ulcerative Colitis competing agents in the same mechanistic class as CHST15 Oligonucleotide Small Interfering RNA therapies", "anchors": ["GUT-1"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q040-batch", "query": ["GUT-1 CHST15 ulcerative colitis competitors", "GUT-1 siRNA CHST15 ulcerative colitis", "GUT-1 CHST15 moderate to severe ulcerative colitis pipeline"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["GUT-1 CHST15 ulcerative colitis competitors", "GUT-1 siRNA CHST15 ulcerative colitis", "GUT-1 CHST15 moderate to severe ulcerative colitis pipeline"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 3052.3, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Submucosal Injection of the RNA Oligonucleotide GUT-1 in Active ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article/18/3/406/7287358?guestAccessKey=", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Here, we evaluate the efficacy and safety of the double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide GUT-1, a specific blocker of CHST15, as induction therapy in patients with ulcerative colitis [UC].\n...\nGUT-1 250 nM reduced CHST15 expression significantly and suppressed mucosal inflammation and fibrosis.\nGUT-1 application was well tolerated.\n...\nSingle dosing by submucosal injection of GUT-1 repressed CHST15 mucosal expression and may represent a novel induction therapy by modulating tissue remodelling in UC.\n...\nGUT-1 is a synthetic double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide that selectively inhibits the expression of CHST15.^15–17^ Previous *in vitro* studies demonstrated that GUT-1 suppresses CHST15 mRNA in a dose-dependent manner as well as the mRNA expression of vimentin, α-SMA and Wnt3 in a TGF-β-stimulated human fibroblast cell line.\n...\nIn conclusion, the present study shows that local application of GUT-1 could be a novel option for induction therapy in active UC.\nThe effects may be due to a reduced sulphated matrix and CS-E generation, by targeting CHST15, which simultaneously acts to repress neutrophil-based mucosal inflammation and accelerate degradation of established fibrosis, leading to tissue remodelling in UC.", "date": "2024-03-01", "last_updated": "2026-03-17"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Gut Inc.", "url": "https://gut-oligo.com/", "domain": "gut-oligo.com", "snippet": "GUT Inc.\nfocused on the development of therapeutic RNA oligonucleotides in IBD.\n...\n**GUT-1** is locally injectable RNA oligonucleotide, and the positive clinical outcomes in Phase IIa and investigator-initiated trials were demonstrated in ulcerative colitis (UC) patients who showed loss of response to TNF antagonists.\nWe now identified **GUT-2**, a novel **orally feasible** **RNA** oligonucleotide, having the equivalent antisense sequence of GUT-1.\n...\nUnder the agreement, GUT Inc. and Dokkyo Medical University will jointly conduct pharmacokinetic and efficacy studies, including animal studies, using GUT Inc.’s CHST15-targeting oligonucleotide therapeutics.\n...\nThe research aims to further evaluate the pharmacological properties and therapeutic potential of GUT Inc.’s novel CHST15-targeted nucleic acid medicines.\n...\nThe results from Independent Investigational Trial (IIT) study of GUT-1 as an add-on therapy for moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis were published in the Inflammation and Regeneration.\n...\nIn **moderate-to-severe UC** patients who **lost response to TNF antagonists** (n=5), **add-on multiple injections of GUT-1** (250 nM) to TNF antagonist therapy as an induction therapy showed:\n- **60% of clinical remission at week 6.**\n- **80% of endoscopic improvement at week 6.**\n- **80% of histological improvement at week 6.**\nThe results would also provide new insights into treatment concept of biologics-maintenance therapy – Adding local therapy of distinct MOA like GUT-1, rather than simply switching, would be an alternative option for chronic disease like UC that repeats relapses and remissions.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-09"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Add-on multiple submucosal injections of the RNA oligonucleotide GUT-1 to anti-TNF antibody treatment in patients with moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis: an open-label, proof-of concept study", "url": "https://d-nb.info/1335375937/34", "domain": "d-nb.info", "snippet": "Background Carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15 (CHST15) is an enzyme biosynthesizing matrix glycosaminoglycan that modulates tissue remodeling.\nWe evaluated the efficacy of add-on submucosal injections of GUT-1, the RNA oligonucleotide inhibitor of CHST15, to ongoing anti-tumor necrosis factor (TNF) antibody treatment in patients with moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis (UC).\nMethods This was an open-label study of 250 nM of GUT-1 by endoscopic submucosal injections at weeks 0, 2, 4 in five UC patients who lost response during maintenance treatment to anti-TNF antibodies.\n...\nResults At week 6, rates of endoscopic improvement and clinical remission were 80% (n = 4/5) and 60% (n = 3/5),\nrespectively.\n...\nGUT-1\nwas well tolerated.\n...\nConclusions Add-on multiple injections of GUT-1 to ongoing anti-TNF antibody was able to induce rapid and dura- ble clinical responses in UC patients who lost response to anti-TNF therapy.\n...\nGUT-1 is a synthesized double stranded RNA oli- gonucleotide that selectively inhibits the expression of the CHST15 gene through siRNA mechanisms [9, 14,\n15].\nCHST15 siRNA suppressed CHST15 mRNA in a \ndose dependent manner and demonstrated repression of fibrosis-related genes in human fibroblast cell lines\n[11, 13].\n...\nIn a phase IIa induction trial, one-time submu-\ncosal injection of 250 nM of GUT-1 demonstrated 71.4% of endoscopic improvement (vs. 28.6% for placebo) and\n57.1% of clinical remission (vs. 14.3% for placebo) at week\n4 in patients with ulcerative colitis (UC) refractory to \nconventional treatment [16].\n...\nAdd-on multiple injections of 250 nM of\nGUT-1 to ongoing anti-TNF antibody treatment reduced \nSFS at week 2, reduced RBS and Endoscopy Subscore till week 4 and induced 80% of endoscopic improvement,\n100% of clinical response and 60% of clinical remission \nat week 6.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-03-09"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Research and Development - Gut Inc.", "url": "https://gut-oligo.com/about/research/", "domain": "gut-oligo.com", "snippet": "Our target matrix remodeling gene, carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15 (CHST15), is highly expressed in the **colonic mucosa of IBD patients who are infliximab non-responders**, as validated by public databases [9 (EMBL-EBI)].\n...\nThese findings establish CHST15 as a highly promising therapeutic target for oligonucleotide intervention, where suppressing colonic CHST15 directly correlates with clinical improvement.\n...\nIn UC, CHST15 is mainly expressed by inflammatory **neutrophils** in the mucosa and **fibroblasts** in the submucosa.\nGUT-1 and GUT-2, RNA oligonucleotides designed to silence the CHST15 gene, directly target these cell populations to suppress neutrophil-driven inflammation and fibroblast-mediated fibrosis.\nThe clinical efficacy of this CHST15 blockade translates into robust histological healing and symptomatic relief, including improved stool consistency.\nIn fact, the induction of clinical remission by locally administered GUT-1 has already been demonstrated in two completed clinical studies for moderate-to-severe UC [11, 12].\n...\n**GUT-2**, our nominated Development Candidate, is a novel, chemically **modified RNA oligonucleotide** targeting the CHST15 gene.\nIt utilizes the **identical active antisense sequence as GUT-1**, thereby building upon the **clinically validated mechanism of action** described above.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-14"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Submucosal Injection of the RNA Oligonucleotide GUT-1 in Active Ulcerative Colitis Patients: A Randomized, Double-Blind, Placebo-Controlled Phase 2a Induction Trial", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/ecco-jcc/article-abstract/18/3/406/7287358?redirectedFrom=fulltext", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15 [CHST15] biosynthesizes sulphated matrix glycosaminoglycans and is implicated in intestinal inflammation and fibrosis.\nHere, we evaluate the efficacy and safety of the double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide GUT-1, a specific blocker of CHST15, as induction therapy in patients with ulcerative colitis [UC].\nIn this randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled, phase 2a study, we enrolled endoscopically active UC patients, refractory to conventional therapy, in five hospital centres across Germany.\nPatients were randomized 1:1:1 using a block randomized technique to receive a single dosing of 25 nM GUT-1, 250 nM GUT-1, or placebo by endoscopic submucosal injections.\n...\nEndoscopic improvement at weeks 2 or 4 was achieved by 71.4% in the GUT-1 250 nM, 0% in the GUT-1 25 nM, and 28.6% in the placebo group.\nClinical remission was shown by 57.1% in the GUT-1 250 nM, 0% in the GUT-1 25 nM, and 14.3% in the placebo groups.\n...\nGUT-1 250 nM reduced CHST15 expression significantly and suppressed mucosal inflammation and fibrosis.\nGUT-1 application was well tolerated.\nSingle dosing by submucosal injection of GUT-1 repressed CHST15 mucosal expression and may represent a novel induction therapy by modulating tissue remodelling in UC.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-20"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Research progress in molecular targeted therapy for ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12967-025-07385-3?error=cookies_not_supported&code=0c8fefc4-9b58-46fd-9932-8178652bd368", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "GUT-1, a double-stranded RNA oligonucleotide targeting CHST15, was evaluated in a Phase IIa study of 28 UC patients unresponsive to conventional treatments.\nSingle-dose administration (25 nM, 250 nM) was compared with placebo; the primary endpoint was endoscopic improvement at weeks 2 and 4.\nIn the 250 nM group, endoscopic improvement was 71.4%, clinical remission 57.1%, and histological improvement 42.9%, significantly higher than the 25 nM group (0%) and placebo (28.6%, 14.3%, 0%).\nGUT-1 reduces CHST15 expression, inhibiting mucosal inflammation and fibrosis, and may serve as an inducible therapy to regulate tissue remodeling in UC [55].", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-21"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Y-ECCO Literature Review: Lushen Pillay - ECCO - European Crohn’s and Colitis Organisation", "url": "https://ecco-ibd.eu/publications/news/issues/2023/volume-18-issue-4/y-ecco-literature-review-lushen-pillay", "domain": "ecco-ibd.eu", "snippet": "One such target is carbohydrate sulphotransferase 15 (CHST15).\nThis is a type II transmembrane Golgi protein that biosynthesises highly sulphated disaccharide units (E-units) of chondroitin sulphate, which binds to various functional proteins and pathogenic microorganisms.\n...\nBased on this promising pre-clinical data, blockade of CHST15 has emerged as a potentially promising therapeutic target, and such blockade can be achieved by a silencing RNA oligonucleotide molecule called GUT-1 (previously called STNM01).\nA prior phase I clinical trial demonstrated the safety of GUT-1 in patients with Crohn’s Disease [6].\nAccordingly, Atreya and colleagues now sought to evaluate the safety, as well as the efficacy and mode of action, of GUT-1 in patients with UC as part of a phase IIa placebo-controlled, clinical trial.\n...\nTwenty-eight patients were screened and of these 24 were randomised in a 1:1:1 fashion to receive a single dose of 250 nM GUT-1, 25 nM GUT-1 or placebo.\n...\nEndoscopic improvement was achieved in 71.4% of patients in the 250 nM GUT-1 arm, 0% of patients in the 25 nM GUT-1 arm and 28.6% of patients in the placebo arm.\n...\nThe mean percentage positive areas of CHST15 in the 250 nM GUT-1 arm were 19.7% (95% CI 17.0%–22.5%) at baseline, 11.5% (95% CI 6.5%–16.5%) at week 2 and 8.4% (95% CI 3.1%–13.7%) at week 4, suggesting good target engagement with a >50% drop in expression from baseline to week 4.\n...\nAt two hours after injection, the plasma concentration of GUT-1 was below the lower limit of quantification (LLOQ 0.5 ng/ml) in all treatment groups, and both lower and higher doses of GUT-1 were generally well tolerated.\n...\nAtreya and colleagues should be congratulated for conducting this trial, which provides promising evidence of safety, efficacy and target engagement for GUT-1, a selective RNA oligonucleotide molecule in patients with left-sided UC.", "date": "2023-12-18", "last_updated": "2025-08-12"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Add-on multiple submucosal injections of the RNA ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11044299/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15 (CHST15) is an enzyme biosynthesizing matrix glycosaminoglycan that modulates tissue remodeling.\nWe evaluated the efficacy of add-on submucosal injections of GUT-1, the RNA oligonucleotide inhibitor of CHST15, to ongoing anti-tumor necrosis factor (TNF) antibody treatment in patients with moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis (UC).\n...\nThis was an open-label study of 250 nM of GUT-1 by endoscopic submucosal injections at weeks 0, 2, 4 in five UC patients who lost response during maintenance treatment to anti-TNF antibodies.\n...\nAdd-on multiple injections of GUT-1 to ongoing anti-TNF antibody was able to induce rapid and durable clinical responses in UC patients who lost response to anti-TNF therapy.\n...\nWe have here focused on extracellulat matrix (ECM) molecules, especially carbohydrate sulfotransferase 15 (CHST15), as a potential novel therapeutic target in IBD [6, 7].\n...\nGUT-1 is a synthesized double stranded RNA oligonucleotide that selectively inhibits the expression of the CHST15 gene through siRNA mechanisms [9, 14, 15].\nCHST15 siRNA suppressed CHST15 mRNA in a dose dependent manner and demonstrated repression of fibrosis-related genes in human fibroblast cell lines [11, 13].\n...\nIn a phase IIa induction trial, one-time submucosal injection of 250 nM of GUT-1 demonstrated 71.4% of endoscopic improvement (vs. 28.6% for placebo) and 57.1% of clinical remission (vs. 14.3% for placebo) at week 4 in patients with ulcerative colitis (UC) refractory to conventional treatment [16].\n...\nThis was an open-label, investigator-initiated, monocentric-study which included 5 adult patients (age 16–75 years) with moderate-to-severe UC defined by a modified Mayo Score (mMS) of 5 to 9 and a Mayo Endoscopy Subscore of ≥ 2 (Table 1) [18–20].\n...\nThe participants enrolled in this IIT represented patients with moderate-to-severe active UC who did not sufficiently respond to ongoing anti-TNF antibody treatment of IFX or ADA.\n...\nThe present study shows a potential that add-on multiple injections of GUT-1 could provide as a novel therapeutic option for additional induction therapy in patients with anti-TNF antibody-refractory UC.", "date": "2024-04-25", "last_updated": "2025-05-28"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Y-ECCO Literature Review: Lushen Pillay", "url": "https://www.ecco-ibd.eu/publications/ecco-news/item/2023-4-y-ecco-literature-review-lushen-pillay.html", "domain": "ecco-ibd.eu", "snippet": "One such target is carbohydrate sulphotransferase 15 (CHST15).\nThis is a type II transmembrane Golgi protein that biosynthesises highly sulphated disaccharide units (E-units) of chondroitin sulphate, which binds to various functional proteins and pathogenic microorganisms.\n...\nBased on this promising pre-clinical data, blockade of CHST15 has emerged as a potentially promising therapeutic target, and such blockade can be achieved by a silencing RNA oligonucleotide molecule called GUT-1 (previously called STNM01).\nA prior phase I clinical trial demonstrated the safety of GUT-1 in patients with Crohn’s Disease [6].\nAccordingly, Atreya and colleagues now sought to evaluate the safety, as well as the efficacy and mode of action, of GUT-1 in patients with UC as part of a phase IIa placebo-controlled, clinical trial.\n...\nTwenty-eight patients were screened and of these 24 were randomised in a 1:1:1 fashion to receive a single dose of 250 nM GUT-1, 25 nM GUT-1 or placebo.\n...\nEndoscopic improvement was achieved in 71.4% of patients in the 250 nM GUT-1 arm, 0% of patients in the 25 nM GUT-1 arm and 28.6% of patients in the placebo arm.\n...\nThe mean percentage positive areas of CHST15 in the 250 nM GUT-1 arm were 19.7% (95% CI 17.0%–22.5%) at baseline, 11.5% (95% CI 6.5%–16.5%) at week 2 and 8.4% (95% CI 3.1%–13.7%) at week 4, suggesting good target engagement with a >50% drop in expression from baseline to week 4.\n...\nAt two hours after injection, the plasma concentration of GUT-1 was below the lower limit of quantification (LLOQ 0.5 ng/ml) in all treatment groups, and both lower and higher doses of GUT-1 were generally well tolerated.\n...\nAtreya and colleagues should be congratulated for conducting this trial, which provides promising evidence of safety, efficacy and target engagement for GUT-1, a selective RNA oligonucleotide molecule in patients with left-sided UC.", "date": "2024-09-27", "last_updated": "2024-11-29"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "News", "url": "https://gut-oligo.com/about/news/", "domain": "gut-oligo.com", "snippet": "Under the agreement, GUT Inc. and Dokkyo Medical University will jointly conduct pharmacokinetic and efficacy studies, including animal studies, using GUT Inc.’s CHST15-targeting oligonucleotide therapeutics.\n...\nThe research aims to further evaluate the pharmacological properties and therapeutic potential of GUT Inc.’s novel CHST15-targeted nucleic acid medicines.\n...\nThe results from Independent Investigational Trial (IIT) study of GUT-1 as an add-on therapy for moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis were published in the Inflammation and Regeneration.\n...\nThis was a single arm IIT study in Japan.\nIn **moderate-to-severe UC** patients who **lost response to TNF antagonists** (n=5), **add-on multiple injections of GUT-1** (250 nM) to TNF antagonist therapy as an induction therapy showed:\n- **60% of clinical remission at week 6.**\n- **80% of endoscopic improvement at week 6.**\n- **80% of histological improvement at week 6.**\nThe results would also provide new insights into treatment concept of biologics-maintenance therapy – Adding local therapy of distinct MOA like GUT-1, rather than simply switching, would be an alternative option for chronic disease like UC that repeats relapses and remissions.\n...\nThe results from Phase 2a clinical study of GUT-1 in active ulcerative colitis patients were published in the Journal of Crohn’s and Colitis.\n...\nThis was a phase IIa RCT in Germany (PI: **Prof. Markus Neurarh**, Erlangen Univ.).\nIn **moderate-to-severe UC** patients (n=24) whose previous TNF antagonists were washed-out for 4 weeks, **single injection** of **GUT-1** (250 nM) as an induction therapy showed:\n- **57.1% of clinical remission (vs placebo 14.3%) by week 4.**\n- **71.4% of endoscopic improvement (vs placebo 28.6%) by week 4.**\n- **42.9% of histological improvement (vs placebo 0.0%) by week 4.**", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-10"}], "raw": {"id": "29f3d2d2-d653-4d9f-b729-5098ff46b12a"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:07+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q043", "objective": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease competing agents in the same mechanistic class as VNN1, including Small Molecule therapies", "anchors": ["MY009"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q043-batch", "query": ["MY009 VNN1 inflammatory bowel disease competing agents"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["MY009 VNN1 inflammatory bowel disease competing agents"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 585.0, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "The Present and Future of Inflammatory Bowel Disease ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4969780/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Anti-tumor necrosis factor (TNF) drugs currently include infliximab (Remicade, Janssen), adalimumab (Humira, AbbVie), and certolizumab pegol (Cimzia, UCB).\n...\nApproximately 2 years ago, the anti— alpha-4 beta-7 integrin antibody vedolizumab (Entyvio, Takeda) was approved for induction and maintenance of Crohn’s disease.\nThis drug is fairly slow-acting, so it is somewhat better for maintenance than for induction.\nIt is selective in its targeting, even though it is administered intravenously, so it does not have black box warnings for serious infection and malignancy as anti-TNF agents do.\nThere is emerging use of vedolizumab as first-line biologic therapy because it tends to show better results in patients who have not previously had anti-TNF drugs and because it is likely safer than anti-TNF drugs.\n...\n**WS** Etrolizumab (Genentech) is an interesting drug in the same general class as vedolizumab that is currently being tested in phase 3 trials in patients with ulcerative colitis as well as in patients with Crohn’s disease.\nLike vedolizumab, etrolizumab blocks alpha-4 beta-7, but it also blocks alpha-E beta-7, which affects lymphocyte trafficking to the skin and to the gut.\n...\n**WS** Anti-P40 and anti-P19 antibodies block signaling through the Th1 and Th17 pathways.\nUstekinumab (Stelara, Janssen) is an anti-P40 antibody that blocks the P40 subunit of interleukin (IL)-12 and -23.\nThis agent is currently approved by the FDA for psoriasis and psoriatic arthritis and has finished phase 3 testing in Crohn’s disease.\nClinical trial data have recently been presented in abstract form showing that ustekinumab is effective for induction of remission in patients with inflammatory bowel disease who are failing conventional therapy (not anti-TNF drugs) and separately for induction of remission in patients who have failed anti-TNF drugs.\nA maintenance trial mixed these 2 populations and showed that the drug is effective for maintaining remission for over a year.\n...\n**WS** Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors block a variety of proinflammatory cytokines by blocking the JAK/Signal Transducer and Activator of Transcription signaling pathway.\nTofacitinib (Xeljanz, Pfizer), which is currently approved by the FDA for rheumatoid arthritis, is a small molecule that blocks predominantly JAK1 and JAK3 receptors but also has some JAK2 effects at higher doses.\nA phase 2 study showed that the drug was highly effective in ulcerative colitis, and two phase 3 studies recently showed that the drug was effective for inducing response, remission, and mucosal healing, both in patients with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis who are failing anti-TNF drugs as well as in patients who are naive to anti-TNF therapy.\nA phase 3 maintenance trial will be completed in the third quarter of this year, which means that tofacitinib may be sent for FDA review sometime next year.\n...\n**WS** A metalloproteinase-9 antibody (GS-5745, Gilead) showed some evidence of efficacy in a phase 1A study in ulcerative colitis and will be undergoing phase 2/3 trials in ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease.\n...\n**WS** Most of these agents are being studied in patients who have failed mesalamine, corticosteroids, and perhaps immunosuppressants.\nSome, but not all, of the protocols require failure of a biologic agent as well.\nThese agents are not being examined for first-line treatment in Crohn’s disease or ulcerative colitis.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-23"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Current status of novel biologics and small molecule drugs ...", "url": "https://www.wjgnet.com/1007-9327/full/v28/i48/6888.htm", "domain": "wjgnet.com", "snippet": "In the last decade, increasing numbers of new biologics and SMDs have been developed for IBD treatment[4], and a novel therapy combining different biologics and/or SMDs targeting multiple inflammatory signalling pathways, which is called dual targeted therapy (DTT), has begun to emerge in recent years[5].\n...\nTo date, the approved biological and small molecule therapies for IBD consist of the following anti-tumor necrosis factor (TNF) agents [infliximab (IFX), adalimumab (ADA), certolizumab (CZP), golimumab (GOL)], anti-adhesion agents [vedolizumab (VDZ), natalizumab (NAT)], anti-interleukin (IL)-12/23 agents [ustekinumab (UST)], and Janus kinase (JAK) inhibitors (tofacitinib).\n...\nVDZ is the most commonly used antiadhesion agent with a selective blocking effect of the α4β7 integrin in the intestine[20].\n...\nWith the permission of the FDA and EMA, VDZ has become the first choice among second-line biologics for moderate to severe CD and UC patients who have experienced failure with conventional medications or anti-TNF agents[31].\nThus, in clinical practice, anti-TNF + VDZ is the most common combination paradigm used in DTT.", "date": "2022-12-28", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Vascular non-inflammatory molecule 1 (VNN1) | Gosset", "url": "https://gosset.ai/targets/vascular-non-inflammatory-molecule-1", "domain": "gosset.ai", "snippet": "Therapeutic strategies targeting Vanin-1 focus on small-molecule inhibition to reduce cysteamine-mediated oxidative damage and dampen chronic inflammation (PubMed: 24911123).\nExperimental inhibitors like RR6 have shown promise in preclinical models by modulating the inflammatory response without significantly disrupting systemic pantothenate levels.\nConsequently, Vanin-1 is being explored as a drug target for treating inflammatory bowel disease, metabolic disorders, and acute kidney injury.\n...\nInhibition of pantetheinase activity to prevent the conversion of pantetheine into pantothenic acid and cysteamine, thereby modulating oxidative stress and inflammatory pathways.\n...\n## Interacting drugs\nRR6", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Methods and pharmaceutical compositions for repairing intestinal ...", "url": "https://patents.google.com/patent/US20250009694A1/en", "domain": "patents.google.com", "snippet": "Based on the VIVA transgenic model of Vnn1 overexpression on intestinal cells, the inventors show that the epithelial pantetheinase Vnn1 has a dual effect on colon: (1) its enzymatic products, cysteamine and pantothenic acid (vitamin B5) enhance coenzyme A regeneration and colon fitness through metabolic rewiring: (2) they favor microbiota-dependent accumulation of butyrate, previously shown to regulate mucosal energetics and to be reduced in IBD patients.\n...\nOur pioneering studies in mouse showed that the Vnn1 pantetheinase isoform regulates metabolic, inflammatory and cytoprotective programs in diverse tissues [28-32].\n...\nLikewise, the colonic epithelium of IBD patients displays high VNN1 levels [34].\nIn addition, SNPs in the regulatory regions of the VNN1 gene are associated with disease risk and some VNN1 high patients harbouring specific VNN1 SNPs develop a severe disease [34].\n...\nIn the management of patients with inflammatory bowel diseases (IBD), there is a need to identify prognostic markers and druggable biological pathways to improve mucosal repair and efficacy of TNF alpha biologics.\n...\nHerein the inventors show that enhancement of vitamin B5-driven metabolism should improve mucosal healing and maintain colon fitness and might enhance the efficacy of anti-inflammatory anti-TNF alpha therapy.\n...\nIn some embodiments, the substrate of vanin-1 pantetheinase and/or product(s) of vanin-1 pantetheinase enzymatic activity (i.e the agent(s) of Vnn1 metabolism of the invention) are administered in combination with a therapeutic compound used to treat IBD or IBS.\n...\nThus, the invention refers to a method for treating inflammatory bowel disease in a subject in need thereof, comprising the step of i) determining if the subject have inflammatory bowel disease according to the method of the invention and ii) administering a substrate of vanin-1 pantetheinase and/or product(s) of vanin-1 pantetheinase enzymatic activity when the subject is determined having inflammatory bowel disease.\n...\n- Generation of the VIVA Mouse.\n...\n- High VNN1 Expression Correlates with IBD Severity.\n...\nWe previously shown that VNN1 is highly expressed in colon biopsies from IBD patients [34].\n...\nHere we show that VNN1 expression levels increase with disease severity, in both CD and UC (data not shown).\nIndeed, whereas VNN1 was barely detectable during the quiescent phases of the diseases, its expression augmented during the flares and reached the highest levels in patients resistant to anti-TNFα biologics.\n...\nAltogether, colonic VNN1 overexpression is a feature of IBD patients and reflects the degree of tissue inflammation and damage.\n...\nThus, increased levels of Vnn1 on intestinal epithelial cells in mice limit the development of DSS-induced epithelial lesions, immune-mediated damage and disease severity.\n...\nOur current work clearly established that high VNN1 expression is associated with the degree of inflammation, disease severity and response to therapy in IBD patients.\n...\nOf major interest, we could demonstrated that co-administration of the products of Vnn1 activity (ie. Pan and CEA) to normal mice recapitulates most of the protective phenotypes conferred by the constitutive overexpression of Vnn1 in the VIVA model.\n...\nA pharmaceutical composition comprising at least one agent of Vnn1 metabolism selected from the group consisting of i) pantethine, ii) pantothenate and/or a pantothenate analog, iii) cysteamine, iv) pantothenate and cysteamine, and v) a pantothenate analog and cysteamine for use for repairing intestinal mucosal barrier in subject in need thereof.", "date": "2022-10-31", "last_updated": "2026-02-17"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Landscape of new drugs and targets in inflammatory bowel disease", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/ueg2.12305", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "Among the most promising molecules are anti-IL-23, JAK-inhibitors, anti-integrins and S1P modulators.\n...\nlthough UST (an inhibitor of p40 subunit) has been shown to be effective in IBD,^8^ the p19 antagonists (selective IL-23 inhibitors) have proven to be more effective than UST in other immune-mediated conditions, leading us to evaluate them in IBD.^15, 16^ Four IL-23 p19 inhibitors are currently being evaluated in IBD: risankizumab, guselkumab, brazikumab and mirikizumab.", "date": "2022-12-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting the Unmet Needs in IBD: Emerging Therapies Beyond ...", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1471489225000736", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-30"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease Biomarkers - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10321231/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α) inhibitors, also called biologics, are available as injectables to treat IBD.", "date": "2022-05-23", "last_updated": "2026-07-25"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "VNN1 Gene: Vanin 1 - Function, Mutations, and Disease ...", "url": "https://www.editxor.com/genemap/VNN1", "domain": "editxor.com", "snippet": "VNN1 encodes vanin 1, a glycosylphosphatidylinositol (GPI)-anchored pantetheinase that hydrolyzes pantetheine to pantothenic acid (vitamin B5) and cysteamine.\nIt is involved in oxidative stress response, inflammatory regulation, and cellular metabolism.\n...\n|Inflammatory bowel disease|VNN1 regulates oxidative stress and immune cell recruitment; altered expression linked to intestinal inflammation.|NCBI Gene, OMIM|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Inflammatory Bowel Disease: Understanding Therapeutic Effects of ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12649937/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Five major classes of advanced therapies, comprising biologics and oral small molecules, are currently approved, each targeting different points of the inflammatory cascade: tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-α antagonists, adhesion and trafficking inhibitors (including anti-integrin agents and sphingosine1-phosphate receptor modulators), interleukin Inhibitors, and Janus kinase inhibitors.\n...\nGuselkumab, the newest IL-23 inhibitor available for IBD treatment, was first approved for UC in 2024 in the United States, based on results from the QUASAR phase 3 induction and maintenance [15] trials.\n...\nThe two JAK 1 selective inhibitors approved for IBD are upadacitinib and filgotinib.", "date": "2025-10-30", "last_updated": "2026-08-20"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Revealing VNN1: An Emerging and Promising Target ...", "url": "https://www.mendeley.com/catalogue/e7fd8f35-4326-382c-a859-c502a2656f47/", "domain": "mendeley.com", "snippet": "Inhibiting VNN1, either genetically or pharmacologically with compounds like RR6, OMP-7, or natural products such as oleuropein, demonstrates significant anti-inflammatory and antioxidant effects in preclinical models.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-23"}], "raw": {"id": "e7e66983-e0cf-4abb-8d29-6690a973f788"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:08+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q042", "objective": "Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis, Duration \u00143 Months, Modified Mayo Score 5 to 9, With Inadequate Response, Loss of Response, or Intolerance to Prior Standard-of-Care Medications competing agents in the same mechanistic class as TYK2 Small Molecule", "anchors": ["D-2570"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q042-batch", "query": ["D-2570 TYK2 ulcerative colitis competitor Phase 2 status"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["D-2570 TYK2 ulcerative colitis competitor Phase 2 status"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 2230.9, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs ...", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/gastroenterology/articles/10.3389/fgstr.2026.1747118/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "|TYK2 Inhibitor|D-2570|Novel oral TYK2 inhibitor|NCT07035041|Phase 2|Recruiting; began May 2025, estimated completion Dec 2026|\n...\nIt is being tested in a Phase 2, multicenter, placebo-controlled, double-blind RCT for moderately to severely active UC in 207 participants aged 18–75 who have inadequate response to conventional, biologic, or advanced therapies, with the primary outcome measuring clinical remission at week 12 using mMS, currently recruiting with estimated completion in August 2027 (NCT06254950).\n...\n#### 2.1.9 D-2570\nD-2570 is a novel oral TYK2 inhibitor being developed for autoimmune diseases including psoriasis and UC evaluated in a Phase 2 multicenter double-blind, placebo-controlled RCT (NCT07035041) with 120 participants randomized 1:1:1 to two different D-2570 dosing arms or placebo for 12 weeks.\n...\nThe study began May 2025 with estimated primary completion July 2026 and final completion December 2026, currently in the recruiting Phase with no results yet available as the trial is ongoing.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Advancing therapeutic frontiers: a pipeline of novel drugs for ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|TYK2 Inhibitor|Zasocitinib (TAK-279)|AI-designed highly selective allosteric TYK2 inhibitor|NCT06254950, NCT06764615|Phase 2|Recruiting; estimated completion Aug 2027|\n...\n|TYK2 Inhibitor|D-2570|Novel oral TYK2 inhibitor|NCT07035041|Phase 2|Recruiting; began May 2025, estimated completion Dec 2026|\n...\nIt is being tested in a Phase 2, multicenter, placebo-controlled, double-blind RCT for moderately to severely active UC in 207 participants aged 18–75 who have inadequate response to conventional, biologic, or advanced therapies, with the primary outcome measuring clinical remission at week 12 using mMS, currently recruiting with estimated completion in August 2027 (NCT06254950).\n...\n#### 2.1.9. D-2570\nD-2570 is a novel oral TYK2 inhibitor being developed for autoimmune diseases including psoriasis and UC evaluated in a Phase 2 multicenter double-blind, placebo-controlled RCT (NCT07035041) with 120 participants randomized 1:1:1 to two different D-2570 dosing arms or placebo for 12 weeks.", "date": "2026-01-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "SUMMARY", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2026/108029/a128849/sehk26010100538.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "Within our clinical-stage pipeline, our Core Products tyrosine kinase 2 (“TYK2”) inhibitor D-2570 and oral estrogen receptor degrader (“SERD”) taragarestrant (D-0502) are both advancing through Phase III registration trials, while our urate transporter 1 (“URAT1”) inhibitor darbinurad (D-0120) has completed a Phase IIb clinical trial.\n...\nD-2570\nTYK2\nAutoimmune \ndiseases\nSubmit NDA in H2 2027\nGlobal\nInitiate Phase III trial in H1 2026\nComplete Phase II trial in H2 2026\nComplete Phase I trial in H1 2026\nInitiate Phase II trial in H1 2026\nInitiate Phase II trial in H1 2026\nSubmit IND in H1 2026\n...\nOur Core Product D-2570 is a self-developed, orally administered, highly selective TYK2 allosteric inhibitor for the treatment of psoriasis and other autoimmune diseases.\nOur Phase II clinical trial in moderate-to-severe psoriasis delivered outstanding results, with efficacy significantly superior to both placebo and approved products of the same class (based on published clinical data), and comparable to that of biologics, while maintaining strong safety and tolerability.\nWe have agreed with the NMPA on the Phase III clinical trial protocol and have initiated the registrational Phase III clinical trial in September 2025.\nIn parallel, we have also commenced a Phase II clinical study for ulcerative colitis (“UC”) in China.\n...\nD-2570 is a TYK2 allosteric inhibitor under development primarily for the treatment of plaque psoriasis and UC.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-06"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Top 7 Leading Drug Candidates in Ulcerative Colitis Treatment", "url": "https://www.delveinsight.com/blog/emerging-drugs-for-ulcerative-colitis-treatment", "domain": "delveinsight.com", "snippet": "**Takeda’s Zasocitinib**\n*Phase II*\n**Zasocitinib (TAK-279)** is an oral, investigational TYK2 inhibitor being studied for moderate-to-severe ulcerative colitis.\nIt is designed to reduce inflammation by targeting the IL-23/TYK2 pathway, and Takeda is evaluating it in phase 2 studies for both ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including a long-term extension trial for patients who responded in the parent studies.", "date": "2026-06-25", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Study Evaluating ISM5411 Administered Orally to Subjects With Active Ulcerative Colitis (BETHESDA)", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT07265570", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "snippet": "NCT02368717 ·Status: COMPLETED ·Phase: PHASE3\n...\n- A Phase 2 Study of D-2570 in Subjects With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis\nNCT07035041 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE2", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-05"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Interleukin-23p19 Inhibitors in Inflammatory Bowel Disease", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12942054/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In the ANTHEM-UC trial, a phase 2b study involving 252 adults with moderate-to-severe UC, preliminary results reported dose-dependent improvements in clinical response rates.\n...\nIn addition, small-molecule inhibitors targeting JAK1 and TYK2, which act downstream of the IL-23 receptor, are currently under clinical development.\nDeucr\navacitinib is a selective, allosteric inhibitor of TYK2, an intracellular signaling mediator of IL-23.\nHowever, in three phase 2 clinical trials in patients with moderately to severely active CD and UC (LATTICE-CD, LATTICE-UC, and IM011-127), deucravacitinib demonstrated modest signals of efficacy, but failed to achieve the primary or secondary efficacy endpoints after 12 weeks of treatment across multiple dosing regimens [48].\n...\nOf interest, a next-generation allosteric TYK2 inhibitor, TAK-279 (zasocitinib), is currently in phase 2 evaluation for both UC and CD [51,52].\nTAK-279 is designed for sustained 24 h pathway suppression and these trials will help understand if deeper TYK2 target engagement can translate to mucosal efficacy in IBD.", "date": "2026-02-14", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "A Study on the Safety of TAK-279 and Whether it Can Reduce Inflammation in the Bowel of Participants With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis", "url": "https://med.agiltrace.com/clinical-trials/NCT06254950", "domain": "med.agiltrace.com", "snippet": "NCT03943446 ·Status: TERMINATED ·Phase: PHASE2\n- A Phase 2 Study of D-2570 in Subjects With Moderately to Severely Active Ulcerative Colitis\nNCT07035041 ·Status: ACTIVE_NOT_RECRUITING ·Phase: PHASE2", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-05"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging Therapies for Ulcerative Colitis: Updates from Recent ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10441644/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "OST-122 is a gut selective oral JAK inhibitor with selectivity for JAK3, TYK2 and AMPK-related protein kinase 5 (ARK5).\nThe phase Ib/IIa trial which enrolled 32 patients with moderate-to-severe UC has been recently completed and results are yet to be published (NCT04353791).84\n...\nDeucravacitinib is an oral TYK2 inhibitor in development for the treatment of moderate-to-severe UC.\nIn a phase II, double-blind trial (LATTICE-UC), 131 patients were randomized to deucravacitinib 6 mg twice daily vs placebo.89 The trial failed to meet its primary endpoint of clinical remission at 12 weeks defined by the modified Mayo score (stool frequency sub-score ≤1 with a decrease from baseline of at least 1 point, a rectal bleeding sub-score of 0, and an endoscopy sub-score of ≤1).", "date": "2023-08-17", "last_updated": "2026-08-12"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "BUSINESS", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/app/sehk/2026/108029/a128849/sehk26010100550.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "inhibitor D-2570 and oral estrogen receptor degrader (“SERD”) taragarestrant (D-0502) are both advancing through Phase III registration trials, while our urate transporter 1 (“URAT1”) inhibitor darbinurad (D-0120) has completed a Phase IIb clinical trial.\n...\nOur Core Product D-2570 is a self-developed, orally administered, highly selective TYK2 allosteric inhibitor for the treatment of psoriasis and other autoimmune diseases.\n...\nWe have agreed with the NMPA on the Phase III clinical trial protocol and have initiated the registrational Phase III clinical trial in September 2025.\nIn parallel, we have also commenced a Phase II clinical study for ulcerative colitis (“UC”) in China.\n...\nWe have initiated the registrational Phase III clinical trial of D-2570 for moderate-to- severe plaque psoriasis in China and are actively preparing for expansion into additional\n...\nMeanwhile, we will continue with the China Phase II clinical trial targeting UC and the U.S.\n...\nD-2570 (TYK2 inhibitor): The U.S. Phase I clinical trial has been initiated.\nThe clinical data are expected in the first half of 2026.\n...\nD-2570 is an oral selective allosteric inhibitor of TYK2 under clinical development for the treatment of autoimmune diseases, including psoriasis and UC.\n...\nCurrently, a registrational Phase III clinical trial of D-2570 targeting moderate-to-severe plaque psoriasis is ongoing.\nWe expect to complete the trial in the second half of 2027 and thereafter submit to the NMPA an NDA for D-2570.\nMeanwhile, we are exploring the potential of D-2570 as an induction therapy for moderately to severely active UC.\nA Phase II clinical trial is ongoing for that purpose in China, and we expect to complete the trial in the second half of 2026.\nWe expect to conduct other\nPhase II clinical trials targeting PsA and SLE in China in the first half of 2026.\nWe have also initiated Phase I clinical trial in U.S., with completion expected in the first half of 2026.\n...\nWe initiated the Phase II clinical trial in January 2024 and completed the trial in April 2025.\n...\nWe initiated the trial in September 2025 and expect to complete the trial in the second half of 2027.\nA Phase II clinical trial to evaluate the safety and efficacy of D-2570 as induction therapy in subjects with moderately to severely active UC in China sponsored by us\nOverview.\nThis is a multicenter, randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled Phase II clinical trial.\nThe target population is the patients with moderately to severely active UC.\nThe primary objective is to conduct a preliminary evaluation of the efficacy of different doses of\nD-2570 in the treatment of moderately to severely active UC.\n...\nTrial design.\nA total of 120 subjects are planned to be included.\nThe trial is divided into two phases: the induction treatment phase and the extended treatment phase.\n...\nTrial status.\nWe initiated the Phase II clinical trial in May 2025 and expect to complete the trial in the second half of 2026.\n...\nIn March 2025, the CDE granted the IND approval to conduct the Phase II clinical trial of D-2570 as induction therapy in subjects with moderately to severely active UC.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-06"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Table 1.", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12952356/table/T1/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|JAK Inhibitor|Brepocitinib (PF-06700841)|Oral dual TYK2/JAK1 inhibitor|NCT02958865 (VIBRATO)|Phase 2b|Completed May 2021; advanced to alopecia areata trials|\n...\n|TYK2 Inhibitor|D-2570|Novel oral TYK2 inhibitor|NCT07035041|Phase 2|Recruiting; began May 2025, estimated completion Dec 2026|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-14"}], "raw": {"id": "039b1554-c2de-4efe-a9e7-6af354dc0884"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:08+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q045", "objective": "BMF-500 first regulatory approval or market launch year in acute myeloid leukemia", "anchors": ["BMF-500"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q045-batch", "query": ["BMF-500 first regulatory approval AML", "BMF-500 expected regulatory approval AML", "BMF-500 market launch AML", "BMF-500 expected launch date AML"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["BMF-500 first regulatory approval AML", "BMF-500 expected regulatory approval AML", "BMF-500 market launch AML", "BMF-500 expected launch date AML"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 2284.1, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "BMF-500 - Drug Targets, Indications, Patents", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/drug/0e8fac2d50284b9fac3c91177d16d79c", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Active Indication Acute Myeloid Leukemia|\n...\n|Drug Highest PhasePhase 1|\n|First Approval Date-|\n|Regulation-|\n...\nNCT05918692\n/ Active, not recruitingPhase 1\n...\n|Acute Myeloid Leukemia|Phase 1|United States|Biomea Fusion, Inc.|26 Jul 2023|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "BMF-500(BMF-500) - 药物靶点：FLT3_在研适应症：急性髓性白血病 ...", "url": "https://synapse.zhihuiya.com/drug/0e8fac2d50284b9fac3c91177d16d79c", "domain": "synapse.zhihuiya.com", "snippet": "BMF-500(BMF-500) - 药物靶点：FLT3_在研适应症：急性髓性白血病_专利_临床_研发\n...\n|在研适应症 急性髓性白血病|\n...\n|最高研发阶段临床1期|\n|首次获批日期-|\n|最高研发阶段(中国)-|\n...\nA Phase 1 first-in-human dose-escalation and dose-expansion study of BMF-500, an oral FLT3 inhibitor, in adult patients with acute leukemia.\n...\nMedian overall survival (mOS) among all treated FLT3m patients (n=18) was 3.8 months (Arm A) and 3.5 months (Arm B) during dose escalation.\n...\n|急性髓性白血病|临床1", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Loading Inline Form.", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/ix?doc=/Archives/edgar/data/0001840439/000095017024118366/bmea-20240930.htm", "domain": "sec.gov", "snippet": "In January 2022, we announced that we had initiated dosing in COVALENT-101, a Phase 1 clinical trial to explore the safety and efficacy of icovamenib in patients with relapsed/refractory AML and acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), including those with MLL/KMT2A gene arrangements or NPM1 mutations.\n...\nIn December 2023, we reported the achievement of minimal residual disease negativity (MRD-neg) in first complete responder in a participant with AML.\n...\nIn May 2022, we announced the nomination of our second development candidate, BMF-500, a third-generation oral covalent small molecule inhibitor of activating mutations of the FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3), which are the most frequent genetic alt\neration in AML and are associated with poor prognosis.\n...\nIn May 2023, we announced FDA clearance of our IND to study BMF-500 in a Phase 1 study (COVALENT-103) examining safety and efficacy in patients with relapsed or refractory acute leukemia with FLT3 wild-type and FLT3 mutations, including those with MLLr/NPM1 mutations.\nIn \nOctober 2023, we announced the first participant had been dosed with BMF-500 in relapsed or refractory acute leukemia.\nWe anticipate that the dose escalation for COVALENT-103 will be completed by year end 2024.\n...\nIn addition, our lead product candidate, icovamenib, is in clinical development, and we dosed the first participant with our second product candidate, BMF-500, in October 2023, following clearance of our IND by the FDA in May 2023.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-21"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "EX-99.1", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840439/000095017024039304/bmea-ex99_1.htm", "domain": "sec.gov", "snippet": "In 2023, reported initial topline data from Phase 1 study of BMF-219 in acute myeloid leukemia (AML); continuing to progress COVALENT-101 in liquid tumors, COVALENT-102 in solid tumors and COVALENT-103 (BMF-500) in AML.\n...\nIn 2023 BMF-500 entered the clinic as the only covalent FLT3 inhibitor in clinical development.”\n...\nWe expect these data will inform potentially registrational studies, which we plan to start in 2025, pending discussions with regulatory authorities.\n...\n•\nAnnounced FDA clearance of IND for BMF-500 in COVALENT-103, a Phase 1 study, and started enrollment of Biomea’s novel third generation investigational oral covalent inhibitor of FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-29"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "10-K", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840439/000095017023010312/bmea-20221231.htm", "domain": "sec.gov", "snippet": "In May 2022, we announced the nomination of our second development candidate, BMF-500, a covalent inhibitor of FLT3, and presented preclinical data on this program in December 2022.\nWe expect to file an Investigational New Drug application (IND) with the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) to study BMF-500 in acute leukemias in the first half of 2023.\n...\nWe expect to file an\nIND to study BMF-500 in acute leukemias in the first half of 2023.\n...\nIn May 2022, we announced our second development candidate, BMF-500, a covalent inhibitor of FLT3.\nBMF-500 is being developed as a highly potent and selective, covalent, small molecule inhibitor of FLT3, that is designed to bind irreversibly to a reactive cysteine in the kinase active site.\n...\nPotent FLT3 inhibition and high selectivity of BMF-500 translated to sustained tumor regression and improved survival in both subcutaneous and disseminated xenograft models of mutant FLT3-driven AML.\n...\nWe cannot commercialize product candidates in the United States without first obtaining regulatory approval from the FDA.\n...\nWe expect to file an Investigational New Drug application (IND) with the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) to study BMF-500 in acute leukemias in the first half of 2023.", "date": "2023-03-28", "last_updated": "2025-04-01"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Biomea Fusion, Inc.", "url": "https://synapse.patsnap.com/organization/26bae87fc714320a3f21ce8509ae5e58", "domain": "synapse.patsnap.com", "snippet": "|Inactive Indication Acute Myeloid Leukemia [+12]|\n|Drug Highest PhasePhase 2|\n|First Approval Ctry.\n/ Loc.-|\n|First Approval Date-|\nBMF-500\n|Target FLT3|\n...\n|Active Indication Acute Myeloid Leukemia|\n|Inactive Indication-|\n|Drug Highest PhasePhase 1|\n|First Approval Ctry.\n/ Loc.-|\n...\n|BMF-500 ( FLT3 )|Acute Myeloid Leukemia More|Phase 1|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "10-K - SEC.gov", "url": "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1840439/000119312526121825/bmea-20251231.htm", "domain": "sec.gov", "snippet": "We have a limited operating history, limited experience in conducting clinical trials, have not completed the clinical development of any product candidates, have no products approved for commercial sale, and have not generated any revenue, which may make it difficult for you to evaluate our current business and likelihood of success and viability.\n...\nIn September 2025, we announced that we received IND clearance from the FDA for BMF-650, and initiated our ongoing Phase I trial in obese, otherwise healthy volunteers.\nIn January 2025, we announced that we plan to conclude our studies exploring icovamenib’s potential in oncology and explore partnerships to further advance our oncology assets, including BMF-500.\n...\nWe have not received marketing approval for any product candidate, and we have not generated any revenue from any product sales or other sources since our inception.\nWe do not expect to generate revenue unless or until we successfully complete preclinical and clinical development and obtain regulatory approval of, and then successfully commercialize, at least one product candidate.\n...\nIn September 2025, we announced that BMF-650, our investigational, next-generation, oral small molecule GLP-1 RA, received IND-clearance from the FDA.\nInitiation of our Phase I GLP-131 trial for BMF-650 in obese, otherwise healthy volunteers is ongoing, and data is anticipated in the second quarter of 2026.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "BMF-500 / Biomea Fusion - LARVOL DELTA", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=7dcd09a2-09b0-4543-b9f1-8d95262deb65", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "## COVALENT-103: A Phase 1 Study of BMF-500 in Adults With Acute Leukemia (clinicaltrials.gov) - P1 | N=35 | Completed | Sponsor: Biomea Fusion Inc. | Active, not recruiting ➔ Completed | Trial primary completion date: Apr 2026 ➔ Jan 2026\n...\n## Biomea Fusion Announces FDA Clearance of Investigational New Drug (IND) Application for Covalent FLT3 Inhibitor BMF-500 in Relapsed or Refractory Acute Leukemia (GlobeNewswire) - \"Biomea Fusion...announced that the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has cleared the company's Investigational New Drug (IND) application to begin a Phase I trial (COVALENT-103) of BMF-500, an investigational covalent FLT3 inhibitor, in adult patients with relapsed or refractory acute leukemia....Phase I study (COVALENT-103) of BMF-500 will examine its safety and efficacy in patients with relapsed or refractory acute leukemia with FLT3 wild-type and FLT3 mutations, including those with MLLr / NPM1 mutations.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Biomea Fusion (BMEA) FDA Approvals, PDUFA Dates & Drug Alerts 2026 $BMEA", "url": "https://www.marketbeat.com/stocks/NASDAQ/BMEA/fda-events/?drug=icovamenib&anchor=fda-icovamenib-2026-06-05", "domain": "marketbeat.com", "snippet": "Given that the GLP-1 receptors in monkeys are functionally similar to those in humans, the findings support further clinical development of BMF-650 as a promising, patient-friendly treatment for obesity.Read Announcement BMF-500 FDA Regulatory Timeline and Events BMF-500 is a drug developed by Biomea Fusion for the following indication: Orally Bioavailable Covalent Inhibitor of FLT3.\nThis drug is under review by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA).\nBelow is a timeline of key regulatory milestones for this therapy.\nUpdated data - June 13,2025Updated Data Phase 1Drug: BMF-500Announced Date: June 13, 2025Indication: Orally Bioavailable Covalent Inhibitor of FLT3 AnnouncementBiomea Fusion, Inc.\nannounced updated preliminary clinical data from the ongoing Phase I COVALENT-103 trial of BMF-500 in adults with relapsed or refractory (\"R/R\") acute leukemia (\"AL\").\n...\nThe trial focuses on safety, pharmacokinetics, and early signs of clinical activity in heavily pretreated patients, many with FLT3 mutations.\nSignificant data include bone marrow blast reductions in several patients, with some achieving a complete remission with incomplete hematologic recovery or a morphologic leukemia-free state.\n...\nThe study has enrolled 20 patients with relapsed or refractory acute leukemia, including 13 with FLT3 mutations, all of whom had previously failed treatment with gilteritinib.\n...\nIn the past two years, Biomea Fusion (BMEA) has not received FDA approval for any therapies.\nHowever, the company does have drugs under review or in active clinical development.\n...\nIn the past two years, Biomea Fusion (BMEA) has reported FDA regulatory activity for the following drugs: icovamenib, BMF-650, BMF-500, BMF-219 and COVALENT-111.", "date": "2026-06-08", "last_updated": "2026-06-22"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Biomea Fusion Reports Second Quarter 2025 Financial ...", "url": "https://investors.biomeafusion.com/news-releases/news-release-details/biomea-fusion-reports-second-quarter-2025-financial-results-and", "domain": "investors.biomeafusion.com", "snippet": "**BMF-500 (Oral Small Molecule FLT3 Inhibitor in Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML))**\n- Presented updated Phase I data at EHA 2025, showing sustained antileukemic responses, deep bone marrow blast reductions, and survival benefit in relapsed/refractory FLT3-mutant AML patients, all of whom had failed FLT3 inhibitor gilteritinib.\n- The Company concluded its oncology efforts and is now exploring strategic partnerships for BMF-500.", "date": "2025-08-05", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}], "raw": {"id": "f8b1a434-e56a-44f0-8edd-e71a4a56e022"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:11+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q046", "objective": "BW-101 first regulatory approval or market launch year in acute myeloid leukemia", "anchors": ["BW-101"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q046-batch", "query": ["BW-101 AML regulatory approval market launch year"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["BW-101 AML regulatory approval market launch year"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 929.9, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "NDA and BLA Approvals", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/drug-and-biologic-approval-and-ind-activity-reports/nda-and-bla-approvals", "domain": "fda.gov", "snippet": "Please see the list below for available calendar year reports on New Drug Application (NDA) and Biologic License Application (BLA) approvals.", "date": "2024-10-06", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "JavaScript is required...", "url": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/patent/PH-12014501719-A1", "domain": "pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Please enable Javascript in order to use PubChem website.\nAn official website of the United States government", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-08-05"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "MeSH Linked Data", "url": "https://id.nlm.nih.gov/mesh/C506979.html", "domain": "id.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Skip Navigation\n...\nProducts and Services\n...\nFor the Public\n...\nSPARQL Query Editor", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-12"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Bloomberg", "url": "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2019-06-03/amgen-drug-spurs-responses-on-white-whale-cancer-target", "domain": "bloomberg.com", "snippet": "For inquiries related to this message please contact our support team and provide the reference ID below.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2024-08-27"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Disclaimer", "url": "https://www.bioinvent.se/en/mid-disclaimer/1279/886", "domain": "bioinvent.se", "snippet": "*Access to the information and documents on this section of the website is restricted for regulatory reasons.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-25"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Regulatory timeline - OnCo", "url": "https://onco.cc/regulatory/", "domain": "onco.cc", "snippet": "|29 Oct 2021|AsciminibFDA 2026-09-22 Small-molecule allosteric ABL1 inhibitor (STAMP)|Accelerated approval|US|Adult patients with Philadelphia chromosome-positive chronic myeloid leukemia (Ph+ CML) in chronic phase (CP), previously treated with 2 or more tyrosine kinase inhibitors (TKIs)Accelerated approval on a surrogate endpoint, with a confirmatory trial required.|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "NCI Drug Dictionary", "url": "https://www.cancer.gov/publications/dictionaries/cancer-drug/def/wt1-a10-as01b-immunotherapeutic-gsk2130579a", "domain": "cancer.gov", "snippet": "NCI Dictionaries\n...\nYou need to enable JavaScript to run this app.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-18"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Acute Myeloid Leukemia Market Summary", "url": "https://www.delveinsight.com/report-store/acute-myeloid-leukemia-aml-market", "domain": "delveinsight.com", "snippet": "|**BP1001**|Interim safety and efficacy of BP1001 in a Phase II acute myeloid leukemia (AML) study.|6511|- Total evaluable- Number of partial remission: 1 (5%), number of stable disease: 3 (15%) - Total enrolled- Number of partial remission: 1 (4%), number of stable disease: 3 (11%) - In relapsed/refractory, among enrolled patients, median survival was 5.7 months.\nAmong evaluable patients, median survival was 5.9 months| |", "date": "2026-06-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Register of pharmaceutical products - Bulgarian Drug Agency", "url": "https://www.bda.bg/en/registers/3076-register-of-pharmaceutical-