{"row_id": "q051", "objective": "DWP216 in vivo monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in KRAS G12C Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["DWP216"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q051-batch", "query": ["DWP216 KRAS G12C pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma monotherapy xenograft tumour growth inhibition regression"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["DWP216 KRAS G12C pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma monotherapy xenograft tumour growth inhibition regression"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 1703.3, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Key Considerations for Targeting KRAS in Pancreatic Cancer - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13037533/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|Divarasib (GDC-6036)|Covalent KRAS G12C inhibitor|Phase 1|Basket trial with advanced/solid malignancies; PR 36% (8/22 non-NSCLC/CRC patients, 3 with PDAC); Grade ≥3 TRAEs in 12%; no fatal TRAEs, discontinuation in 3%|", "date": "2026-03-27", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Modeling response to the KRAS-G12C inhibitor AZD4625 in KRAS G12C NSCLC patient-derived xenografts reveals insights into primary resistance mechanisms", "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41416-025-03216-w", "domain": "nature.com", "snippet": "To ensure reproducibility of G12Ci sensitivity in vivo, NCI-H358 cell-derived xenografts were treated with AZD4625, which led to stable disease (Supplemental Fig. S1D).\nAlso, the PDX PHLC207 was treated with AZD4625 or sotorasib for 4 weeks (Fig. 1e).\nBoth monotherapies led to similar tumour regression and both compounds were well tolerated by mice, suggesting similar antitumor efficacy.\nAZD4625 monotherapy is sensitising in subset of KRAS G12C PDX models\n...\nPHLC82, PHLC151, PHLC194 and PHLC207 regressed on AZD4625 treatment (had a rate of change in tumour volume below 1, meaning volume was decreasing) and were categorised as AZD4625-sensitive, while the remaining eight models that continued to grow on AZD4625 were classified as AZD4625-resistant (Fig. 2a).\n...\nThrough a 1 month-long drug monotherapy treatment regimen, tumour regression occurred in 4/12 (33.3%) of our tumour models.", "date": "2025-10-11", "last_updated": "2025-10-11"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "KRAS G12C Inhibitors: Clinical Progress, Resistance Mechanisms ...", "url": "https://www.noah.bio/insight/b8ec81a6-15df-415b-8981-2cf19f47a6e0", "domain": "noah.bio", "snippet": "|Olomorasib (prior KRAS G12Ci)|LOXO-RAS-20001|I/II|Prior-KRAS G12C inhibitor NSCLC|41–42|NR|8.1–8.2|NR|No grade ≥3 in toxicity-related discontinuations|\n...\nIn PDAC specifically, KRAS G12C monotherapy with adagrasib or sotorasib has shown ORRs of approximately 21–33% and PFS of 4.0–5.4 months—meaningful but inferior to NSCLC—likely reflecting a dense immunosuppressive stroma and high prevalence of pre-existing resistance alterations (16.4–36.4% of KRAS G12C PDAC cases) 24252829.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-26"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting KRAS for the potential treatment of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma: Recent advancements provide hope (Review)", "url": "https://www.spandidos-publications.com/10.3892/or.2023.8643?text=fulltext", "domain": "spandidos-publications.com", "snippet": "While preclinical studies showed promising results, clinical data using the inhibitors alone and in combination with other standard therapies have shown limited practicality, largely due to the lack of efficacy and dose‑limiting toxicities.\n...\nMK-2206, a novel AKT inhibitor, inhibited in vitro proliferation and induced apoptosis of PDAC cells.\n...\nIn KRAS-mutant patient-derived xenograft (PDX) models, a combination of MK-2206 with dinaciclib, an inhibitor of cyclin-dependent kinases, demonstrated a reduction in PDAC growth (by >90%; P<0.001) and the number of metastatic lesions (by >88%; P<0.001) (33).\n...\nIn preclinical models (in vitro tumor cell lines and in vivo tumor cell-derived xenografts) of KRASG12C-mutant cancers including PDAC, sotorasib exhibited antitumor activity and significantly synergized with the proapoptotic agent DT2216 (122).\nIn a phase I/II trial in patients with KRASG12C-mutant PDAC who had received at least one previous systemic therapy, sotorasib demonstrated an acceptable safety profile and promising antitumor activity (median PFS, 4 months; median OS, 6.9 months) (123).\n...\nIn a preclinical study, RMC-6236, an inhibitor of multiple RAS mutations and wild-type RAS, demonstrated profound and durable tumor regression in multiple CDX and PDX models of KRAS-mutant PDAC (131).", "date": "2023-11-01", "last_updated": "2025-02-05"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Targeting KRAS in pancreatic cancer - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11055996/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "First data demonstrated significant activity in PDAC, with 6/8 patients exhibiting stable disease and three showing a 30% reduction at 4.3 months median follow-up [33].\n...\nMRTX1133 exhibits single-digit nanomolar activity in cellular tests, suppresses KRAS G12D signaling and it showed high antitumor activity in murine animal models [40].\nIn detail, this inhibitor showed tumor regression >30% in 73% (8/11) of KRAS G12D-mutated PDAC cell lines and patient-derived xenografts (PDX) [40].\n...\nTH-Z835 has been demonstrated to suppress MAPK signaling and cell proliferation as well as growth of xenografts in models of pancreatic cancer and additionally shows synergy with an anti-PD-1 checkpoint antibody.\n...\nTo date, 65 patients with PDAC have been enrolled in the trial’s dose-escalation phase of RMC-6236, with tumors harboring variants other than KRAS G12C.\nG12D in never smokers made up 35% of this group.\nIn the PDAC cohort, the Overall Response Rate (ORR) was 20% and the Disease Control Rate (DCR) was 87% among 46 evaluable patients.\n...\nFirst data from a dose escalation trial employing BI-1701963 as monotherapy showed good tolerability and stable disease up to 18 weeks in 7/31 patients with KRAS mutated solid tumors [65].", "date": "2024-04-23", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Kras G12d", "url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.2147/DDDT.S559325", "domain": "tandfonline.com", "snippet": "In the Phase 1/2 CodeBreaK 100 study, which primarily enrolled heavily pretreated patients, sotorasib demonstrated a median PFS of 4.0 months and median OS of 6.9 months in *KRAS* G12C–mutant PDAC, with an objective response rate (ORR) of approximately 21%.\n...\nPreliminary results presented at ASCO 2025 from the G12D–mutant PDAC cohort (40 evaluable patients) demonstrated encouraging antitumor activity, with an ORR of approximately 30% and disease control achieved in 80% of patients.^Citation36^ Pharmacodynamic analyses showed on-target activity, including reductions in *KRAS* G12D circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) in responding patients.\n...\nProof-of-concept for *KRAS*-targeted cellular therapy was demonstrated by Leidner et al, who reported regression of metastatic PDAC following adoptive transfer of autologous T cells engineered to recognize *KRAS* G12D presented by *HLA-C* *08:02.\n...\nDaraxonrasib (RMC-6236) is a multi-selective RAS(ON) inhibitor that targets active RAS independent of the specific mutant allele.\nUpdated phase 1 data in previously treated metastatic KRAS G12X-mutated PDAC show encouraging clinical activity, with ORRs of 36% at 300 mg versus 27% in patients with broader RAS mutations, and 29% versus 25% across the 160–300 mg dose range.\n^Citation44^ Disease control was seen in 91–95% across groups and doses.\nMedian PFS was 8.5 months and median OS 14.5 months, exceeding historical benchmarks for second-line chemotherapy.\n...\nIn orthotopic *KRAS* G12D pancreatic tumors, AAV8-delivered CasRx guided by mutation-specific sgRNAs effectively suppressed mutant *KRAS* expression, abrogated downstream signaling, and significantly prolonged survival without detectable toxicity.^Citation67^ These effects extended to patient-derived xenografts, where CasRx treatment inhibited tumor growth and enhanced sensitivity to gemcitabine ().\n...\nWhile allele-specific inhibitors targeting G12C, G12D, and G12V, as well as pan-RAS(ON) agents, have shown promising activity in preclinical models and early-phase trials, clinical responses remain limited and variable.", "date": "2026-03-27", "last_updated": "2026-07-06"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "KRAS Inhibition in Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12842425/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "This drug has been further investigated in clinical trials across xenograft models of KRAS G12D mutant cancer.\nThis drug has also been investigated in 9 patients with PDAC.\nThis study showed that oral administration of Zoldonrasib at 100 mg/kg was well tolerated and that tumor regression was observed when it was combined with a MAPK pathway inhibitor.\n...\nRMC 6236 in phase 1 clinical trial elicited an objective response rate (ORR) of 20% and a disease control rate of 87% in PDAC.", "date": "2026-01-21", "last_updated": "2026-09-27"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Frontiers | Evaluation of KRAS inhibitor-directed therapies for pancreatic cancer treatment", "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/oncology/articles/10.3389/fonc.2024.1402128/full", "domain": "frontiersin.org", "snippet": "Several studies have shown that in mouse models, ablation of mutant KRAS in established tumors promotes PDAC regression (27, 28, 45), emphasizing the promising therapeutic potential for KRAS-directed inhibitors.\n...\nA confirmed partial response was seen in 21% of the patients, with an 84% disease control rate (DCR).\nThe median progression-free survival and overall survival were 4.0 and 6.9 months, respectively.\nIn the KRYSTAL-1 trial, adagrasib was evaluated in 21 patients with unresectable or metastatic PDAC harboring KRAS^G12C^ mutations (102).\nA partial response was seen in 33% of the patients, with a 100% DCR, and the median progression-free survival and overall survival were 5.4 and 8.0 months, respectively.", "date": "2024-05-10", "last_updated": "2026-05-19"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Direct GDP-KRASG12C inhibitors and mechanisms of resistance: the tip of the iceberg - Joshua C. Rosen, Adrian Sacher, Ming-Sound Tsao, 2023", "url": "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/17588359231160141", "domain": "journals.sagepub.com", "snippet": "*In vivo*, sotorasib led to maximal inhibition of p-ERK 2–4 h after treatment which was sustained for 48 h, and cell-derived xenografts regressed over 3 weeks of treatment.\n...\n#### D-1553\n76Activity was observed in NSCLC and PDAC cell-derived xenografts, and combinations with a MEKi, SHP2i, or other cytotoxic agents led to tumor regression.", "date": "2023-03-10", "last_updated": "2025-04-10"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "KRAS inhibitors: resistance drivers and combinatorial strategies", "url": "https://www.cell.com/trends/cancer/fulltext/S2405-8033(24)00275-9", "domain": "cell.com", "snippet": "Similar to RMC-7977, preclinical models treated with RMC-6236 tolerated all doses tested and induced strong tumor regression in both PDAC and NSCLC models.", "date": "2025-02-01", "last_updated": "2025-08-12"}], "raw": {"id": "b1b163c3-b49b-4948-b84c-211ad1308036"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:15+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q050", "objective": "ONC201/dordaviprone in vivo monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["ONC201/dordaviprone", "ONC201", "dordaviprone"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q050-batch", "query": ["ONC201/dordaviprone PDAC monotherapy xenograft tumour growth inhibition"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["ONC201/dordaviprone PDAC monotherapy xenograft tumour growth inhibition"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 4111.6, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Oncoceutics, Inc. - 新药情报- 智慧芽", "url": "https://synapse.zhihuiya.com/organization/a5bf4297675f2f507f28f8f8d99e6f95", "domain": "synapse.zhihuiya.com", "snippet": "ONC201 monotherapy was well tolerated and exhibited durable and clinically meaningful efficacy in recurrent H3 K27M–mutant DMG.\n...\nCombination of ONC201 and TLY012 induces selective, synergistic apoptosis in vitro and significantly delays PDAC xenograft growth in vivo\n...\n*In vivo* experiments using BxPC3 and HPAFII xenograft models showed that the combination of ONC201 plus TLY012 significantly delays tumor growth as compared to controls.\n...\nONC201 inhibited tumor growth at a well-tolerated dose of 120 mg/kg/week administered by oral gavage and decreased MTC xenograft cell proliferation and angiogenesis.\nThe protein levels of RET, IGFBP2, and VEGFR2 were decreased in ONC201-treated xenografts.\n...\n去年8月，FDA加速批准了一款口服靶向药dordaviprone（商品名Modeyso，研发代号ONC201）。\n这是全球首个专门针对H3 K27M突变弥漫性中线胶质瘤的全身性疗法。\n...\nFDA的加速批准基于50名复发患者的临床数据——客观缓解率达到22%，中位缓解持续时间10.3个月。\n在11名有响应的患者中，超过七成疗效维持了半年以上，近三成维持超过一年。\n...\n2025年8月，FDA批准上市，商品名：Modeyso，用于治疗年龄≥1岁前线治疗进展的携带H3 K27M突变的弥漫性中线胶质瘤（DMG）患者。", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-13"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Preclinical efficacy and mechanism of action of dordaviprone in H3 K27M-mutant diffuse glioma", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/neuro-onc-peds/article/2/3/wuag033/8750125?searchresult=1", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Single-agent dordaviprone inhibited tumor growth and/or improved survival in vivo in multiple H3 K27M-mutant diffuse glioma models, including intracranial, patient-derived xenografts, immune-competent, and genetically engineered mouse models.\n...\n^1^^,^^2^ In vitro experiments in cell lines and patient-derived cells have demonstrated the monotherapy efficacy of dordaviprone across hematological and solid tumors, including in tumor cells that are resistant to standard-of-care treatments.^2-6^ Dordaviprone has demonstrated tumor growth inhibition, tumor regressions, improved survival, and anti-metastatic efficacy as a single agent across a range of in vivo mouse models.\n^4^^,^^7-9^ These models include subcutaneous or orthotopic xenografts with cell lines and patient-derived tumor cells, as well as immune-competent syngeneic and genetically engineered models.^2^^,^^9^ In vitro and in vivo studies have also demonstrated synergistic or additive efficacy of dordaviprone in combination with chemotherapy, radiation, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy across a wide range of cancers.^10^", "date": "2026-07-08", "last_updated": "2026-08-07"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Preclinical efficacy and mechanism of action of dordaviprone in H3 ...", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/neuro-onc-peds/article/2/3/wuag033/8750125", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Single-agent dordaviprone inhibited tumor growth and/or improved survival in vivo in multiple H3 K27M-mutant diffuse glioma models, including intracranial, patient-derived xenografts, immune-competent, and genetically engineered mouse models.\n...\n^1^^,^^2^ In vitro experiments in cell lines and patient-derived cells have demonstrated the monotherapy efficacy of dordaviprone across hematological and solid tumors, including in tumor cells that are resistant to standard-of-care treatments.^2-6^ Dordaviprone has demonstrated tumor growth inhibition, tumor regressions, improved survival, and anti-metastatic efficacy as a single agent across a range of in vivo mouse models.\n^4^^,^^7-9^ These models include subcutaneous or orthotopic xenografts with cell lines and patient-derived tumor cells, as well as immune-competent syngeneic and genetically engineered models.^2^^,^^9^ In vitro and in vivo studies have also demonstrated synergistic or additive efficacy of dordaviprone in combination with chemotherapy, radiation, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy across a wide range of cancers.^10^\n...\nSingle-agent dordaviprone has shown inhibition of tumor growth and improved survival in multiple in vivo preclinical mouse models, including intracranial, PDXs, immune-competent, and genetic mouse models (Figure 3, Table S1).\n...\nSingle-agent dordaviprone has consistently shown inhibition of tumor growth and/or improved survival in several in vivo preclinical mouse models of H3 K27M-mutant diffuse glioma, including intracranial, patient-derived xenografts, immune-competent, and genetic mouse models.", "date": "2026-07-08", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Dordaviprone/ONC201 Activation of the ClpP Mitochondrial ... - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12822620/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "**It has not yet been peer reviewed by a journal.**\n...\n# Dordaviprone/ONC201 Activation of the ClpP Mitochondrial Protease Inhibits the Growth of KRAS-Mutant Pancreatic Cancer and Overcomes RAS Inhibitor Resistance\n...\nTo identify potential combination strategies, we first assessed the ability of dordaviprone/ONC201, an FDA-approved agent, to inhibit PDAC cell and organoid growth.\nWe observed that ONC201 reduced the growth of a broad panel of KRAS-mutant PDAC cell lines, and that the expression of mitochondrial protease ClpP was required for this efficacy.\nMechanistically, we observed that treatment with ONC201 led to inhibition of mitochondrial respiration, causing a compensatory increase in glycolysis.\nFurthermore, ONC201 caused ClpP-dependent activation of PI3K-AKT-mTOR signaling and concurrent PI3K and mTOR inhibition further enhanced ONC201 growth suppression.\nONC201 demonstrated an additive effect when combined with a RAS(ON) multi-selective inhibitor RMC-7977 in PDAC cells and organoids.\nFinally, PDAC cell lines with acquired resistance to RMC-7977 or\n*KEAP1* loss-driven resistance retained sensitivity to ONC201.\nWe propose that concurrent treatment with ONC201 may delay onset of resistance to RAS inhibitor therapy.\n...\nClpP activation by dordaviprone/ONC201 suppressed PDAC cell growth and overcame resistance to the RAS(ON) multi-selective inhibitor RMC-7977, providing support for investigating this combination as a potential combination treatment for KRAS-mutant pancreatic cancer.", "date": "2025-12-03", "last_updated": "2026-02-16"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "dordaviprone (ONC201)", "url": "https://drughunter.com/molecule/dordaviprone-onc201", "domain": "drughunter.com", "snippet": "A first-in-class, oral, brain-penetrant imipridone, dordaviprone combines mitochondrial ClpP hyperactivation with D2R antagonism, dual mechanisms implicated in anti-tumor activity across glioma models.", "date": "2025-10-10", "last_updated": "2026-09-26"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "ONC-201 Dihydrochloride", "url": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/ONC-201-Dihydrochloride", "domain": "pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Dordaviprone Hydrochloride is the hydrochloride salt form of dordaviprone, a water soluble, orally bioavailable inhibitor of the serine/threonine protein kinase Akt (protein kinase B) and extracellular signal-regulated kinase (ERK), with potential antineoplastic activity.\nUpon administration, dordaviprone binds to and inhibits the activity of Akt and ERK, which may result in inhibition of the phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase (PI3K)/Akt signal transduction pathway as well as the mitogen-activated protein kinase (MAPK)/ERK-mediated pathway.\nThis may lead to the induction of tumor cell apoptosis mediated by tumor necrosis factor (TNF)-related apoptosis-inducing ligand (TRAIL)/TRAIL death receptor type 5 (DR5) signaling in AKT/ERK-overexpressing tumor cells.\nThe PI3K/Akt signaling pathway and MAPK/ERK pathway are upregulated in a variety of tumor cell types and play a key role in tumor cell proliferation, differentiation and survival by inhibiting apoptosis.\nIn addition, dordaviprone is able to cross the blood-brain barrier.", "date": "2025-06-21", "last_updated": "2025-06-25"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "ONC201 (Dordaviprone) in Recurrent H3 K27M-Mutant Diffuse ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38335473/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "** Histone 3 (H3) K27M-mutant diffuse midline glioma (DMG) has a dismal prognosis with no established effective therapy beyond radiation. This integrated analysis evaluated single-agent ONC201 (dordaviprone), a first-in-class imipridone, in recurrent H3 K27M-mutant DMG.\n**\n...\n** Fifty patients (pediatric, n = 4; adult, n = 46) with recurrent H3 K27M-mutant DMG who received oral ONC201 monotherapy in four clinical trials or one expanded access protocol were included. Eligible patients had measurable disease by Response Assessment in Neuro-Oncology (RANO) high-grade glioma (HGG) criteria and performance score (PS) ≥60 and were ≥90 days from radiation; pontine and spinal tumors were ineligible.\nThe primary end point was overall response rate (ORR) by RANO-HGG criteria. Secondary end points included duration of response (DOR), time to response (TTR), corticosteroid response, PS response, and ORR by RANO low-grade glioma (LGG) criteria. Radiographic end points were assessed by dual-reader, blinded independent central review.\n...\n** The ORR (RANO-HGG) was 20.0% (95% CI, 10.0 to 33.7). The median TTR was 8.3 months (range, 1.9-15.9); the median DOR was 11.2 months (95% CI, 3.8 to not reached).\nThe ORR by combined RANO-HGG/LGG criteria was 30.0% (95% CI, 17.9 to 44.6). A ≥50% corticosteroid dose reduction occurred in 7 of 15 evaluable patients (46.7% [95% CI, 21.3 to 73.4]); PS improvement occurred in 6 of 34 evaluable patients (20.6% [95% CI, 8.7 to 37.9]).\nGrade 3 treatment-related treatment-emergent adverse events (TR-TEAEs) occurred in 20.0% of patients; the most common was fatigue (n = 5; 10%); no grade 4 TR-TEAEs, deaths, or discontinuations occurred.\n**\nConclusion:\n** ONC201 monotherapy was well tolerated and exhibited durable and clinically meaningful efficacy in recurrent H3 K27M-mutant DMG.", "date": "2024-01-05", "last_updated": "2026-09-19"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "A review of current therapeutics targeting the mitochondrial protease ClpP in diffuse midline glioma, H3 K27-altered", "url": "https://academic.oup.com/neuro-oncology/article/26/Supplement_2/S136/7243676", "domain": "academic.oup.com", "snippet": "Key Points\n...\n- In DMG, dordaviprone is an agonist of the mitochondrial protease ClpP.\n...\nFor the most part, DMG clinical trials examined the dordaviprone safety and efficacy profile without the establishment of robust preclinical data for its use, including no in vivo DMG orthotopic xenograft studies, or understanding of its cellular target.\n...\nIndeed, the initial MOA reported by Allen et al., in colon cancer suggested dordaviprone promotes TRAIL-mediated apoptosis independent of p53, leading to inhibition MAPK/ERK and PI3K/Akt signaling.^26^\n...\nHistone deacetylases (HDACs), enzymatically remove acetyl groups, regulating gene expression across many different cancer types.\nNguyen et al., showed HDAC inhibition with either panobinostat and romidepsin-induced synthetic lethality when combined with imipridones which suppressed tumor cell metabolism in glioblastoma cells.\n...\nEmploying a proteogenomic profiling approach, exposure to dordaviprone in DMG cells increased mitochondrial oxidative stress that was seen to promote redox activation of PI3K/Akt signaling, resulting in metabolic adaption and cell survival.\n...\nDordaviprone’s potential role in the promotion of immune cell infiltration into the TME suggests it might be effectively combined with ICIs.\nImportantly, dordaviprone increases levels of both NK cells and T cells, both expressing PD-1, making the blockade of PD-1 an attractive approach.\nIndeed, combining 100 mg/kg dordaviprone with PD-1 inhibitors decreased tumor volume of mice orthotopically engrafted with the undifferentiated colon carcinoma cell line CT26, compared with PD-1 inhibitors alone.^74^ However, the effect was not seen with lower doses of dordaviprone or in mice with chemically induced murine colon adenocarcinoma cells MC38 pressing the need for DMG studies investigating the efficacy of combining dordaviprone with ICIs.", "date": "2024-05-03", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Dordaviprone | C24H26N4O | CID 73777259 - PubChem", "url": "https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/compound/73777259", "domain": "pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "It is a selective antagonist of the dopamine receptor D~2~ and an allosteric agonist of mitochondrial protease caseinolytic protease P.\nIt has a role as a dopamine receptor D2 antagonist, an antineoplastic agent and an apoptosis inducer.\n...\nIt is used to treat patients with diffuse midline glioma harboring an H3 K27M mutation.\n...\nDordaviprone has several modes of action, including the activation of mitochondrial caseinolytic protease P (ClpP), antagonism of the dopamine D2 receptor, activation of Activating Transcription Factor 4 (ATF4), and induction of endoplasmic reticulum (ER) stress response.\nDordaviprone is a Protease Activator.\n...\nDordaviprone is indicated for the treatment of adult and pediatric patients 1 year of age and older with diffuse midline glioma harboring an H3 K27M mutation with progressive disease following prior therapy.\n...\nDordaviprone is a drug with anti-tumour activity: It exhibited anti-tumour activity in cell-based assays and in vivo models of H3 K27M-mutant diffuse glioma.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-04"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "ONC201 (Dordaviprone): review of evidence to date in diffuse midline glioma, hope or hype?", "url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/14728214.2024.2426649", "domain": "tandfonline.com", "snippet": "The outcome of 71 unique patients (30 from ONC201–014 [Citation22]; and 41 patients from ONC201–018) were described and, from this analysis, the authors concluded that treatment with ONC201 results in a significant improvement in the PFS and OS of patients with either non-recurrent or recurrent DMG.\n...\nTheir conclusion was that ONC201 was the first monotherapy to improve the outcome in H3K27M mutant DMG beyond radiation.\n...\nThe data, as it is presented and published to date, does not meet the metric to justify the conclusion that ‘ONC201 is the first monotherapy to improve outcomes in H3K27M mutant DMG beyond radiation.’", "date": "2024-10-01", "last_updated": "2025-08-06"}], "raw": {"id": "8ba9aed6-9bca-4e0c-a5ff-33d8aa501ac7"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:18+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q053", "objective": "ADT-1004 in vivo single-agent monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression or shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["ADT-1004"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q053-batch", "query": ["ADT-1004 quantitative tumour growth inhibition percentage PDAC monotherapy", "ADT-1004 tumour regression percentage MIA PaCa-2 xenograft", "ADT-1004 KRAS G12D PDX tumour shrinkage monotherapy", "ADT-1004 KPC orthotopic tumour volume change day 27", "ADT-1004 MIA-AMG-R xenograft final tumour volume monotherapy"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["ADT-1004 quantitative tumour growth inhibition percentage PDAC monotherapy", "ADT-1004 tumour regression percentage MIA PaCa-2 xenograft", "ADT-1004 KRAS G12D PDX tumour shrinkage monotherapy", "ADT-1004 KPC orthotopic tumour volume change day 27", "ADT-1004 MIA-AMG-R xenograft final tumour volume monotherapy"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 1077.7, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "ADT-1004: a first-in-class, oral pan-RAS inhibitor with robust ...", "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12943-025-02288-9?error=cookies_not_supported&code=d921b7d0-2285-448c-af48-bb748e5ce2b0", "domain": "link.springer.com", "snippet": "ADT-1004 potently blocked tumor growth and RAS activation in mouse PDAC models without discernable toxicity with target engagement and reduced activated RAS and ERK phosphorylation.\nIn addition, ADT-1004 suppressed tumor growth in PDX PDAC models with KRAS^G12D^, KRAS^G12V^, KRAS^G12C^, or KRAS^G13Q^ mutations and increased CD4^+^ and CD8^+^ T cells in the TME consistent with exhaustion and increased MHCII^+^ M1 macrophage and dendritic cells.\nADT-1004 demonstrated superior efficacy over sotorasib and adagrasib in tumor models resistant to these KRAS^G12C^ inhibitors and MRTX1133 resistant KRAS^G12D^ mutant cells.\n...\nADT-1004 has strong antitumor activity in aggressive and clinically relevant PDAC models with unique selectivity to block RAS-mediated signaling in RAS mutant cells.\n...\nThese findings support clinical trials of ADT-1004 for KRAS mutant PDAC.\n...\nMost mice in the ADT-1004 treatment groups displayed reduced bioluminescence compared with mice treated with vehicle.\nDoses of ADT-1004 from 20 to 50 mg/kg demonstrated comparable statistically significant reductions in tumor weights compared to vehicle-treated mice or mice treated with 10 mg/kg ADT-1004 (Fig. 1G).\n...\nGiven previous reports that ADT-007 inhibits the growth of KRAS mutant cancer cells regardless of the KRAS mutation, we determined the antitumor activity of ADT-1004 across a panel of PDAC PDX models harboring three different KRAS mutations (KRAS^G12D^, KRAS^G12C^, and KRAS^G13Q^) and one pancreaticobiliary PDX model (KRAS^G12V^).\n...\nADT-1004 treatment significantly inhibited the growth of PDAC tumors with KRAS^G12D^ (Fig. 5A and C, Supplementary Fig. 5A), KRAS^G12C^ (Fig. 5E and G, Supplementary Fig. 5B), KRAS^G12V^ (Fig.\n5I and K, Supplementary Fig. 5C) and KRAS^G13Q^ (Fig. 5M and O, Supplementary Fig. 5D) with no change in body weight over the course of treatment, further demonstrating tolerability at dosages that inhibit tumor growth (Fig. 5B, F and J, and 5N, respectively).\n...\nThese results lead us to conclude that ADT-1004 selectively inhibits RAS-dependent tumor growth in mouse tumor PDAC models and are consistent with previous reports characterizing the in vitro growth inhibitory activity of ADT-1004 [14].\n...\nIn conclusion, this study establishes ADT-1004 as a 1st -in-class orally bioavailable pan-RAS inhibitor specific for the apoenzyme state of mutant RAS.\nADT-1004 exhibits promising antitumor activity against RAS-mutant PDAC, including resistant phenotypes.", "date": "2025-03-13", "last_updated": "2026-09-09"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "ADT-1004: a first-in-class, oral pan-RAS inhibitor with ... - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40082968/?fc=20250217093552&ff=20250316024649&v=2.18.0.post9+e462414", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "We developed a novel pan-RAS inhibitor, ADT-1004 (an oral prodrug of ADT-007) and evaluated antitumor activity in murine and human PDAC models.\n...\nTo assess potential to overcome RAS inhibitor resistance, parental and resistant MIA PaCa-2 PDAC cells (KRAS^G12C^ mutation) were implanted subcutaneously.\n...\n** ADT-1004 potently blocked tumor growth and RAS activation in mouse PDAC models without discernable toxicity with target engagement and reduced activated RAS and ERK phosphorylation.\nIn addition, ADT-1004 suppressed tumor growth in PDX PDAC models with KRAS^G12D^, KRAS^G12V^, KRAS^G12C^, or KRAS^G13Q^ mutations and increased CD4^+^ and CD8^+^ T cells in the TME consistent with exhaustion and increased MHCII^+^ M1 macrophage and dendritic cells.\nADT-1004 demonstrated superior efficacy over sotorasib and adagrasib in tumor models resistant to these KRAS^G12C^ inhibitors and MRTX1133 resistant KRAS^G12D^ mutant cells.\nAs evidence of selectivity for tumors with mutant KRAS, ADT-1004 did not impact the growth of tumors from RAS^WT^ PDAC cells.\n...\n** ADT-1004 has strong antitumor activity in aggressive and clinically relevant PDAC models with unique selectivity to block RAS-mediated signaling in RAS mutant cells.\nAs a pan-RAS inhibitor, ADT-1004 has broad activity and potential efficacy advantages over allele-specific KRAS inhibitors.\nThese findings support clinical trials of ADT-1004 for KRAS mutant PDAC.", "date": "2025-03-13", "last_updated": "2026-05-18"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Kras G12d", "url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.2147/DDDT.S559325", "domain": "tandfonline.com", "snippet": "Early clinical trials of targeted therapies have shown encouraging results, including tumor shrinkage and disease control in some patients.\n...\nPreliminary results presented at ASCO 2025 from the G12D–mutant PDAC cohort (40 evaluable patients) demonstrated encouraging antitumor activity, with an ORR of approximately 30% and disease control achieved in 80% of patients.^Citation36^ Pharmacodynamic analyses showed on-target activity, including reductions in *KRAS* G12D circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) in responding patients.\n...\nEarly dose-escalation cohorts have demonstrated preliminary antitumor activity, and preclinical studies suggest synergistic effects when combined with proteasome inhibition with carfilzomib.^Citation37^ TSN1611 targets both GTP-bound (active) and GDP-bound (inactive) G12D and has demonstrated good tolerability with preliminary tumor shrinkage in refractory G12D–mutant tumors, including PDAC, non-small cell lung cancer, and colorectal cancer, in early phase I/II studies (NCT06385925).^Citation38^\n...\nMultiple other pan-RAS candidates are advancing in parallel.\nADT-007 and its orally bioavailable prodrug ADT-1004 is a pan-RAS inhibitor currently under clinical investigation in PDAC and other RAS-driven cancers, with superior efficacy over sotorasib and adagrasib in tumor resistant models.", "date": "2026-03-27", "last_updated": "2026-07-06"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Adt-1004: A First-in-Class, Orally Bioavailable Selective ...", "url": "https://augusta.elsevierpure.com/en/publications/adt-1004-a-first-in-class-orally-bioavailable-selective-pan-ras-i/fingerprints/", "domain": "augusta.elsevierpure.com", "snippet": "### Keyphrases\n- Tumor 100%\n...\n- Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma 100%\n...\n- PDAC Cells 75%\n- Tumor Growth 50%\n- Antitumor Activity 50%\n...\n- Pancreas Adenocarcinoma 100%\n- Tumor Growth 66%", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "ADT-007 / ADT Pharma, ChemomAb", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=6ac1f0ab-2853-4944-b164-e472fe11819b", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "Consistent with resistance experiments, ADT-1004 displayed superior efficacy than sotorasib or adagrasib in a xenograft model using a resistant MIA-PaCa-2.\nThese findings support further development of ADT-1004 that holds promise for broad and durable efficacy against RAS-driven cancers.\"\n...\nThese results highlight the promise of ADT-1004 as a potential breakthrough for treating PDAC.\nThis novel pan-RAS inhibitor demonstrates potent inhibition of PDAC growth, irrespective of the specific KRAS mutation.\nFurthermore, it shows an excellent safety profile, with profound tumor regression and no impact on body weight in animal models.\"\n...\nAn orally bioavailable prodrug of ADT-007 (ADT-1004) is well tolerated in vivo, and produced sustained concentrations of ADT-007 well above IC50 values in plasma and even higher levels in tumors.\nDaily oral administration of ADT-1004 significantly inhibited RAS signaling and tumor growth in orthotopic and PDX mouse models of pancreatic cancer.\"\n...\n(PubMed, Cancers (Basel))\n...\n- \"ADT-1004 has strong antitumor activity in aggressive and clinically relevant PDAC models with unique selectivity to block RAS-mediated signaling in RAS mutant cells.\n...\nIn vitro growth inhibition by ADT-007 was very potent in mutant KRAS cells (IC50 = 2 nM KRASG12C MIA PaCa-2 PDAC), but also highly selective (RASWT BxPC3 PDAC cells IC50 = 2500 nM).\nGrowth of KRAS-mutant PDAC tumors was suppressed by ADT-1004 and was accompanied by inhibition of signaling downstream of RAS (MAPK/AKT).\n...\n(AACR 2023)\n- \"ADT-1004 represents a 1st-in-class pan-RAS inhibitor with potential advantages over mutant-specific KRAS inhibitors for greater efficacy and ability to avert mechanisms of resistance.\nThese data support future clinical trials of ADT-1004 as a monotherapy for the treatment of patients with PDA regardless of the underlying mutation.\"", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-18"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Key Considerations for Targeting KRAS in Pancreatic Cancer - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13037533/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "|ADT-1004/007|Pan-RAS inhibitor|Preclinical|Significant tumor suppression in KRAS G12D orthotopic and PDX models; activity across G12D, G12V, G12C, G12Q; increases CD4+/CD8+ infiltration; superior to G12C inhibitors in resistant models|\n...\nADT-007 and its orally bioavailable prodrug ADT-1004 is a pan-RAS inhibitor currently under clinical investigation in PDAC and other RAS-driven cancers, with superior efficacy over sotorasib and adagrasib in tumor resistant models.45,46 AMG-410 is a non-covalent,", "date": "2026-03-27", "last_updated": "2026-09-29"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Pan-RAS Inhibitors: Expanding Therapeutic Potential and Evading Resistance", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2072-6694/18/11/1844", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "To overcome first-pass metabolism, an orally bioavailable prodrug of ADT-007, ADT-1004, was developed.\n...\nIn murine models of RAS-driven PDAC, ADT-1004 demonstrated robust and durable antitumor activity at well-tolerated doses.\nOral dosing maintained therapeutic plasma levels of ADT-007 and produced significant tumor growth inhibition, which correlated with reduced activated RAS levels and ERK signaling [62].\nADT-1004 was effective in PDX models of pancreatic cancer harboring KRAS mutations (G12D, G12V, G12C, and G13Q) but did not affect the growth of tumors established from the WT RAS BxPC3 pancreatic tumor cell line, which is driven by a downstream BRAF mutation.\n...\nOral administration of ERAS-4001 in mice achieved dose-dependent plasma exposure, reduced P-ERK levels, and produced antitumor activity, including 77% tumor growth inhibition in KRAS G12D xenograft models and tumor regression in G12V xenograft models [80].\n...\n|pan-RAS|ADT-1004|Preclinical [62]|", "date": "2026-06-04", "last_updated": "2026-08-13"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Abstract 7236: Selective deletion of the KRAS mutant gene with ADGN-123 and ADGN-121 peptide-RNA nanoparticles: A new strategy to overcome acquired resistance to KRASG12C and KRASG12D small molecule inhibitors", "url": "https://bohrium.dp.tech/paper/arxiv/ffee0d817592d670e297e4ab211cf142a28ff61c465334c6c900b42f135d3637", "domain": "bohrium.dp.tech", "snippet": "In a mouse clinical trial with KRASG12D xenograft tumor models, RMC-9805 administered orally as a single agent was well tolerated and induced objective responses in 7 of 9 PDAC PDX and CDX models and 6 of 9 NSCLC PDX models, as assessed by mRECIST.\n...\nOverall, RMC-9805 monotherapy elicited tumor regressions in most preclinical PDAC and NSCLC cancer models harboring KRASG12D.\n...\nResults: ADT-1004, a prodrug of ADT-007 that exhibits potent inhibitory effects on the proliferation of both human and murine RAS mutant PDAC cell lines with single-digit nM IC50 values, compared with μM IC50 in PDAC cells with WT RAS, was well tolerated in mice at a dose up to 175 mg/kg bid orally with sustained plasma levels of ADT-007 that exceeded IC50 values by 50-fold and even higher concentrations in pancreatic tumors.\nWhen administered orally at 40mg/kg body weight, ADT-1004 resulted in substantial growth inhibition of the KRAS-G12D and KRAS-G12V tumors, leading to profound tumor regression without any accompanying loss in body weight.\nADT-1004 also displayed a significant inhibitory effect on ERK phosphorylation.\nNotably, this inhibition is effective regardless of the specific RAS mutational codon or isozyme, as it specifically targets activated RAS and disrupts downstream KRAS signaling pathways.\nConclusion: These results highlight the promise of ADT-1004 as a potential breakthrough for treating PDAC.\nThis novel pan-RAS inhibitor demonstrates potent inhibition of PDAC growth, irrespective of the specific KRAS mutation.", "date": "2024-03-31", "last_updated": "2025-06-04"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Emerging KRAS G12D inhibitor in the treatment of digestive ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13066353/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "In concert, these mechanisms demonstrate that durable responses with MRTX1133 monotherapy are unlikely.\n...\nADT-1004 has been reported as a first-in-class, orally available pan-RAS inhibitor with robust antitumor activity in preclinical models of PDAC, representing an alternative modality that emphasizes broader RAS pathway suppression (91).", "date": "2026-03-20", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "A potent and selective pan-RAS inhibitor, ADT-1004, targeting complex KRAS mutations for pancreatic cancer.", "url": "https://ascopubs.org/doi/10.1200/JCO.2024.42.23_suppl.90", "domain": "ascopubs.org", "snippet": "**Results:**ADT-007 potently and selectively inhibited the growth of human and mouse PDAC cell lines.\nFor example, the IC\n50value pf ADT-007 for KRAS\nG12CMIA PaCA-2 PDAC cells was as low as 2 nM compared to 2500 nM for RAS\nWTBxPC3 PDAC cells.\n...\nWhen administered orally at 40 mg/kg body weight, ADT-1004 displayed robust antitumor activity in mouse tumor models using patient or mouse derived PDAC cell lines with G12D, G12V, G12C, or G13Q KRAS mutations, causing significant inhibitory effects on ERK phosphorylation.\n**Conclusions:**ADT-1004 inhibited tumor growth of KRAS mutant PDA tumors by targeting activated RAS to disrupt downstream MAPK/AKT signaling.", "date": "2024-08-10", "last_updated": "2025-03-03"}], "raw": {"id": "5d9b4f3d-cc5f-4de4-9eca-d2fb2151e9e3"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:19+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q052", "objective": "HM100714 in vivo efficacy in combination with another agent in Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma, including the combination partner", "anchors": ["HM100714"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q052-batch", "query": ["HM100714 HNSCC combination in vivo efficacy", "HM100714 head and neck squamous cell carcinoma xenograft combination", "HM100714 combination partner xenograft", "HM100714 AACR HNSCC combination", "HM100714 cisplatin or pembrolizumab HNSCC"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["HM100714 HNSCC combination in vivo efficacy", "HM100714 head and neck squamous cell carcinoma xenograft combination", "HM100714 combination partner xenograft", "HM100714 AACR HNSCC combination", "HM100714 cisplatin or pembrolizumab HNSCC"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 4093.8, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Hanmi Records Highest Number of AACR Presentations ...", "url": "https://hanmipharm.com/about/investor-relations/press/detail-3962.hm", "domain": "hanmipharm.com", "snippet": "The company will also unveil research on its oral targeted therapy candidate, the selective HER2 inhibitor (HM100714), which is under development to treat HER2-mutant cancers.", "date": "2026-04-06", "last_updated": "2026-08-24"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "“비만 넘어 항암으로 혁신 확장”…한미, AACR서 차세대 신약 대거 공개", "url": "https://www.hanmi.co.kr/about/news-media/press/detail-4042.hm", "domain": "hanmi.co.kr", "snippet": "HM100714는 HER2 변이 암에서 강력한 항암 효능과 함께 낮은 EGFR(표피 성장인자 수용체) 관련 독성을 바탕으로, 엔허투 내성 이종이식 마우스 모델에서 항종양 활성이 확인됐고, 뇌 및 연수막 전이 모델에서도 우수한 효능과 양호한 안전성 프로파일을 나타냈다.", "date": "2026-04-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "“비만 넘어 항암으로 혁신 넓힌다”…한미, AACR서 국내 최다 연구성과 발표 | 한미약품", "url": "https://www.hanmi.co.kr/about/news-media/press/detail-1862.hm", "domain": "hanmi.co.kr", "snippet": "또 한미약품은 HER2 변이암에 대한 경구용 치료제로 개발 중인 ‘선택적 HER2 저해제(HM100714)’의 중추신경계 종양에 대한 항암 효력과 엔허투 내성 극복에 대한 연구 결과를 공개한다.", "date": "2025-04-14", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "“탄탄한 근거 중심 R&D 집중”…한미약품, AACR서 국내 최다 ...", "url": "https://www.hanmi.co.kr/about/news-media/press/detail-3942.hm", "domain": "hanmi.co.kr", "snippet": "또 한미약품은 HER2 변이 암 치료 목표로 개발 중인 경구용 표적항암 신약 ‘선택적 HER2 저해제(HM100714)’와 관련, 인공지능(AI) 기반 최신 분석 기술을 활용해 최적 적응증을 도출한 연구 결과를 공개한다.", "date": "2026-04-06", "last_updated": "2026-07-20"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Immunotherapy and Biomarker Testing in Recurrent and Metastatic Head and Neck Cancers: ASCO Guideline", "url": "https://ascopubs.org/doi/pdfdirect/10.1200/JCO.22.02328", "domain": "ascopubs.org", "snippet": "Recommendation 2.1.\nPembrolizumab monotherapy or pembrolizumab, platinum, and fluorouracil should be offered as first- line treatment for patients with recurrent or metastatic HNSCC with a CPS $ 1 (Type: Evidence based, benefits outweigh harms; Evidence quality: High; Strength of recommendation: Strong).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-03-20"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Real-world use of first-line pembrolizumab + platinum + ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10881782/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "The programmed death-1 (PD-1) immune checkpoint inhibitor pembrolizumab is currently approved in the US for the first-line (1L) treatment of recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (R/M HNSCC), either alone or in combination with platinum and 5-fluorouracil (5-FU).\n...\nThe rwOS and other real-world outcomes observed for this study population further support pembrolizumab + platinum + taxane combination therapy as a potential 1L treatment option for R/M HNSCC.\n...\nIn the registration clinical trial (KEYNOTE-048), pembrolizumab + platinum + 5-FU was associated with longer OS compared with cetuximab + platinum + 5-FU in all individuals (median OS: 13.0 versus 10.7 months), while pembrolizumab monotherapy\nwas associated with longer OS compared with cetuximab + platinum + 5-FU in individuals with a combined positive score (CPS, a measure of programmed death ligand-1 [PD-L1] expression in tumor and infiltrating immune cells) of ≥1 (12.3 versus 10.3 months in the CPS ≥1 group and 14.9 versus 10.7 months for the CPS ≥20 group) (8).\n...\nThe 2024 NCCN ^1^ Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology (NCCN Guidelines^®^) for Head and Neck Cancers list pembrolizumab + platinum (cisplatin or carboplatin) + 5-FU as a category 1 option for 1L treatment of R/M HNSCC, with pembrolizumab monotherapy as a category 1 option for tumors that express PD-L1 with CPS ≥1 (11).\n...\nPembrolizumab in combination with cisplatin or carboplatin plus paclitaxel or docetaxel is currently listed as a category 2A option for 1L treatment of R/M HNSCC in the NCCN Guidelines^®^ (11), and thus is already available in the US.\n...\nThe median (95% CI) rwOS was 14.9 (8.8–23.3) months, with an OS rate of 53.2% at 12 months and 31.4% at 24 months.\n...\nIn conclusion, the rwOS, rwToT, and rwTTNT for 1L pembrolizumab + platinum + taxane observed in this real-world outcome analysis provide support for this regimen as a potential 1L combination therapy option for R/M HNSCC.", "date": "2024-02-08", "last_updated": "2026-01-12"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Hanmi Showcases Next Generation Drug Innovations at AACR", "url": "https://www.hanmipharm.com/about/investor-relations/press/detail-4164.hm", "domain": "hanmipharm.com", "snippet": "HM100714 demonstrated robust antitumor efficacy in HER2-mutant cancers along with low EGFR (epidermal growth factor receptor)-related toxicity, confirming antitumor activity in an Enhertu-resistant xenograft mouse model; it also showed strong efficacy and a favorable safety profile in brain and leptomeningeal metastasis models.", "date": "2026-04-28", "last_updated": "2026-09-22"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "1744602650044.docx", "url": "https://hanmi.co.kr/file/admin/pressRelease/1744602650044.docx", "domain": "hanmi.co.kr", "snippet": "또 한미약품은 HER2 변이암에 대한 경구용 치료제로 개발 중인 ‘선택적 HER2 저해제(HM100714)’의 중추신경계 종양에 대한 항암 효력과 엔허투 내성 극복에 대한 연구 결과를 공개한다.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-04-16"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "ASCO Publishes New Guideline on Immunotherapy, Biomarker Testing in Advanced Head and Neck Cancer", "url": "https://dailynews.ascopubs.org/do/asco-publishes-new-guideline-immunotherapy-biomarker-testing-advanced-head-and-neck", "domain": "dailynews.ascopubs.org", "snippet": "First-line therapy options for patients with a CPS ≥ 1 include pembrolizumab as either monotherapy or in combination with platinum and 5-FU.\nFirst-line options for patients with a CPS < 1 include pembrolizumab, platinum, and 5-FU.\nFor patients with platinum-refractory recurrent or metastatic HNSCC, pembrolizumab or nivolumab may be considered, regardless of CPS status.\nFor patients with recurrent or metastatic nasopharyngeal cancer, recommended first-line options include toripalimab, camrelizumab, and tislelizumab, each with gemcitabine and cisplatin.\nIf those immune checkpoint inhibitors are not available, pembrolizumab or nivolumab may be used with gemcitabine and cisplatin.", "date": "2022-12-16", "last_updated": "2025-04-03"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "FDA Approves Pembrolizumab for First-Line Treatment ...", "url": "https://www.ons.org/publications-research/voice/news-views/06-2019/fda-approves-pembrolizumab-first-line-treatment", "domain": "ons.org", "snippet": "On June 10, 2019, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved pembrolizumab (Keytruda^®^) for the first-line treatment of patients with metastatic or unresectable recurrent head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC).\nPembrolizumab was approved for use in combination with platinum and fluorouracil (FU) for all patients and as a single agent for patients whose tumors express PD‑L1 (Combined Positive Score [CPS] ≥ 1) as determined by an FDA‑approved test.\n...\nApproval was based on KEYNOTE-048 (NCT02358031), a randomized, multicenter, three-arm, open‑label, active‑controlled trial conducted in 882 patients with metastatic HNSCC who had not previously received systemic therapy for metastatic disease or with recurrent disease who were considered incurable by local therapies.\nPatients were randomized (1:1:1) to receive one of the following treatments: pembrolizumab as a single agent; pembrolizumab, carboplatin or cisplatin, and FU; or cetuximab, carboplatin or cisplatin, and FU.\n...\nOverall survival (OS), sequentially tested in the subgroup of patients with CPS ≥ 20 HNSCC, the subgroup of patients with CPS ≥ 1 HNSCC and the overall population, was the major efficacy measure.\nThe trial demonstrated a statistically significant improvement in OS in the overall population for patients randomized to pembrolizumab plus chemotherapy compared with cetuximab plus chemotherapy at a pre-specified interim analysis.\nThe median OS was 13.0 months for the pembrolizumab plus chemotherapy arm and 10.7 months for the cetuximab plus chemotherapy arm (HR 0.77; 95% CI: 0.63, 0.93; p = 0.0067).\nResults were similar in the CPS ≥20 subgroup (HR 0.69; 95% CI: 0.51, 0.94) and CPS ≥ 1 subgroup (HR 0.71; 95% CI: 0.57, 0.88).\nThe trial also demonstrated statistically significant improvements in OS for the subgroups of patients with PD‑L1 CPS ≥ 1 HNSCC and PD-L1 CPS ≥ 20 HNSCC randomized to pembrolizumab as a single agent compared with cetuximab plus chemotherapy.\nIn the CPS ≥ 1 subgroup, the median OS was 12.3 months for the pembrolizumab arm and 10.3 months for the cetuximab plus chemotherapy arm (HR 0.78; 95% CI: 0.64, 0.96; p = 0.0171).\nFor the CPS ≥ 20 subgroup, the median OS was 14.9 months for the pembrolizumab arm and 10.7 months for the cetuximab plus chemotherapy arm (HR 0.61; 95% CI: 0.45, 0.83; p = 0.0015).\nAt the time of the interim analysis, there was no significant difference in OS between the pembrolizumab as a single agent arm and the cetuximab plus chemotherapy arm for the overall population.\nThere were no significant differences in progression-free survival for either pembrolizumab-containing arm compared to the cetuximab plus chemotherapy arm in any population.", "date": "2019-06-11", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}], "raw": {"id": "6423990c-bb08-45ad-931f-e48b974c979a"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:20+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q054", "objective": "DWP216 in vivo animal models and results: cell-line xenograft (CDX), patient-derived xenograft (PDX), syngeneic, orthotopic, or genetically engineered models", "anchors": ["DWP216"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q054-batch", "query": ["DWP216 HNSCC in vivo xenograft monotherapy", "DWP216 NCI-H1373 monotherapy tumour volume", "DWP216 pancreatic cancer PDX syngeneic orthotopic", "DWP216 CAPAN-1 xenograft monotherapy 1200 mm3", "DWP216 genetically engineered mouse model"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["DWP216 HNSCC in vivo xenograft monotherapy", "DWP216 NCI-H1373 monotherapy tumour volume", "DWP216 pancreatic cancer PDX syngeneic orthotopic", "DWP216 CAPAN-1 xenograft monotherapy 1200 mm3", "DWP216 genetically engineered mouse model"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 2251.7, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] Development of DWP216, a TEAD1 selective inhibitor for NF2 ...", "url": "https://www.kddf.org/upload/files/1737437863_554b09394faaeee65d0a.pdf", "domain": "kddf.org", "snippet": "TEAD1 selective inhibitors, on the other hand, lack sufficient potency.\n• ‌\u0007In an in vivo mouse model, DWP216 is superior to a TEAD1 selective \ninhibitor, with tumor inhibition equivalent to a pan-TEAD inhibitor.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-03-13"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Patient Derived Xenograft", "url": "https://tumor.informatics.jax.org/mtbwi/pdxSearch.do", "domain": "tumor.informatics.jax.org", "snippet": "Patient-Derived Xenografts (PDX) are generated through the engraftment and passaging of tissue or cells from a patient's tumor into an immunodeficient or humanized mouse host.\nPDX models are an in vivo platform for cancer biology research and for pre-clinical testing of cancer drug efficacy.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-24"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "pan-TEAD inhib News", "url": "https://sigma.larvol.com/moa.php?MoaId=1032265&tab=newstrac", "domain": "sigma.larvol.com", "snippet": "In conclusion, DWP216 is a promising TEAD1 selective inhibitor for NF2 mutant tumor and may be a proper option for drug-resistance of targeted therapy.", "date": "2024-11-14", "last_updated": "2025-06-15"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Resource Page", "url": "https://www.wagnerlab.net/resource-page", "domain": "wagnerlab.net", "snippet": "This page lists genetically engineered mouse models generated and published by our team and how to obtain them from public repositories (links to repositories not shown on the mobile page).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "{{ngMeta['og:title']}}", "url": "https://bio.tools/pdxnet_portal", "domain": "bio.tools", "snippet": "This is a development version of the registry.\nThere is no guarantee that the data entered here will be preserved in the future.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2024-10-21"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Gene search results", "url": "https://www.mousephenotype.org/data/search", "domain": "mousephenotype.org", "snippet": "Content on this site is licensed under aCreative Commons Attribution 4.0 International license", "date": "2019-05-01", "last_updated": "2025-04-02"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Cloudflare", "url": "https://www.advancesradonc.org/article/S2452-1094(23)00177-X/fulltext", "domain": "advancesradonc.org", "snippet": "Cloudflare is currently unable to resolve your requested domain (www.advancesradonc.org).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "PDC000198 - Proteomic Data Commons - National Cancer Institute", "url": "https://proteomic.datacommons.cancer.gov/pdc/study/PDC000198", "domain": "proteomic.datacommons.cancer.gov", "snippet": "Warning\n...\nThis system is provided for Government-authorized use only.\nUnauthorized or improper use of this system is prohibited and may result in disciplinary action and/or civil and criminal penalties.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-22"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Pancreatic cancer PDX models - Crown Bioscience", "url": "https://www.crownbio.com/model/pdx/pancreatic-cancer", "domain": "crownbio.com", "snippet": "Patient-derived xenograft (PDX) models provide a predictive preclinical model for the evaluation of an agent’s efficacy prior to entering the clinic.\n...\nDerived directly from patient tumors and never adapted to grow *in vitro*, Patient derived xenografts reflect the heterogeneity and diversity of the human patient population.", "date": "2026-08-17", "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "NCI-H1373 [H1373] - CRL-5866", "url": "https://www.atcc.org/500-error-page?aspxerrorpath=/products/crl-5866", "domain": "atcc.org", "snippet": "Please include:\n...\n- the URL you were trying to reach\n...\n+1-703-365-2700", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-21"}], "raw": {"id": "a75947d5-0a47-41a5-b943-17c672c9dc23"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:21+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q055", "objective": "DB-1305/Anti-PD-L1/VEGF Bispecific Antibody patients studied in Recurrent or Disseminated HNSCC (Oral Cavity/Oropharynx/Hypopharynx/Larynx), Incurable by Local Therapies trials: biomarker selection, disease severity, line of therapy, and required prior treatment", "anchors": ["DB-1305"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q055-batch", "query": ["DB-1305 recurrent metastatic HNSCC prior treatment line of therapy", "DB-1305 HNSCC PD-L1 biomarker eligibility", "DB-1305 HNSCC ECOG measurable disease inclusion criteria"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["DB-1305 recurrent metastatic HNSCC prior treatment line of therapy", "DB-1305 HNSCC PD-L1 biomarker eligibility", "DB-1305 HNSCC ECOG measurable disease inclusion criteria"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 1560.5, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "PD-1, PD-L1: Head and Neck Cancer", "url": "https://www.ons.org/clinical-tools/biomarkers/head-and-neck-cancer/pd-1-pd-l1", "domain": "ons.org", "snippet": "PD-L1 is a predictive biomarker.** ** PD-L1 positivity of greater than 1% or CPS of 1 or greater indicates potential response to immune checkpoint inhibitors [2].\nPD-L1 is also a prognostic biomarker.\n...\n#### Pembrolizumab (Keytruda®)\nAnti-PD1 antibody\n- Indicated in combination with platinum and fluorouracil, for first-line treatment of patients with metastatic or unresectable, recurrent HNSCC [4,5].\n- Indicated as a single agent for first-line treatment of patients with metastatic or unresectable, recurrent HNSCC whose tumors express PD-L1 (CPS of 1 or greater) as determined by an FDA-approved test [4,5].\n- Indicated as a single agent, for patients with recurrent or metastatic HNSCC with disease progression on or after platinum-containing chemotherapy [4,5].\n- Indicated for previously treated, PD-L1–positive, recurrent or metastatic NPC [4].\n- Indicated for adults with resectable locally advanced HNSCC whose tumors express PD-L1 (CPS of 1 or greater) as determined by an FDA-approved test [6}.\n...\n- Indicated for adult patients with recurrent or metastatic HNSCC with disease progression on or after platinum-based therapy [4,7].\n- Indicated for previously treated, recurrent or metastatic nonkeratinizing NPC, regardless of PD-L1 status or CPS score [4].\n...\nNCCN guidelines recommend testing for PD-L1 in advanced or metastatic disease or when considering clinical trial options at any stage of treatment [4].\nA tissue sample is needed for analysis.\nTesting technologies may include PCR, NGS, and IHC.\nIHC measures the CPS, which is the number of all PD-L1–positive cells divided by all viable tumor cells multiplied by 100.\nA tissue sample is considered to express PD-L1 if the CPS is at least 1 [8].", "date": "2024-11-25", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Assessing PD-L1 Expression in Head and Neck Squamous ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10739626/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "PD-L1 expression assessed by immunohistochemistry is the single most clinically relevant biomarker predicting response to PD-1-blocking antibodies.\n...\nThe palliative treatment of unresectable locally recurrent or metastatic (R/M) HNSCC has recently been improved with the introduction of the immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICI), pembrolizumab and nivolumab [2–4].\n...\nDespite extensive ongoing efforts to identify novel predictive biomarkers, only PD-L1 expression assessed by immunohistochemistry (IHC) on formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded (FFPE) tissue is used to influence treatment decisions in the palliative treatment setting of HNSCC patients.\n...\nUnfortunately, because these exploratory translational efforts are not often followed up by prospective validation trials, the single best predictive biomarker in HNSCC remains PD-L1 expression as assessed by IHC.\n...\nHowever, only PD-L1 CPS and TPS are clinically relevant in HNSCC.\n...\nTo date in HNSCC, therapies involving anti-PD-1 blockade are U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved only in the palliative setting, i.e., R/M HNSCC: Nivolumab or pembrolizumab monotherapy—compared to monotherapy with standard chemotherapeutic agents—was shown to improve overall survival (OS) in HNSCC patients that suffered early disease recurrence after platinum-containing chemotherapy that was used in the adjuvant setting or as treatment of primary or metastatic disease [3, 4].\nSimilarly, pembrolizumab prolonged OS in the first-line treatment of R/M HNSCC either as monotherapy or in combination with 5-fluorouracil (5-FU) and platinum, compared to the previous standard of care, cetuximab combined with 5-FU/platinum (the “EXTREME” regimen) [2].\n...\nIn all three trials, higher PD-L1 expression correlated with improved OS with anti-PD-1 therapy compared to PD-L1 low or negative tumors, suggesting PD-L1 expression is a predictive biomarker for response to anti-PD-1 antibodies in R/M HNSCC.\n...\nFor example, whereas based on the results of the Keynote-040 trial, pembrolizumab monotherapy for R/M HNSCC progressing during or after platinum-containing chemotherapy is approved by the FDA regardless of PD-L1 status, in Switzerland by Swissmedic and by the European Medicines Agency (EMA) only in case of a PD-L1 TPS of ≥ 50 [19–25].\n...\n|1^st^-line palliative|Pembrolizumab monotherapy|PD-L1 CPS ≥ 1|PD-L1 CPS ≥ 1|Not approved|[2, 19, 20, 22]|\n|1^st^-line palliative|Pembrolizumab + platinum/5-FU|–|PD-L1 CPS ≥ 1|PD-L1 CPS ≥ 1|[2, 19, 20, 22]|\n|Early recurrence after platinum|Pembrolizumab monotherapy|–|PD-L1 TPS ≥ 50|PD-L1 TPS ≥ 50|[3, 19, 21, 22]|\n|Early recurrence after platinum|Nivolumab monotherapy|–|–|–|[4, 23, 24, 25]|\n...\nIn summary, PD-L1 testing in HNSCC is only clinically relevant in the palliative setting with the use of pembrolizumab.\n...\nThus, obtaining a biopsy before the initiation of an ICI is not only important to confirm the clinical suspicion of HNSCC relapse but also to test for PD-L1 CPS of R/M HNSCCs.\n...\nThus, in HNSCC employing the companion diagnostic to pembrolizumab, PD-L1 IHC 22C3 pharmDx (PD-L1 antibody clone 22C3 together with the EnVision FLEX visualization system on Autostainer Link 48) is formally required and currently sufficient as the only other approved therapeutic in HNSCC, the anti-PD-1 antibody nivolumab, does not require PD-L1 testing.\n...\nWhereas, PD-L1 expression testing is not relevant for prescribing nivolumab, PD-L1 companion diagnostic testing guides patient selection for pembrolizumab therapy to identify those cancer patients who may benefit most from immunotherapy.\n...\n- PD-L1 testing should be performed before the initiation of treatment with pembrolizumab.\n- Pembrolizumab therapy has an FDA-approved companion PD-L1 IHC test using the PD-L1 antibody clone 22C3 with the EnVision FLEX visualization system on Autostainer Link 48.\n...\n- In the case of suspected R/M disease, a fresh biopsy should be obtained to (i) confirm recurrent and/or metastatic disease and (ii) to perform PD-L1 IHC on the tumor burden being treated with immunotherapy.\nThis recommendation has to be weighed based on a favorable benefit:risk ratio.\n- FNA specimens can be used for PD-L1 IHC.\nHowever, assessing TPS rather than CPS may be more accurate on FNA samples.\nIf the FNA is PD-L1 negative, obtaining a new tissue biopsy (e.g., core biopsy) may be considered to confirm a true-negative result, given the tumor heterogeneity.\n- Alcohol-based fixation methods should be avoided for FNA specimens.", "date": "2023-11-06", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "DB-1311 in Combination With BNT327 or DB-1305 ...", "url": "https://www.uclahealth.org/clinical-trials/db-1311-combination-with-bnt327-or-db-1305", "domain": "uclahealth.org", "snippet": "- For HNSCC patients: Histologically or cytologically confirmed recurrent (recurrent disease that is not amendable to curative treatment with local/ or systemic therapies)/ (disseminated) HNSCC of the oral cavity, oropharynx, hypopharynx, and larynx that is considered incurable by local therapies.\n...\n- 1.\nPrior treatment with B7H3 targeted therapy.\n- Prior treatment with antibody-drug conjugate with topoisomerase inhibitor.\n...\n- Any autoimmune, connective tissue or inflammatory disorders.\n- Has spinal cord compression or clinically active central nervous system (CNS) metastases.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-28"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "First-in-human Study of DB-1305/BNT325 for... | Clinical Trial", "url": "https://trial.medpath.com/clinical-trial/7a3097cbb8397dc7/nct05438329-db-1305-advanced-metastatic-solid-tumors", "domain": "trial.medpath.com", "snippet": "This study will enroll subjects with advanced/unresectable, recurrent, or metastatic malignant solid tumors.\n...\n### Inclusion Criteria\n- •Male or female adults (defined as ≥ 18 years of age or acceptable age according to local regulations at the time of voluntarily signing of informed consent).\n- •Histologically or cytologically confirmed unresectable advanced/ metastatic solid tumors who have relapsed or progressed on or after standard systemic treatments or for which no standard treatment is available.\n- •At least one measurable lesion as assessed by the investigator according to RECIST version 1.1 criteria.\n- •Has a life expectancy of ≥ 3 months.\n- •Has an Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) performance status (PS) of 0-\n- •Has Left Ventricular Ejection Fraction (LVEF) ≥ 50% by either echocardiography (ECHO) or multiple-gated acquisition (MUGA) within 28 days before enrollment.\n- •Has adequate organ functions within 7 days prior to Day 1 of Cycle\n- •Has adequate treatment washout period prior to Day 1 of Cycle\n- •Is willing to provide pre-existing resected tumor samples or undergo fresh tumor biopsy for the measurement of Trop-2 level and other biomarkers if not contraindicated.", "date": "2026-05-12", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Immunotherapy and Biomarker Testing in Recurrent and Metastatic Head and Neck Cancers: ASCO Guideline", "url": "https://ascopubs.org/doi/pdfdirect/10.1200/JCO.22.02328", "domain": "ascopubs.org", "snippet": "Patients with HNSCC commonly present with locoregionally advanced disease and often receive multimodality treatments, including surgery, radiation therapy, and/or chemotherapy.\nAlthough cisplatin is the most commonly used chemotherapy, the choice of chemotherapy depends on several factors, including prior use of platinum-based chemotherapy and primary tumor site.\nRecurrent or metastatic HNSCC is typically treated with systemic therapy involving chemotherapy and/or immunotherapy.\n...\nRecommendation 2.1.\nPembrolizumab monotherapy or pembrolizumab, platinum, and fluorouracil should be offered as first- line treatment for patients with recurrent or metastatic HNSCC with a CPS $ 1 (Type: Evidence based, benefits outweigh harms; Evidence quality: High; Strength of recommendation: Strong).\nRecommendation 2.2.\nPembrolizumab, platinum, and fluorouracil may be offered as first-line treatment for patients with recurrent or metastatic HNSCC with a CPS , 1 (Type: Evidence based, benefits outweigh harms; Evidence quality: Moderate;\n...\nRecommendation 2.1.\nPembrolizumab monotherapy or pembrolizumab, platinum, and fluorouracil (5-FU) should be offered as first-line treatment for patients with recurrent or metastatic HNSCC with a CPS $ 1 (Type: Evidence based, benefits outweigh harms; Evidence quality: High;", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-03-20"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "UCLA Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma Clinical Trials", "url": "https://ucla.clinicaltrials.researcherprofiles.org/head-and-neck-squamous-cell-carcinoma", "domain": "ucla.clinicaltrials.researcherprofiles.org", "snippet": "HNSCC is a type of cancer that develops in the head and neck regions, including the outer tissue layer of the mouth and throat.\nThis study will focus on participants with HNSCC who are treatment-naive (have not received prior treatment) in the R/M setting.\n...\n- ## DB-1311 in Combination With BNT327 or DB-1305 in Advanced/Metastatic Solid Tumors\nopen to eligible people ages 18 years and up\nA Phase II, Multicenter, Open-Label Trial of DB-1311 in combination with BNT327 or DB-1305 in Participants with Advanced/Metastatic Solid Tumors\n...\n- ## FORTIFI-HN01: A Study of Ficerafusp Alfa (BCA101) or Placebo in Combination With Pembrolizumab in First-Line PD-L1-pos, R or M HNSCC\nopen to eligible people ages 18 years and up\nFicerafusp alfa is directed against two targets, Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor (EGFR) and Transforming Growth Factor beta (TGF-β).\nThis study intends to evaluate the safety and efficacy of ficerafusp alfa in combination with pembrolizumab versus placebo with pembrolizumab in 1L PD-L1-positive, recurrent or metastatic Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma (HNSCC).\n...\nAn open-label, controlled, multi-site, interventional, 2-arm, Phase II/III trial of BNT113 in combination with pembrolizumab vs pembrolizumab monotherapy as first line treatment in patients with unresectable recurrent or metastatic HPV16+ HNSCC expressing programmed cell death ligand-1 (PD-L1) with combined positive score (CPS) ≥1.", "date": "2026-09-18", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "PD-L1: Test Selection Guide", "url": "https://testdirectory.questdiagnostics.com/test/test-guides/TG_PD-L1/pd-l1-test-selection-guide", "domain": "testdirectory.questdiagnostics.com", "snippet": "|HNSCC|Keytruda® (pembrolizumab),6 see test code 36260 in **Table 2** for test information|*PD-L1 testing required (companion diagnostic)*- 1st-line treatment for PD-L1+ (CPS ≥1) metastatic or unresectable recurrent disease *PD-L1 testing indicated but not required*- 1st-line treatment (combo) for metastatic or unresectable recurrent disease - Treatment for metastatic or recurrent disease with progression on or after platinum-based therapy|\n...\n|HNSCC|Opdivo® (nivolumab),8 see test code 93793 in **Table 2** for test information|*PD-L1 testing optional (complementary diagnostic)*- Treatment for recurrent or metastatic disease with progression on or after platinum-based therapy|", "date": "2026-07-12", "last_updated": "2026-07-13"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "PD-L1 Testing in Cancer - MyPathologyReport", "url": "https://www.mypathologyreport.ca/biomarkers/pd-l1-testing-in-cancer/", "domain": "mypathologyreport.ca", "snippet": "PD-L1 testing is mandatory for all patients with advanced or metastatic non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) because the result directly determines first-line treatment.\n...\nPD-L1 testing is standard for all patients with recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (HNSCC).\nThe 22C3 assay with CPS scoring is used, and two cut-offs are clinically meaningful:\n- **CPS ≥ 1.** Pembrolizumab combined with platinum-based chemotherapy is approved for first-line treatment of recurrent/metastatic HNSCC with CPS ≥ 1, based on the KEYNOTE-048 trial.\n- **CPS ≥ 20.** Pembrolizumab monotherapy (without chemotherapy) is approved as first-line treatment for CPS ≥ 20, based on demonstrated non-inferiority to chemotherapy with a more favorable tolerability profile.\nThis higher threshold identifies patients most likely to achieve durable responses from immunotherapy alone.\n...\nIn most cancer types with established PD-L1 thresholds, particularly NSCLC with TPS ≥ 50%, HNSCC with CPS ≥ 20, and cervical cancer with CPS ≥ 1, a high result supports the use of checkpoint inhibitor monotherapy or combination therapy.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-09-23"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "Immune biomarkers for head and neck cancer - PMC", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12715102/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "These therapies have shown better outcomes in terms of overall survival (OS) and progression-free survival (PFS) relative to standard chemotherapy, with the greatest benefits observed in HNSCC cases with elevated levels of PD-L1 [7, 12].\n...\nPD-L1 level, TMB, and microsatellites are the only approved biomarkers for ICI responses [41].\nIn 2016, FDA approval was granted to two PD-1 inhibitors in patients with relapsed or metastatic HNSCC.\nThese two monoclonal antibodies were pembrolizumab and nivolumab.\n...\nPD-L1 expression levels could be used as biomarkers for anti-PD-L1 drugs.\nTherefore, PD-L1 is a static predictive biomarker for relapsed or metastatic HNSCC exhibiting a combined positive score (CPS) ≥ 1 [41].\nCompared with standard-of-care chemotherapy, treatment of HNSCC with pembrolizumab or nivolumab resulted in better OS.\n...\nIn KEYNOTE-012, a phase 1b clinical trial of 188 HNSCC patients, a CPS of 1 or higher was considered a significant predictor of HNSCC’s response to pembrolizumab.\nCPS-positive patients accounted for 81% of the cohort and had better ORRs, PFS, and OS than CPS-negative patients.\n...\nAccordingly, the anti-PD-1 antibodies pembrolizumab and nivolumab received FDA approval to treat HNSCC recurring after the use of platinum-based regimens.\nPembrolizumab also was approved alongside chemotherapy (platinum and fluorouracil) for all HNSCC patients and received approval as a single-agent therapy for patients PD-L1-positive patients with CPS ≥ 1 [123].\n...\nFor HNSCC that is recurring or metastatic, a CPS ≥ 1 is the FDA-approved threshold that justifies the using pembrolizumab in the first-line setting either as single agent, or alongside chemotherapy.\n...\nPD-L1 remains a key predictive biomarker, but inconsistencies in testing methods and thresholds hinder its clinical utility [42, 43, 175].\nRecent evidence suggests that analyzing PD-L1 at relapse may yield more accurate predictions than using archival samples.\n...\nCurrently, HPV status and PD-L1 CPS remain the most widely established biomarkers with direct implications for clinical decision-making in head and neck cancer.", "date": "2025-12-18", "last_updated": "2026-08-06"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Driving Progress in Recurrent and Metastatic Head and Neck Cancer", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13101959/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Anti-programmed death 1 (PD-1) agents such as nivolumab^4^ and pembrolizumab^5,6,7^ in pretreated patients with R/M HNSCC and their subsequent incorporation in the first-line advanced-disease setting^8^, has provided clinical benefits offering durable responses in a subset of patients.\n...\nThe KEYNOTE-048 study^8^ established the use of pembrolizumab in the first-line setting, either as monotherapy (limited to patients with PD-L1 ≥ 1 expression) or in combination with platinum and 5-fluorouracil (5-FU).\nAs a result, immunotherapy was established as the core element of standard-of-care in the first-line treatment of platinum-sensitive R/M HNSCC.\n...\nSubsequently, other 5-FU free regimens based on platinum plus taxane combination with immunotherapy have also proven be beneficial in the platinum-sensitive population.\n...\nBased on available evidence, current guidelines recommend the use of immunotherapy as monotherapy in this setting^23^.\n...\nDespite biological^10^ and technical limitations, PD-L1 remains a dominant factor to guiding first-line treatment decision.\n...\nThe treatment of recurrent/metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (R/M HNSCC) remains heavily reliant on chemotherapy-based regimens.\nHowever, immunotherapy and targeted therapy approaches offer the opportunity to introduce chemotherapy-sparing strategies.\n...\nPetosentamab (MCLA-158), a bispecific antibody that targets the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) and the leucine-rich repeat containing G-protein-coupled receptor 5 (LGR5), currently holds two breakthrough therapy designations by the FDA for patients with R/M HNSCC who have experienced disease progression following treatment with platinum-based chemotherapy and a PD-L1/PD-1 inhibitor^91^ and also in the first-line setting in combination with pembrolizumab for patients with PD-L1 (CPS≥1)^92^.\n...\nThe phase II/III FORTIFI-HN01 study (NCT06788990) is currently evaluating the combination of pembrolizumab with ficerafusp alfa (BCA101) vs placebo in the first-line setting for PD-L1-positive R/M HNSCC^95^.\n...\nPrevious data suggest that second-line treatment should be considered after IO progression in patients with HNSCC, even in cases with poor PS^98^.", "date": "2026-07-01", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}], "raw": {"id": "badb3144-1a1d-4a11-9168-422561369248"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:21+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q057", "objective": "CNP200137 first regulatory approval or market launch year for acute myeloid leukemia", "anchors": ["CNP200137"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q057-batch", "query": ["CNP200137 regulatory approval AML", "CNP200137 market launch AML"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["CNP200137 regulatory approval AML", "CNP200137 market launch AML"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 1884.1, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Menin Inhibitor Drug Development Pipeline: AML, Diabetes ...", "url": "https://www.inflectionlabs.bio/reports/menin-inhibitor-pipeline", "domain": "inflectionlabs.bio", "snippet": "**3.\nCNP200137 (CoBX Bio, Pharos iBio)**\nThis is a pre-clinical stage, next-generation menin inhibitor that is designed to address menin resistance challenges.^[58]^ CNP200137 may well be the same molecule as CXB-001 with a different name, or a completely separate molecule being developed by the same South Korean biotech partnership.^[59]^", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Oncology (Cancer)/Hematologic Malignancies Approval ...", "url": "https://www.fda.gov/drugs/resources-information-approved-drugs/oncology-cancerhematologic-malignancies-approval-notifications", "domain": "fda.gov", "snippet": "venetoclax for newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia|On May 13, 2026, the Food and Drug Administration approved an oral combination of decitabine and cedazuridine tablets (Inqovi, Taiho Oncology, Inc.) with venetoclax for the treatment of newly diagnosed acute myeloid leukemia (AML) in adults 75 years or older, or who have comorbidities that preclude the use of intensive induction chemotherapy.|5/13/2026|", "date": "2026-09-24", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "555 Security Incident Detected", "url": "https://2npharma.com/category/press-releases/page/4", "domain": "2npharma.com", "snippet": "You can see the detailed reason for this in your webserver logs.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-02-12"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "520: Web server is returning an unknown error", "url": "https://www.q-depot.com/blog/cnp-laboratory", "domain": "q-depot.com", "snippet": "Please include the Ray ID (which is at the bottom of this error page).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Acute Myeloid Leukemia: 2025 Update on Diagnosis, Risk-Stratification, and Management", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/ajh.27625", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "The FDA approved revumenib (SNDX−5613) for R/R *KMT2A* rearranged AML based on the results of AUGMENT-101, a phase I single agent clinical trial in *KMT2A*-rearranged and *NPM1*-mutated R/R AML [221].", "date": "2025-05-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Circulars & Announcements-CStone Pharmaceuticals", "url": "https://www.cstonepharma.com/en/relation/notice21.html", "domain": "cstonepharma.com", "snippet": "Select “Accept all” for giving your consent to all cookies or select “Reject all” for using only strictly necessary cookies.", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-12-03"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Please verify you are a human", "url": "https://www.zoominfo.com/c/cnp-cyprialife/466887957", "domain": "zoominfo.com", "snippet": "Reference ID: #d39a734b-726a-11ef-a9b7-b2997cca9ae0", "date": null, "last_updated": "2024-09-14"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Circulars & Announcements-CStone Pharmaceuticals", "url": "https://www.cstonepharma.com/en/relation/notice14.html", "domain": "cstonepharma.com", "snippet": "Select “Accept all” for giving your consent to all cookies or select “Reject all” for using only strictly necessary cookies.", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-02-11"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "NCATS Inxight Drugs", "url": "https://drugs.ncats.io/substances?page=1333&facet=Development+Status/Clinical", "domain": "drugs.ncats.io", "snippet": "{{facet.count}}\n{{facet.count}}\n{{facet.count}}", "date": "2025-01-01", "last_updated": "2025-05-29"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Regulatory timeline - OnCo", "url": "https://onco.cc/regulatory/", "domain": "onco.cc", "snippet": "|20 Jul 2023|Quizartinib Small-molecule kinase inhibitor (FLT3-ITD, type II)|Approval|US|Frontline FLT3-ITD AML (QuANTUM-First)|", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}], "raw": {"id": "c346d16c-f719-40f2-b265-6b3636b5889d"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:23+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q056", "objective": "MNPS Asset planned or expected data readouts, regulatory submissions, regulatory decisions, and expected timing in Head and Neck Cancer", "anchors": ["MNPS"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q056-batch", "query": ["MNPS Asset first-line recurrent metastatic head and neck cancer data readout", "MNPS Asset head and neck cancer regulatory submission", "MNPS Asset head and neck cancer regulatory decision"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["MNPS Asset first-line recurrent metastatic head and neck cancer data readout", "MNPS Asset head and neck cancer regulatory submission", "MNPS Asset head and neck cancer regulatory decision"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 3173.6, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Internal Server Error", "url": "https://cdas.cancer.gov/approved-projects/5003/", "domain": "cdas.cancer.gov", "snippet": "Please contact the server administrator at webcontacts@imsweb.com to inform them of the time this error occurred, and the actions you performed just before this error.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2024-10-20"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Search Page - Drug and Health Product Register", "url": "https://hpr-rps.hres.ca/reg-content/regulatory-decision-summary.php", "domain": "hpr-rps.hres.ca", "snippet": "## Regulatory Decision Summary Search\nAll Regulatory Decision Summaries\n- **Date modified:**\n2016-04-13", "date": "2014-10-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Live Head And Neck Cancer updates", "url": "https://www.miragenews.com/tag/head-and-neck-cancer/", "domain": "miragenews.com", "snippet": "Live Head And Neck Cancer news coverage of developments with the latest updates.", "date": "2026-10-02", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Regulatory Decision Summaries - Drug and Health Product Register", "url": "https://hpr-rps.hres.ca/reg-content/regulatory-decision-summary-result.php?lang=en&term=", "domain": "hpr-rps.hres.ca", "snippet": "Below are the results for Regulatory Decision Summaries.\n- Download excel\n...\n2016-04-13", "date": "2014-10-23", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma...", "url": "https://datalookout.com/head-neck-cancer-clinical-trials", "domain": "datalookout.com", "snippet": "The checkpoint immunotherapy era transformed recurrent/metastatic HNSCC: pembrolizumab (Keytruda) is FDA-approved as first-line monotherapy for high CPS scores and in combination with chemotherapy for all-comers; nivolumab (Opdivo) is approved in platinum-refractory disease.\n...\n|NCT06589804|Cetuximab + pembrolizumab vs pembrolizumab in 2L+ relapsed/metastatic HNSCC|National Cancer Institute (NCI)|Recruiting|\n...\n|NCT07026474|Re-chemoradiotherapy + pembrolizumab vs immunotherapy alone for locoregional recurrence (PD-L1 CPS≥1)|Universität des Saarlandes|Recruiting|\n...\nMerus subsequently launched two Phase 3 registrational trials covering the two most commercially significant HNSCC settings:\n- **LiGeR-HN1 (NCT06525220):** Petosemtamab + pembrolizumab vs pembrolizumab in first-line R/M HNSCC.\nThis is the pivotal trial for the 1L commercial indication, competing directly with pembrolizumab + chemotherapy standard of care.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-22"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] 2022 Update edited by the Japan Society for Head and Neck Cancer", "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/am/pii/S0385814623001402", "domain": "sciencedirect.com", "snippet": "If you are unable to access your content please\ntry accessing the content again or\ncontact our support team", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-09-02"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Recurrent and Metastatic Head and Neck Cancer", "url": "https://www.mdpi.com/2072-6694/17/1/144", "domain": "mdpi.com", "snippet": "Agents such as programmed cell death protein 1 (PD-1), programmed death ligand 1 (PD-L1), and cytotoxic T-lymphocyte-associated protein 4 (CLTA-4) inhibitors, in combination or as monotherapy, are finding increasing use as first-line agents for patients with R/M HNSCC.\n...\nPer the NCCN, pembrolizumab/platinum/5-FU is emphasized as the preferable first line option for R/M HNSCC with no surgical or radiotherapeutic option following the results of the KEYNOTE-048 trial [50].\nIn patients with a higher CPS (>1), pembrolizumab monotherapy may be recommended.\nConversely, lower CPSs (<1) necessitate a combination therapy with additional platinum and 5-FU.\nIn patients with platinum-refractory R/M HNSCC, CPS status is not used and PD-1 inhibitors pembrolizumab and nivolumab may be offered first-line to all patients with recurrent, unresectable, or metastatic disease without a surgical or radiotherapeutic alternative, regardless of CPS status.\n...\nAdditionally, per the ASCO, TMB testing may be pursued in R/M HNSCC cancer patients without a CPS or in patients with rare tumors, and a TMB score of >10 is interpreted as being consistent with a clinical benefit of PD-1/PD-L1 system inhibition [2].\n...\nIn 2019, the randomized, open-label KEYNOTE-048 phase III trial of patients with untreated locally incurable R/M HNSCC examined three treatment arms, pembrolizumab alone, pembrolizumab plus platinum and 5-fluorouracil (pembrolizumab with chemotherapy), or cetuximab plus platinum and 5-fluorouracil (cetuximab with chemotherapy), and based on the findings of this study, pembrolizumab plus platinum and 5-FU shortly thereafter joined the ranks of first-line therapies for R/M HNSCC [50].\nPatients in this trial were stratified by their PD-L1 expression, p16 status, and performance status.\nPD-L1 expression combined positive score (CPS) influenced overall survival, supporting the importance of immunotherapy and immune targeting in the treatment of R/M HNSCC, and emphasized the importance of the PD-1/PD-L1 pathway in mediating immune evasion in HNSCC.", "date": "2025-01-05", "last_updated": "2026-08-13"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Legal News & Analysis on Litigation, Policy, Deals : Law360", "url": "https://www.law360.com/subscribe/free_trial?target_url=https://www.law360.com/companies/molecular-pathology-laboratory-network-inc", "domain": "law360.com", "snippet": "Legal News & Analysis on Litigation, Policy, Deals : Law360\n...\nConfirm Password", "date": "2026-09-30", "last_updated": "2026-09-30"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "First-Line Therapy in Recurrent or Metastatic Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma: A Retrospective, Multicenter, Real-World Study", "url": "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/hed.28211", "domain": "onlinelibrary.wiley.com", "snippet": "# First-Line Therapy in Recurrent or Metastatic Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma: A Retrospective, Multicenter, Real-World Study\n...\nIn Europe, pembrolizumab with or without chemotherapy is the recommended first-line treatment for recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (R/M HNSCC), with a combined positive score (CPS) ≥ 1.\nIn France, the TPExtreme (TPEx) regimen is preferred for CPS < 1.\n...\nWe reviewed all R/M HNSCC cases treated with first-line regimens between January 2021 and December 2023 in Nancy and Lille.\nData on patient history, treatments, survival, response, and safety were collected.\n...\nThe analysis included 290 patients; 19% received pembrolizumab alone, 28% received pembrolizumab with chemotherapy, 19% received TPEx, and 20% received adapted regimens.\nPFS and OS differed significantly between groups.\nMedian PFS and OS were respectively 2.9 and 9.4 months for pembrolizumab, 6 and 15.5 months for pembrolizumab-chemotherapy, 5.9 and 13.7 months for TPEx, and 4.3 and 10 months for adapted regimens.\nPembrolizumab alone is the safest regimen.\n...\nThis retrospective study reflects real-world first-line therapy for R/M HNSCC in two French cancer institutes.\nPembrolizumab combined with chemotherapy is the preferred treatment option.\n...\nHistorically, the first-line treatment for recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (R/M HNSCC) has been chemotherapy alone.\n...\nThe European Society for Medical Oncology (ESMO) recommends [9] the use of pembrolizumab for patients with a CPS ≥ 1, potentially associated with chemotherapy, and the EXTREME or TPEx regimens for patients with a CPS < 1.\nThese recommendations apply only to patients in good health condition.\n...\nThis retrospective, multicentric study included patients treated for R/M HNSCC from January 1st 2021 to December 31th 2023, in Nancy (ICL) and Lille (COL).\n...\nBetween January 1, 2021 and December 31, 2023, 290 patients treated for R/M HNSCC were included: 157 (54%) in ICL and 133 (46%) in COL.\n...\nORR was 18% in the pembrolizumab group (10 patients, with 1 complete response at 2–12 months), 57.5% in the pembrolizumab-chemotherapy group (46 patients, with 1 complete response at 2–12 months), 59% in the TPEx group (33 patients), and 39% in the adapted regimens group (23 patients, 2 complete responses at 2 and 4 months).\n...\nPFS differed significantly between groups, with a median PFS of 2.9 months [95% CI 2.1–3.7] for pembrolizumab, 6 [4.7–7.4] for pembrolizumab-chemotherapy, 5.9 [4.3–7.4] for TPEx, and 4.3 [3.2–5.4] for adapted regimens (Figure 1).\n...\nPembrolizumab, alone or in combination with chemotherapy, is favored for patients with a CPS ≥ 1, while the TPEx regimen remains common for patients with a CPS < 1.", "date": "2025-11-01", "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "First-Line Therapy in Recurrent or Metastatic Head and Neck ...", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40491285/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "# First-Line Therapy in Recurrent or Metastatic Head and Neck Squamous Cell Carcinoma: A Retrospective, Multicenter, Real-World Study\n...\n** In Europe, pembrolizumab with or without chemotherapy is the recommended first-line treatment for recurrent or metastatic head and neck squamous cell carcinoma (R/M HNSCC), with a combined positive score (CPS) ≥ 1.\nIn France, the TPExtreme (TPEx) regimen is preferred for CPS < 1.\n...\n** We reviewed all R/M HNSCC cases treated with first-line regimens between January 2021 and December 2023 in Nancy and Lille.\nData on patient history, treatments, survival, response, and safety were collected.\n...\n** The analysis included 290 patients; 19% received pembrolizumab alone, 28% received pembrolizumab with chemotherapy, 19% received TPEx, and 20% received adapted regimens.\nPFS and OS differed significantly between groups.\nMedian PFS and OS were respectively 2.9 and 9.4 months for pembrolizumab, 6 and 15.5 months for pembrolizumab-chemotherapy, 5.9 and 13.7 months for TPEx, and 4.3 and 10 months for adapted regimens.\nPembrolizumab alone is the safest regimen.\n...\n** This retrospective study reflects real-world first-line therapy for R/M HNSCC in two French cancer institutes.\nPembrolizumab combined with chemotherapy is the preferred treatment option.", "date": "2025-11-05", "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}], "raw": {"id": "1d63fa9a-2e84-4503-a822-a8c790a18b9c"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:24+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q058", "objective": "HEC234055 in vivo efficacy in combination with another agent in Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma, including the combination partner", "anchors": ["HEC234055"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q058-batch", "query": ["HEC234055 combination pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma in vivo", "HEC234055 combination partner PDAC xenograft", "HEC234055 AACR 2026 combination efficacy"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["HEC234055 combination pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma in vivo", "HEC234055 combination partner PDAC xenograft", "HEC234055 AACR 2026 combination efficacy"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 2375.4, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "C262019A_Sunshine Pharma 1..4", "url": "https://www.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2026/0423/2026042301900_c.pdf", "domain": "hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "本集團欣然宣佈，於2026 年4 月17 日至22 日在美國加利福尼亞州聖地亞哥舉行的\n...\n二、HEC234055 — 攻克「不可成藥」靶點的口服高活性Pan-RAS分子膠抑制劑（摘\n要號#2634）\n...\n的口服高活性Pan-RAS 分子膠抑制劑，旨在廣譜覆蓋KRAS 多種突變及野生型\n...\n亞納摩爾級細胞活性：在多種KRAS 突變驅動的癌細胞系中，其抗增殖活\n性達到亞納摩爾級別，半抑制濃度（「IC50」）低至0.49 納摩爾（「n M」），顯\n示出優於當前對標分子的強效抑制能力。\n...\n顯著的體內療效：在多種KRAS 突變的異種移植瘤模型中（涵蓋胰腺癌、\n肺癌及結直腸癌），口服給藥可產生劑量依賴性的腫瘤生長抑制乃至誘導\n腫瘤消退。\n...\n廣泛的聯合用藥潛力：與化療藥物(5 -FU) 、抗VEGF 抗體、VEGFR 抑制劑\n及KRAS G12C抑制劑聯合給藥，均表現出顯著的協同增效作用。", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-01"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Sunshine Lake Pharma Co., Ltd. Showcases Three Innovative ...", "url": "https://www.marketscreener.com/news/sunshine-lake-pharma-co-ltd-showcases-three-innovative-oncology-drug-candidates-at-2026-aacr-annu-ce7f58d8dd8ffe27", "domain": "marketscreener.com", "snippet": "At the 2026 Annual Meeting of the American Association for Cancer Research (AACR), held from 17 to 22 April 2026 in San Diego, California, USA, the Group presented the latest preclinical research results of three oncology pipeline candidates through three poster presentations.\n...\nHEC234055 is an orally available high-activity Pan-RAS molecular glue inhibitor independently developed by the Group, designed to target a broad spectrum of multiple KRAS mutations as well as wild-type amplifications.\n...\nSignificant in vivo efficacy: In multiple KRAS-mutated xenograft tumor models (including pancreatic, lung and colorectal cancers), oral administration can produce dose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and even induce tumor regression.\n...\nThe preclinical research data disclosed in this announcement are derived from laboratory and animal model studies, and the relevant drug candidates have not yet undergone clinical trials in humans.", "date": "2026-04-23", "last_updated": "2026-05-14"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Combing Treatment for PDAC", "url": "https://public-pages-files-2025.frontiersin.org/journals/oncology/articles/10.3389/fonc.2020.01378/epub", "domain": "public-pages-files-2025.frontiersin.org", "snippet": "H*WI#��U�4&��Y� i�ϐ�����4#ʹ �9i1H�.���%�'�H+miMZ�T҆���-i�u �H;--iO��9ҁt���#鈿'�H'��L:kKH�O%]IWW)ҍt��Hw�]=Cz��'�r��\"���7��>�xI_��U��#��Gҟ�G�2@kF����Ad�@��8B��b��d��'F�)�p2\\IF���d$�P2��R���d4NFƐ1�a2����82���'��d��L$�RJ�IdJN&��Z2�L�+�T2UkE��iZ2�L���2��d&�Mf�Y�l�/�A>#�)}�2�!��ϕ���%s���<2O9B����, p#��,TYD)��d�v�,!K@!K�R-YF�)g�r���d�چ�$+��dY�'��jxN5����Uӑud�+.YO֫���A��l$�d٤.'��f-)�B��z��d+� ��v!;����|�[��d't&��.HMv���������^�W�O��}�y���WZ���J���j�� G#��!8M��( ��|��F��#h9J������=9F���qr\\}MN�� r����\"����[�-�HN���C�#", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-12-04"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "AACR Annual Meeting 2026 – Post-event summary", "url": "https://www.insights.bio/bioinsights/journal/article/3821/aacr-annual-meeting-2026-postevent-summary-clinical-data-readouts-and-emerging-themes", "domain": "insights.bio", "snippet": "TNG961 caused complete tumor regression in pancreatic and lung cancer models and demonstrated combinatorial activity with inhibitors targeting MTAP deletion, which frequently co-occurs.", "date": "2026-05-14", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "東陽光薬（06887.HK）の3種類の創薬が2026年AACR年次総会で発表", "url": "https://www.moomoo.com/ja/news/post/68755580", "domain": "moomoo.com", "snippet": "グローベンター（格隆汇）4月23日付｜東陽光薬（06887.\nHK）は、2026年4月17日から22日まで米国カリフォルニア州サンディエゴで開催された2026年米国癌学会（AACR）年会上において、グループが3つのポスター発表形式で腫瘍\n治療領域における3つの研究開発パイプライン（CDH17/4-1BB二重特異性抗体「HEC-922」、経口高活性Pan-RAS分子グリュー阻害剤「HEC234055」、経口低分子PD-L1阻害剤「HEC201625」）の最新前臨床研究成果を集中的に発表したことを発表しました。", "date": "2026-04-23", "last_updated": "2026-04-24"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Annual Meeting 2026 Session Recaps Archives", "url": "https://www.aacrmeetingnews.org/category/2026/recap-2026/", "domain": "aacrmeetingnews.org", "snippet": "Alberto Giovanni Leone, MD, shared results from cohort two of the single-arm, nonrandomized phase II INFINITY clinical trial assessing a combination treatment of two immune checkpoint inhibitors, tremelimumab and durvalumab.", "date": "2026-05-07", "last_updated": "2026-09-23"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Advances in antibody-drug conjugates in cancer - PMC - NIH", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13332605/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Across multiple preclinical and early clinical studies, emerging ADC technologies demonstrated improved precision, enhanced efficacy, and the ability to overcome resistance mechanisms that have historically limited this class of therapeutics.", "date": "2026-07-04", "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "AACR 2026: Emerging Antibody-Drug Conjugates for the ...", "url": "https://www.adcreview.com/conference-coverage/aacr/aacr-2026-emerging-antibody-drug-conjugates-for-the-treatment-of-solid-tumors-part-1/", "domain": "adcreview.com", "snippet": "IN30758, a novel ADC being developed by InxMed (Ying Shi Bio), targets integrins such as *alphaVbeta6*, an epithelial-specific transmembrane receptor that is typically absent in healthy adult tissues but significantly upregulated during wound healing, chronic inflammation, and in various solid epithelial cancers, including pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC), non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC), and ovarian cancer.", "date": "2026-05-05", "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "AACR 2026", "url": "https://medical.emdserono.com/en_US/medinfo/therapeutic-areas/oncology/congress-presentations/aacr-2026.html", "domain": "medical.emdserono.com", "snippet": "2026 American Association for Cancer Research (AACR) Annual Meeting – April 17-22, 2026 / San Diego, CA", "date": "2026-05-11", "last_updated": "2026-09-21"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "Unbiased in vivo preclinical evaluation of anticancer drugs identifies effective therapy for the treatment of pancreatic adenocarcinoma - PubMed", "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33199632/", "domain": "pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "To identify effective PDAC therapies, we leveraged a syngeneic orthotopic PDAC transplant mouse model to perform a large-scale, in vivo screen of 16 single-agent and 41 two-drug targeted therapy combinations in mice.\nAmong 57 drug conditions screened, combined inhibition of heat shock protein (Hsp)-90 and MEK was found to produce robust suppression of tumor growth, leading to an 80% increase in the survival of PDAC-bearing mice with no significant toxicity.\nMechanistically, we observed that single-agent MEK inhibition led to compensatory activation of resistance pathways, including components of the PI3K/AKT/mTOR signaling axis, which was overcome with the addition of HSP90 inhibition.\nThe combination of HSP90(i) + MEK(i) was also active in vitro in established human PDAC cell lines and in vivo in patient-derived organoid PDAC transplant models.\nThese findings encourage the clinical development of HSP90(i) + MEK(i) combination therapy and highlight the power of clinically relevant in vivo model systems for identifying cancer therapies.", "date": "2020-12-01", "last_updated": "2025-07-20"}], "raw": {"id": "9b5aed69-1316-46e5-b64a-48d97521368f"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:25+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q059", "objective": "HEC234055 in vivo animal models and results: cell-line xenograft (CDX), patient-derived xenograft (PDX), syngeneic, orthotopic, genetically engineered models", "anchors": ["HEC234055"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q059-batch", "query": ["HEC234055 HNSCC xenograft", "HEC234055 PDX model", "HEC234055 syngeneic model", "HEC234055 orthotopic pancreatic model", "HEC234055 genetically engineered mouse model"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["HEC234055 HNSCC xenograft", "HEC234055 PDX model", "HEC234055 syngeneic model", "HEC234055 orthotopic pancreatic model", "HEC234055 genetically engineered mouse model"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 1499.6, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "[PDF] E262019A_Sunshine Pharma 1..4 - HKEXnews", "url": "https://www1.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2026/0423/2026042301899.pdf", "domain": "www1.hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "HEC234055 — Orally Available High-Activity Pan-RAS Molecular Glue Inhibitor\nTargeting the ‘‘Undruggable’’ (Abstract #2634)\nRAS gene mutations drive approximately one-quarter of human cancers, with common mutation subtypes such as KRAS G12D and G12V still representing significant unmet clinical needs.\nThe drug candidate under development HEC234055 is an orally available high-activity Pan-RAS molecular glue inhibitor independently developed by the Group, designed to target a broad spectrum of multiple KRAS mutations as well as wild-type amplifications.\n...\nBroad-spectrum coverage and high activity: It can act on both active (guanosine triphosphate (‘‘GTP’’)-bound) and inactive (GDP-bound) states of KRAS, and covers various mutation types such as G12D, G12C, G12V, G13D, Q61H, and wild-type amplification.\n...\nSignificant in vivo efficacy: In multiple KRAS-mutated xenograft tumor models\n(including pancreatic, lung and colorectal cancers), oral administration can produce\ndose-dependent tumor growth inhibition and even induce tumor regression.\n...\nExcellent in vivo efficacy: In MC38-hPDL1 homology models and multiple PBMC humanized xenograft models, the tumor growth inhibition rate (TGI) of single- agent administration can reach up to approximately 70%. In particular, in PD-L1 antibody-insensitive models such as NCI-H358 (non-small cell lung cancer) and\nMDA-MB-231\n(triple-negative\nbreast\ncancer),\nthe\nsingle-agent\nefficacy\nof\nHEC201625 is significantly superior to that of PD-L1 antibodies, demonstrating a differentiated advantage.\n...\nThe preclinical research data disclosed in this announcement are derived from laboratory and animal model studies, and the relevant drug candidates have not yet undergone clinical trials\nin\nhumans.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-04-23"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Find Your Research Mouse Model 丨 GemPharmatech", "url": "https://en.gempharmatech.com/product/search_608.html", "domain": "en.gempharmatech.com", "snippet": "Use this search tool to find knockout, knock-in, transgenic, humanized mouse models and more by gene, disease area, or model type.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-19"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "International Mouse Strain Resource", "url": "https://www.findmice.org/", "domain": "findmice.org", "snippet": "The IMSR is a searchable online database of mouse strains, stocks, and mutant ES cell lines available worldwide, including inbred, mutant, and genetically engineered strains.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-02"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "C262019A_Sunshine Pharma 1..4", "url": "https://www.hkexnews.hk/listedco/listconews/sehk/2026/0423/2026042301900_c.pdf", "domain": "hkexnews.hk", "snippet": "二、HEC234055 — 攻克「不可成藥」靶點的口服高活性Pan-RAS分子膠抑制劑（摘\n要號#2634）\n...\n亞型仍存在巨大未滿足的臨床需求。在研產品HEC234055 為本集團自主研發\n的口服高活性Pan-RAS 分子膠抑制劑，旨在廣譜覆蓋KRAS 多種突變及野生型\n擴增。此次公佈的臨床前數據亮點如下：\n...\n廣譜覆蓋與高活性：可同時作用於KRAS 的活性（三磷酸鳥苷（「GTP 」）結\n合）與非活性（GDP 結合）狀態，並覆蓋G12D 、G12C 、G12V 、G13D 、\nQ61H等多種突變類型及野生型擴增。\n...\n亞納摩爾級細胞活性：在多種KRAS 突變驅動的癌細胞系中，其抗增殖活\n性達到亞納摩爾級別，半抑制濃度（「IC50」）低至0.49 納摩爾（「n M」），顯\n示出優於當前對標分子的強效抑制能力。\n...\n顯著的體內療效：在多種KRAS 突變的異種移植瘤模型中（涵蓋胰腺癌、\n肺癌及結直腸癌），口服給藥可產生劑量依賴性的腫瘤生長抑制乃至誘導\n腫瘤消退。", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-01"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "PDX Model Database Registration", "url": "https://www.crownbio.com/databases/pdx-models", "domain": "crownbio.com", "snippet": "Our online, searchable patient-derived xenograft (PDX) database enables scientists around the world to quickly and easily search for models that meet their specific research needs.\n...\n**Browse the collection to:**\n- Access a diverse collection of over 2,400 well-characterized and ready-for-service PDX models from US, European, and Asian populations\n- Evaluate and filter models based on indication, target, growth characteristics, standard of care treatment, and ethnicity", "date": "2025-09-26", "last_updated": "2026-07-28"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "MMRRC Repository", "url": "https://www.mmrrc.org/", "domain": "mmrrc.org", "snippet": "STOCK Tg(Cxcr4-EGFP)CD73Gsat/Mmucd is available from the MMRRC and contains multiple copies of a modified BAC in which EGFP reporter gene is inserted immediately upstream of the coding sequence of the targeted gene.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-03"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Resource Page", "url": "https://www.wagnerlab.net/resource-page", "domain": "wagnerlab.net", "snippet": "This page lists genetically engineered mouse models generated and published by our team and how to obtain them from public repositories (links to repositories not shown on the mobile page).", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-10-01"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "Find Your Research Mouse Model 丨 ...", "url": "https://en.gempharmatech.com/product/searchByNcbi_18783_1.html", "domain": "en.gempharmatech.com", "snippet": "Use this search tool to find knockout, knock-in, transgenic, humanized mouse models and more by gene, disease area, or model type.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-07-24"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "PDX Models - PDXNet", "url": "https://www.pdxnetwork.org/models", "domain": "pdxnetwork.org", "snippet": "Models available for research are listed in CancerModels.org.\n...\nPDXNet researchers generated 520 new PDX models across 56 cancer types.\nThe PDXNet Portal can be referenced for an up-to-date list of PDXNet-generated models.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-30"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "{{ngMeta['og:title']}}", "url": "https://bio.tools/pdxnet_portal", "domain": "bio.tools", "snippet": "This is a development version of the registry.\nThere is no guarantee that the data entered here will be preserved in the future.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2024-10-21"}], "raw": {"id": "de8569fe-e060-4af2-9bdb-92d2ab10f6f0"}, "ts": "2026-10-03T12:36:26+00:00"}
{"row_id": "q029", "objective": "TP-317 in vivo monotherapy tumour growth inhibition TGI tumour regression tumour shrinkage in Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma animal models", "anchors": ["TP-317 KPC", "TP-317"], "mode": "batch", "case_id": "q029-batch", "query": ["TP-317 KPC pancreatic cancer tumour regression monotherapy", "TP-317 KPC PDAC dose schedule monotherapy"], "repeat": 0, "provider": "perplexity", "request": {"query": ["TP-317 KPC pancreatic cancer tumour regression monotherapy", "TP-317 KPC PDAC dose schedule monotherapy"], "search_type": "web", "max_results": 10}, "ok": true, "status": 200, "error": null, "attempts": 1, "latency_ms": 32232.3, "n_results": 10, "results": [{"rank": 1, "query_index": null, "title": "Abstract 1135: TP317, a first-in-class resolvin E1 small molecule, potentiates the efficacy of immune checkpoint (ICI) inhibitors in ICI-resistant and ICI-sensitive tumors", "url": "https://aacrjournals.org/cancerres/article/83/7_Supplement/1135/722183/Abstract-1135-TP317-a-first-in-class-resolvin-E1", "domain": "aacrjournals.org", "snippet": "Methods: Subcutaneous murine models of lung (LLC), melanoma (B16F10), and pancreatic (Panc02; KPC) tumors were used to investigate TP-317 monotherapy and combinations with ICI.\nTreatment was initiated when tumors reached 125-240 mm\n3.\nResults: In the LLC model (N=5/group), TP317 (0.75 µg QD) inhibited tumor growth (1419 ± 266 mm\n...\nIn KRAS-mutant KPC tumors (N=10/group), TP317 (7.5 µg Q6D) demonstrated significant anti-tumor activity compared to placebo (500 ± 110 vs 1314 ± 106 mm 3; p<0.001) and was comparable to anti-PD1, while TP-317 + anti-PD1 was superior to anti-PD1 alone (353 ± 82 vs 710 ± 106 mm 3; p<0.01).\n...\nConclusions: TP317 monotherapy and ICI combinations significantly inhibited tumor growth in various murine models of cancer.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-01-17"}, {"rank": 2, "query_index": null, "title": "Pembrolizumab, carboplatin and paclitaxel 1 of 5", "url": "https://www.kmcc.nhs.uk/s3/assets/lun-043-pembrolizumab,-carboplatin-and-paclitaxel-v4.pdf", "domain": "kmcc.nhs.uk", "snippet": "Maximum of 4 cycles of pembrolizumab, carboplatin & paclitaxel followed by, in the absence of disease progression, continued treatment with pembrolizumab monotherapy* to continue for a total treatment duration of 2 years (maximum of 35 cycles including the initial 4 induction cycles of treatment or its equivalent if 6-weekly pembrolizumab monotherapy dosing is used from cycle 5) or until disease progression or unacceptable toxicity or withdrawal of patient consent, whichever occurs first.\n*There are 2 alternative dosing schedules for pembrolizumab monotherapy, 200mg IV \nevery 3 weeks or 400mg IV every 6 weeks.\n...\nCycle 5 onwards: Repeat every 21 days", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-05-05"}, {"rank": 3, "query_index": null, "title": "Cloudflare", "url": "https://www.cell.com/molecular-therapy-family/oncology/fulltext/S2372-7705(22)00151-6", "domain": "cell.com", "snippet": "Please try again in a few minutes.\n...\nIf you are using a CNAME origin record, make sure it is valid and resolvable.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-11-03"}, {"rank": 4, "query_index": null, "title": "Detail - OpenURL Connection", "url": "https://openurl.ebsco.com/contentitem/doi:10.7150/thno.56737?id=ebsco:doi:10.7150/thno.56737", "domain": "openurl.ebsco.com", "snippet": "We were unable to find your item with the information provided.\n\"Access through your institution\" to search for it on your institution's EBSCOhost experience.", "date": "2025-05-30", "last_updated": "2025-05-30"}, {"rank": 5, "query_index": null, "title": "Award | SBIR", "url": "https://www.sbir.gov/awards/203006", "domain": "sbir.gov", "snippet": "To address this opportunity, Thetis is developing TP-317, a Resolvin E1 (RvE1) therapy for PC.\n...\nPreliminary research conducted in collaboration with Dr. Dipak\nPanigrahy’s Lab (Harvard Medical School) has shown that TP-317 can suppress tumor growth in multiple animal\nmodels as a single agent and has an additive effect with chemotherapy.\n...\nThe overall goal of this Fast Track grant is\nto complete key preclinical studies to support an investigational new drug (IND) application and advance TP-317\ninto clinical trials for the treatment of PC.\n...\nPhase I, Specific Aim 1: To investigate TP-317 in Murine Pancreatic Cancer Models.\nThe efficacy of TP-\n317 will be assessed in two established mouse models of PC, the KPC model and a patient derived xenograft\nmodel.\n...\nPhase I Milestones: Improvement in survival and tumor volume greater than 25% with TP-317 monotherapy or\ncombination arm compared to placebo or active comparator and identification of biomarkers that correlate with\nresponse/non-response preclinically, are clinically significant in PC and are easily assayed in clinical trials.\nPhase II, Specific Aim 2.\n...\nPhase II, Specific Aim 3.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-09"}, {"rank": 6, "query_index": null, "title": "Firm | SBIR", "url": "https://www.sbir.gov/portfolio/406944", "domain": "sbir.gov", "snippet": "The efficacy of TP- 317 will be assessed in two established mouse models of PC, the KPC model and a patient derived xenograft model.\n...\nPhase I Milestones: Improvement in survival and tumor volume greater than 25% with TP-317 monotherapy or combination arm compared to placebo or active comparator and identification of biomarkers that correlate with response/non-response preclinically, are clinically significant in PC and are easily assayed in clinical trials.\nPhase II, Specific Aim 2.\n...\nPhase II, Specific Aim 3.", "date": null, "last_updated": "2026-06-09"}, {"rank": 7, "query_index": null, "title": "Unbiased in vivo preclinical evaluation of anticancer drugs identifies ...", "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7720119/", "domain": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov", "snippet": "Among 57 drug conditions screened, combined inhibition of heat shock protein (Hsp)-90 and MEK was found to produce robust suppression of tumor growth, leading to an 80% increase in the survival of PDAC-bearing mice with no significant toxicity.\n...\n() A waterfall representation of the average response of PDAC orthotopic transplants following a 2-wk treatment with the indicated monotherapies.\nNote that none of the drugs caused tumor regression (mean ± SEM; = 4 per group; * < 0.05, ** < 0.01; one-way ANOVA).\n...\nNone of the 16 monotherapies elicited a notable tumor regression following 2 wk of drug administration using concentrations identified in previously published literature (Fig. 1*C*).\nHowever, several drugs, including gemcitabine (GEM), trametinib, a MAPK/ERK kinase (MEK)-1/2 inhibitor, and PU-H71, a heat-shock protein (HSP)-90 inhibitor with specificity for tumor HSP90 variants (11–14), significantly reduced tumor growth when compared to control.\n...\n|CDK4/6|Palbociclib|50|QD|p.o.|Sodium lactate buffer (50 mM, pH 4.0)|\n|Microtubule inhibitor|Abraxane|30|1x/week|i.p.|Saline|\n|PI3K|Alpelisib|50|QD|i.p.|1% (wt/vol) Carboxymethylcellulose (CMC)+0.5% (wt/vol) Tween 80|\n|RTK|Sunitinib|40|QD|p.o.|0.5% Carboxy-methylcellulose, ph3.5|\n|JAK1/2|Ruxolitinib|60|QD|p.o.|0.1% Tween 80|\n|HSP90|PU-H71|75|3x/week|p.o.|Phosphate buffer|\n|EGFR|Erlotinib|25|QD|p.o.|0.5% methylcellulose|\n...\n|DNA|Gemcitabine|400|1x/week|i.p.|Saline|\n|MEK1/2|Trametinib|2|QD|p.o.|0.5% HPMC+0.2%Tween 80|\n|Src|Dasatinib|30|BID|p.o.|80 mM sodium citrate pH 3.1|\n...\nIn contrast, the T/P combination was highly effective at impairing tumor growth and produced a significant survival benefit, extending the median survival of mice by 1.8-fold over control (83 d vs. 46 d, *P* < 0.001, Fig. 2*C*).", "date": "2020-11-16", "last_updated": "2025-10-17"}, {"rank": 8, "query_index": null, "title": "resolvin E1 (TP-317) / Thetis Pharma - LARVOL DELTA", "url": "https://delta.larvol.com/Products/?ProductId=b6c4e3de-1884-4331-bbe9-120e1d94781b", "domain": "delta.larvol.com", "snippet": "P1a | N=NA | \"Thetis Pharmaceuticals LLC...announced the presentation...at Digestive Disease Week (DDW) 2025....Data from a Phase 1a study confirmed TP-317’s safety and target engagement: Single oral doses up to 80 mg were well tolerated with no treatment-related adverse events.\n...\nTP-317 demonstrated robust single-agent efficacy and enhanced activity in combination with chemotherapy and checkpoint inhibitors in MSS CRC models.\n...\nBased on encouraging preclinical efficacy data and its differentiated mechanism of action addressing pro-tumor inflammation, TP-317 could offer a major benefit as adjuvant to existing standard of care and as monotherapy in the maintenance setting.'\"", "date": "2025-06-23", "last_updated": "2025-07-30"}, {"rank": 9, "query_index": null, "title": "�", "url": "https://cdn.clinicaltrials.gov/large-docs/99/NCT03814499/Prot_SAP_000.pdf", "domain": "cdn.clinicaltrials.gov", "snippet": "� ������������������������������\n...\n���������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������������", "date": null, "last_updated": "2025-08-30"}, {"rank": 10, "query_index": null, "title": "߄w����I*�|b�r˕٘�}>�g��_��\":^)Љ��P�}�꼖���(e��㡱Y;�~�]���E�����/�IbU�̓����-Ʊ�{�{t{LA=�Tm)P,�*u����Q:ʾ�,����F�Ѕ��g�cA]�L����A�����LA��´�~�!C;��������X���֟K�|��Xz*�Rp�h�Z-�6�n�*�������.��G9A+��ƴϵXLMfs�^���˝ِ�j�����g?~ܳn�����v.U�-,��ڸ�ٯ_�cw���eo���S�v��k?Ys��'v*�0J�{ �������}����] �Iz�OKU5�������q�\\xy�Z]z#�;Gӧ�-/x�C�)y�&.w��f#�� Z�{�d!� ,�1��Ł�Nt��zm͜+q+����,��Հ�uO��Ž�h�����5��TPGY|���&ws(��\"�����߬���_l`��������S�ߕ�����`wmP����u�ݮ����̣����Ss��&������`�7\\�%�u��1�)U�c�� �V�7J%R����mۦ�S����h>]_(-{����s�yܛ�r$Ŭ��������>v�v�G���.F��@G'�t��1)�����r��3�+�ߝ�˼����K$�ŏ�h3��+)����\\]g��%������j̦Q28>���K��#%��j�Sm|�i:��l1,+�>�1��T���k�&ZV�Oe*��<��6�3���] ��������ǎ���}h�^�N�P_f ?@X�jG܃��ɺ�.�|�7K�K��)���LS�%�����d��C+{��_S��]�S���9�a��v���K;>�^�h��PܕqP��se�]%��ô������J�}�E7��AH:>[h�'�8��r(t�{�����=�C�mg���v���N��\"�>#����,�D��ǒ���lcЉ����8���